WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:11.200 align:start position:0%
On the 30th of January 1649
Charles the First's head was severed

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.200 align:start position:0%
from his body.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:18.900 align:start position:0%
This anointed king was killed in the
full light of day with the backing of

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:25.000 align:start position:0%
Parliament.
This brutal act of regicide comes after

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:30.800 align:start position:0%
seven years of war that divides towns,
friends, and even families.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:35.900 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; There is nothing civil about civil war.
They have seen so much suffering and

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:41.000 align:start position:0%
death and destruction. They've just had
enough.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:47.200 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; I'm historian Lisa Hilton, and over
three episodes I'll trace day by day,

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:51.300 align:start position:0%
hour by hour, the five weeks that led to
one of the most seismic moments in our

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:57.600 align:start position:0%
constitutional history,
the trial and death of Charles the

00:00:53.200 --> 00:01:03.500 align:start position:0%
First, a king anointed by God.
&gt;&gt; No king in England had ever been put on

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:08.600 align:start position:0%
trial for any charge, let alone treason.
It was unprecedented.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:15.200 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; How did the nation affect this radical
step, the reverberations of which are

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:21.000 align:start position:0%
still felt even to this day?
&gt;&gt; What they did do change English law and

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:26.200 align:start position:0%
politics forever.
&gt;&gt; Hidden within these weeks are the chance

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:31.800 align:start position:0%
events, secret manipulations, and
revolutionary actions which led to this

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:36.400 align:start position:0%
ultimate decision.
How did a country kill its king?

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:04.692 align:start position:0%
It's Christmas Day in the of our Lord
1648.

00:02:02.792 --> 00:02:10.292 align:start position:0%
King Charles the First is spending
Christmas imprisoned in Windsor Castle.

00:02:08.192 --> 00:02:15.692 align:start position:0%
He has lost the civil war after being
captured 18 months earlier by his

00:02:12.392 --> 00:02:19.192 align:start position:0%
enemies in Parliament.
Charles eats alone, reading the

00:02:17.492 --> 00:02:21.992 align:start position:0%
Christmas service from his Book of
Common Prayer.

00:02:22.492 --> 00:02:28.792 align:start position:0%
His family are in exile. His beloved
wife is in Paris.

00:02:26.992 --> 00:02:34.492 align:start position:0%
His eldest children are scattered across
Europe. The youngest are in

00:02:30.492 --> 00:02:34.492 align:start position:0%
Parliamentarian custody in England.

00:02:35.092 --> 00:02:39.892 align:start position:0%
There's no family Christmas for Charles
this year.

00:02:42.492 --> 00:02:49.592 align:start position:0%
And here in Windsor, Charles isn't the
only one having a quiet Christmas.

00:02:47.492 --> 00:02:53.892 align:start position:0%
The Puritans are in power and they've
done their best to ban what they

00:02:51.092 --> 00:02:58.492 align:start position:0%
consider to be a pagan festival.
There are laws making the exchanging of

00:02:55.792 --> 00:03:03.492 align:start position:0%
gifts, the wearing of fine clothes,
feasting and dancing punishable with a

00:03:00.892 --> 00:03:06.992 align:start position:0%
hefty five shilling fine.
It's not exactly festive.

00:03:11.492 --> 00:03:19.892 align:start position:0%
So in London, Christmas Day 1648 is like
any other working day.

00:03:17.692 --> 00:03:25.592 align:start position:0%
Markets are open, working men and women
go about their business, and in

00:03:21.892 --> 00:03:29.592 align:start position:0%
Parliament there's no recess.
And today they're urgently debating the

00:03:27.692 --> 00:03:32.992 align:start position:0%
future of the country.
That all depends on what to do with the

00:03:31.692 --> 00:03:36.692 align:start position:0%
king.
In theory, the king and Parliament

00:03:34.592 --> 00:03:39.992 align:start position:0%
should work together, but the events of
the past seven years have put an end to

00:03:38.592 --> 00:03:44.892 align:start position:0%
that.
MPs now speak openly of Charles as a man

00:03:43.392 --> 00:03:48.392 align:start position:0%
of blood,
and they demand that he be made to

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:50.992 align:start position:0%
answer for the horrible violence of the
civil war.

00:03:58.992 --> 00:04:05.094 align:start position:0%
Amongst the MPs sitting that day is
Oliver Cromwell.

00:04:02.894 --> 00:04:10.494 align:start position:0%
Cromwell comes from a relatively humble
background.

00:04:07.294 --> 00:04:15.594 align:start position:0%
He's been an MP since 1628, but his
early life and career were unremarkable.

00:04:14.394 --> 00:04:20.794 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Cromwell's not someone who stands on
ceremony. He's not impressed by any of

00:04:17.894 --> 00:04:24.894 align:start position:0%
that. He famously dresses very shabbily.
He has ill-fitting clothes. His hair

00:04:22.894 --> 00:04:29.694 align:start position:0%
could do with a comb. He's once seen
with blood specks on his collar, you

00:04:27.494 --> 00:04:33.594 align:start position:0%
know, he's cut himself shaving. And this
is sort of part of his shtick, really.

00:04:32.094 --> 00:04:37.994 align:start position:0%
He famously once said that he would
rather have a plain, russet-coated

00:04:35.794 --> 00:04:44.194 align:start position:0%
captain who knows what he fights for and
loves what he knows than that which you

00:04:40.294 --> 00:04:48.894 align:start position:0%
call a gentleman and is nothing else.
&gt;&gt; Cromwell had some sort of nervous

00:04:46.294 --> 00:04:56.294 align:start position:0%
breakdown in his 20s, which he later
referred to as his melancholia. And from

00:04:51.794 --> 00:04:59.394 align:start position:0%
that point on was a devout Puritan.
&gt;&gt; We know that

00:04:57.694 --> 00:05:04.794 align:start position:0%
Cromwell had a religious conversion in
the 16 late 1630s,

00:05:02.894 --> 00:05:11.994 align:start position:0%
which turned him into the kind of
Puritan he was. It's always a dialogue

00:05:08.994 --> 00:05:11.994 align:start position:0%
between him and God.

00:05:12.594 --> 00:05:20.294 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; But Cromwell is up against a king who is
absolutely convinced of his divine right

00:05:17.694 --> 00:05:20.294 align:start position:0%
to rule.

00:05:21.094 --> 00:05:27.094 align:start position:0%
Charles believes that he's answerable to
God, not Parliament.

00:05:25.094 --> 00:05:31.094 align:start position:0%
But the reality is that he's a prisoner
of Parliament.

00:05:28.994 --> 00:05:35.894 align:start position:0%
And for the last three years, they've
tried to get Charles to agree to limit

00:05:33.294 --> 00:05:35.894 align:start position:0%
his power.

00:05:35.994 --> 00:05:41.594 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Parliament keep negotiating. They keep
going back to Charles, back to Charles

00:05:39.894 --> 00:05:47.294 align:start position:0%
saying, you know, come to terms with us,
come to terms with us. Um and these

00:05:43.594 --> 00:05:51.294 align:start position:0%
negotiations are going on and on and on.
&gt;&gt; Cromwell by nature is

00:05:49.394 --> 00:05:55.494 align:start position:0%
a negotiator.
He wants to have a settlement between

00:05:54.194 --> 00:05:58.794 align:start position:0%
the army
and Parliament

00:05:56.994 --> 00:06:03.194 align:start position:0%
and the king.
What he wants

00:06:00.794 --> 00:06:08.295 align:start position:0%
is for the king to come to some kind of
constitutional settlement, to something

00:06:05.695 --> 00:06:10.795 align:start position:0%
that's like a modern constitutional
monarchy.

00:06:11.595 --> 00:06:19.095 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; So far, Charles has refused to
compromise.

00:06:16.295 --> 00:06:25.195 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; He was not prepared to give up.
He was not prepared

00:06:21.595 --> 00:06:30.595 align:start position:0%
to deny his god. He would not agree to
that, and that was demanded of him.

00:06:28.995 --> 00:06:36.795 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; For him, it's very simple. You know, his
will is God's will, and he is

00:06:32.695 --> 00:06:36.795 align:start position:0%
accountable to no earthly authority.

00:06:38.295 --> 00:06:45.395 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; As night falls on Christmas Day, in a
last-ditch attempt, Cromwell sends one

00:06:43.195 --> 00:06:49.695 align:start position:0%
of his parliamentarian supporters, the
Earl of Denbigh, to reason with the

00:06:47.295 --> 00:06:49.695 align:start position:0%
king.

00:06:51.395 --> 00:06:59.895 align:start position:0%
The story goes that Charles refuses to
see Denbigh and turns him away without

00:06:55.995 --> 00:06:59.895 align:start position:0%
even hearing the offer from Parliament.

00:07:03.895 --> 00:07:11.395 align:start position:0%
It seems inconceivable that this
deadlock is ever going to be broken.

00:07:09.695 --> 00:07:14.595 align:start position:0%
The time to negotiate has come to an
end.

00:07:12.795 --> 00:07:19.395 align:start position:0%
Tomorrow, Cromwell and Parliament will
have to think of a new way to resolve

00:07:16.595 --> 00:07:19.395 align:start position:0%
this stalemate.

00:07:29.595 --> 00:07:34.295 align:start position:0%
Charles wakes up on the 26th of
December.

00:07:33.095 --> 00:07:38.595 align:start position:0%
Once he would have spent the day
celebrating the feast of St. Stephen,

00:07:37.095 --> 00:07:41.795 align:start position:0%
and his servants would have enjoyed
their Christmas boxes.

00:07:41.895 --> 00:07:50.395 align:start position:0%
Instead, today he follows his usual
routine as a prisoner at Windsor.

00:07:47.795 --> 00:07:53.595 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; So Charles in Windsor, it's not great
for him. He's used to space. He's used

00:07:51.995 --> 00:07:58.495 align:start position:0%
to majesty. He's used to his own
servants. Now he's got army guards, and

00:07:56.995 --> 00:08:01.795 align:start position:0%
it's quite a strict confinement. You
know, he's got a guard on every door. He

00:08:00.495 --> 00:08:06.795 align:start position:0%
doesn't have his family. He doesn't have
his friends.

00:08:04.795 --> 00:08:09.397 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; So Charles was becoming increasingly
confined.

00:08:08.395 --> 00:08:13.797 align:start position:0%
When he'd had been imprisoned at
Carisbrooke Castle,

00:08:11.197 --> 00:08:19.097 align:start position:0%
he had at first been allowed to walk
around all the grounds. Very physical

00:08:15.797 --> 00:08:27.097 align:start position:0%
man. He liked riding and playing bowls,
but he'd become increasingly confined.

00:08:23.697 --> 00:08:31.797 align:start position:0%
Carisbrooke had even been allowed to see
a woman who was reputed to be his

00:08:28.897 --> 00:08:36.697 align:start position:0%
mistress and he sort of wrote her
rather racy letters saying that he

00:08:34.197 --> 00:08:41.397 align:start position:0%
wanted to meet her in his private closet
and give her a good swiving. Anyway,

00:08:39.197 --> 00:08:45.697 align:start position:0%
there was definitely no swiving to be
had at Windsor that Christmas.

00:08:46.497 --> 00:08:52.497 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Parliament does allow Charles to have a
servant of their choosing,

00:08:50.797 --> 00:08:56.897 align:start position:0%
a gentleman of the bedchamber called
Thomas Herbert.

00:08:55.097 --> 00:09:01.897 align:start position:0%
Herbert later writes an account of his
time with the king.

00:08:59.697 --> 00:09:06.197 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; In his memoir, Thomas Herbert describes
how Charles spends his days.

00:09:04.097 --> 00:09:10.397 align:start position:0%
Mornings are devoted to prayer, and in
the afternoons he takes exercise,

00:09:08.597 --> 00:09:13.997 align:start position:0%
walking here on the north terrace at
Windsor.

00:09:11.697 --> 00:09:18.797 align:start position:0%
According to Herbert, Charles likes this
spot best because it has a very

00:09:16.297 --> 00:09:25.697 align:start position:0%
delightful view of the River Thames with
many pleasant hills and valleys,

00:09:20.897 --> 00:09:25.697 align:start position:0%
villages and fine houses far and near.

00:09:28.397 --> 00:09:35.297 align:start position:0%
Charles has no way of knowing how long
this prison life will go on.

00:09:33.297 --> 00:09:41.197 align:start position:0%
He doesn't know that by the 26th of
December Parliament has had enough of

00:09:37.797 --> 00:09:41.197 align:start position:0%
his refusal to compromise.

00:09:41.897 --> 00:09:52.397 align:start position:0%
Now whispers in Whitehall begin that the
king might be put on trial for treason.

00:09:48.997 --> 00:09:57.797 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Can a king commit treason?
Pretty difficult, you know, when you've

00:09:54.197 --> 00:10:02.897 align:start position:0%
got the 1351 Treason Act which makes it
a crime punishable by death to compass

00:10:01.297 --> 00:10:09.097 align:start position:0%
or imagine
the deposing of a king or to fight for

00:10:05.697 --> 00:10:13.498 align:start position:0%
his enemies. So the law of treason is
really

00:10:10.298 --> 00:10:16.498 align:start position:0%
fashioned to protect the king, not put
him on trial.

00:10:17.998 --> 00:10:25.298 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; So how by Christmas 1648
had this shocking proposition come

00:10:22.698 --> 00:10:29.898 align:start position:0%
about?
Seven years of civil war has left the

00:10:27.398 --> 00:10:33.698 align:start position:0%
country devastated.
Battles, skirmishes and sieges took

00:10:32.098 --> 00:10:39.798 align:start position:0%
place the length and breadth of the
country.

00:10:34.898 --> 00:10:43.898 align:start position:0%
Nowhere and no one was left untouched.
&gt;&gt; I mean for the vast majority of the

00:10:41.498 --> 00:10:46.998 align:start position:0%
people, they just had enough, you know?
They just want to get on with it. They

00:10:45.298 --> 00:10:51.698 align:start position:0%
want to move on with their lives.
Now there is nothing civil about civil

00:10:49.398 --> 00:10:57.398 align:start position:0%
war. And they have seen so much
suffering and death and destruction. And

00:10:55.298 --> 00:11:00.598 align:start position:0%
I just think they just need
closure. They need to move on.

00:11:03.498 --> 00:11:10.798 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; And by Christmas 1648
the winners of the war believed that the

00:11:07.498 --> 00:11:16.298 align:start position:0%
blame for all this bloodshed lies at the
king's feet.

00:11:13.098 --> 00:11:22.398 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The troops were demanding justice.
Great cry of justice on this man of

00:11:19.498 --> 00:11:28.998 align:start position:0%
blood. And they had good reason because
the war that the king started

00:11:25.898 --> 00:11:35.598 align:start position:0%
back in 1642
had killed one in 10 Englishmen. More

00:11:33.698 --> 00:11:42.398 align:start position:0%
comparatively than died in the First
World War. One in 10 Englishmen were

00:11:39.498 --> 00:11:48.198 align:start position:0%
lying dead in the muddy fields of
Marston Moor and Naseby and so on. And

00:11:46.598 --> 00:11:53.798 align:start position:0%
many more
were injured and were permanently

00:11:51.298 --> 00:11:56.498 align:start position:0%
injured. The towns were full of beggars
who were

00:11:54.998 --> 00:12:01.798 align:start position:0%
trailing
a lost limb from the fight with the

00:11:59.498 --> 00:12:01.798 align:start position:0%
king.

00:12:05.898 --> 00:12:12.699 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; I've come to Leicester, a city that
suffered horribly under siege by

00:12:10.098 --> 00:12:18.699 align:start position:0%
royalist forces to look at a collection
of petitions from local residents.

00:12:16.699 --> 00:12:23.299 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The characteristic action of the civil
wars was not a set-piece battle. It was

00:12:20.699 --> 00:12:29.099 align:start position:0%
an attack on a fortified strong point.
And if an army broke into a town and

00:12:26.899 --> 00:12:33.699 align:start position:0%
terms of surrender hadn't been made,
then they could unleash a wave of

00:12:31.199 --> 00:12:37.299 align:start position:0%
violence, a wave of retributory violence
that would have led to murder, um

00:12:36.099 --> 00:12:43.399 align:start position:0%
robbery,
um cutting, maiming, and and rape.

00:12:41.099 --> 00:12:46.899 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; An appalling list of consequences for
someone who is effectively an innocent

00:12:45.599 --> 00:12:52.199 align:start position:0%
bystander.
&gt;&gt; These documents, over 300 years old, are

00:12:50.099 --> 00:12:56.599 align:start position:0%
compensation petitions.
Their claims for damages trace a litany

00:12:54.599 --> 00:13:00.899 align:start position:0%
of human tragedy.
&gt;&gt; What are we looking at right here?

00:12:59.099 --> 00:13:06.599 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Well, here's an example of one of those
petitions from William Sumner, a tailor

00:13:03.799 --> 00:13:11.499 align:start position:0%
in the town of Leicester, to the mayor
and alderman of the town,

00:13:08.799 --> 00:13:16.299 align:start position:0%
complaining that when the king's forces
sacked Leicester in 1645,

00:13:14.299 --> 00:13:21.699 align:start position:0%
they killed his son
and that his house had been pulled down,

00:13:18.899 --> 00:13:26.099 align:start position:0%
and that and with the fright whereof
your petitioner's wife hath been

00:13:23.799 --> 00:13:30.599 align:start position:0%
distracted ever since.
&gt;&gt; There's such a vivid connection reading

00:13:28.199 --> 00:13:34.699 align:start position:0%
about William's wife being distracted
and it's she suddenly becomes a a real

00:13:32.999 --> 00:13:36.599 align:start position:0%
person. Is that something you've
experienced going through these

00:13:35.799 --> 00:13:41.499 align:start position:0%
archives?
&gt;&gt; Well, yes, we've found many similar

00:13:38.299 --> 00:13:44.999 align:start position:0%
stories to this one of people overcome
by grief.

00:13:43.499 --> 00:13:49.599 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; How many of these petitions are in
existence?

00:13:46.399 --> 00:13:55.499 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; We estimate um there are about 4,000
petitions of this ilk in the county

00:13:52.299 --> 00:14:00.799 align:start position:0%
record offices in England and Wales,
plus a huge amount more in the National

00:13:57.699 --> 00:14:00.799 align:start position:0%
Archives as well.

00:14:01.399 --> 00:14:08.099 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Being in the archive gave an immense
jolt of empathy, suddenly seeing the

00:14:06.299 --> 00:14:12.699 align:start position:0%
casualties of the civil war not as
numbers or statistics, but as real

00:14:10.099 --> 00:14:18.101 align:start position:0%
people, people who lost their families,
their livelihoods, their homes. Suddenly

00:14:15.901 --> 00:14:20.601 align:start position:0%
they become immensely and poignantly
human.

00:14:24.401 --> 00:14:31.901 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; And as night draws in on the 26th of
December, Cromwell, Parliament, and the

00:14:29.101 --> 00:14:35.801 align:start position:0%
army are determined that the king must
pay for all this destruction.

00:14:48.901 --> 00:14:57.401 align:start position:0%
The next day, Charles is confronted with
a stark new reality.

00:14:54.601 --> 00:15:02.701 align:start position:0%
The Commons pass a law banning all court
ceremony at Windsor.

00:14:59.901 --> 00:15:06.101 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The ceremonies of state stopped.
All the official doffing of caps, all

00:15:05.301 --> 00:15:10.601 align:start position:0%
the
expected courtesies that would be

00:15:08.101 --> 00:15:15.401 align:start position:0%
normally afforded to a king were halted,
gradually signaling to Charles, if he'd

00:15:13.801 --> 00:15:21.101 align:start position:0%
been under any illusion up to that
point, that the end was coming and the

00:15:17.601 --> 00:15:23.501 align:start position:0%
sense of his kingly authority was ebbing
away.

00:15:25.601 --> 00:15:36.101 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Charles would have seen it as a
deliberate insult to him as king.

00:15:32.801 --> 00:15:40.301 align:start position:0%
He had lived as a soldier for years.
There were times when he had been on the

00:15:37.301 --> 00:15:44.901 align:start position:0%
run, where he had slept on earth floors,
so it wasn't as if he was somebody who

00:15:43.101 --> 00:15:48.501 align:start position:0%
could sort of only, you know, couldn't
stand life if he wasn't, people weren't

00:15:46.901 --> 00:15:52.001 align:start position:0%
sort of bowing and scraping all around
him.

00:15:49.701 --> 00:15:58.101 align:start position:0%
But, you know, he was a king and kings
were treated in a certain way, and to

00:15:55.501 --> 00:16:01.101 align:start position:0%
have this stripped away
was clearly

00:16:00.001 --> 00:16:03.801 align:start position:0%
a threat
and an insult.

00:16:05.201 --> 00:16:11.601 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Charles is left with one small comfort.
His dogs can stay.

00:16:12.801 --> 00:16:19.302 align:start position:0%
This punitive action is part of the
House of Commons' plan to put pressure

00:16:16.702 --> 00:16:19.302 align:start position:0%
on the king.

00:16:23.502 --> 00:16:31.502 align:start position:0%
By the 27th of December, the House of
Commons is effectively in the hands of

00:16:28.202 --> 00:16:31.502 align:start position:0%
the new model army.

00:16:32.502 --> 00:16:38.702 align:start position:0%
The force that had faced Charles across
the battlefield.

00:16:36.302 --> 00:16:42.502 align:start position:0%
But how did a military group come to
control the House of Commons?

00:16:43.502 --> 00:16:49.502 align:start position:0%
Just a few weeks earlier on the 6th of
December 1648,

00:16:47.902 --> 00:16:53.102 align:start position:0%
Colonel Pride and a battalion of
soldiers from the new model army arrived

00:16:51.502 --> 00:16:57.702 align:start position:0%
at Westminster.
Any MPs who still favored negotiating

00:16:55.502 --> 00:17:01.002 align:start position:0%
with the king were blocked from entering
Parliament.

00:16:59.102 --> 00:17:06.002 align:start position:0%
When the MPs demanded to know by whose
power this was done, they were told

00:17:03.002 --> 00:17:11.902 align:start position:0%
bluntly by the power of the sword. It
was unequivocally a coup d'état.

00:17:09.602 --> 00:17:16.902 align:start position:0%
The remaining 80 or so MPs became
insultingly known as the Rump

00:17:14.002 --> 00:17:22.302 align:start position:0%
Parliament, the last remnants clinging
to a stripped carcass.

00:17:19.802 --> 00:17:27.302 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; And that's it. You know, at a stroke,
uh, about 270 peers are shorn from the

00:17:25.002 --> 00:17:32.302 align:start position:0%
Commons.
&gt;&gt; The Rump, and I call it the Rump because

00:17:29.502 --> 00:17:37.302 align:start position:0%
this is not Parliament as we understand
it. It's not even the House of Commons

00:17:34.002 --> 00:17:43.902 align:start position:0%
as we understand it. This is
those MPs who are compliant with the

00:17:41.602 --> 00:17:47.102 align:start position:0%
wishes of the army, effectively. There's
been a military coup.

00:17:48.202 --> 00:17:54.202 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Oliver Cromwell is a member of the Rump
Parliament.

00:17:52.102 --> 00:17:58.402 align:start position:0%
Officially, MPs were not supposed to
hold military posts, but an exception

00:17:56.702 --> 00:18:01.102 align:start position:0%
was made for Cromwell in the light of
his career.

00:18:03.702 --> 00:18:11.102 align:start position:0%
Cromwell grew up in the market town of
Huntington and joined the new model army

00:18:08.102 --> 00:18:15.502 align:start position:0%
in 1642.
Cromwell is second only in the army to

00:18:12.902 --> 00:18:19.202 align:start position:0%
the Lord General, Sir Thomas Fairfax,
and is a celebrated war hero.

00:18:20.102 --> 00:18:26.303 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; He becomes
the most able, the most courageous, the

00:18:24.203 --> 00:18:30.203 align:start position:0%
most decisive of the soldiers that are
produced during the Civil War, and he

00:18:27.803 --> 00:18:34.703 align:start position:0%
comes from almost nothing.
&gt;&gt; At the Cromwell Museum in his hometown

00:18:32.303 --> 00:18:39.703 align:start position:0%
of Huntington, there's a fabulous
momento of his military career.

00:18:38.103 --> 00:18:42.703 align:start position:0%
Tell me about the sword.
Do we know that this did definitively

00:18:41.303 --> 00:18:45.503 align:start position:0%
belong to Oliver Cromwell?
&gt;&gt; He's got the initials on it.

00:18:44.703 --> 00:18:48.503 align:start position:0%
They're just here.
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:18:46.203 --> 00:18:53.703 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It comes down through the family, so
we'd be pretty certain it's his. It's a

00:18:50.203 --> 00:18:59.103 align:start position:0%
classic cavalry sword, both for stabbing
and swiping.

00:18:55.603 --> 00:19:03.903 align:start position:0%
It is a sword that that a cavalry
officer would have worn.

00:19:01.603 --> 00:19:07.803 align:start position:0%
It seems overwhelmingly likely that this
is the sword with which he led charges

00:19:06.503 --> 00:19:12.103 align:start position:0%
into battle. And one of the things we
can say about Cromwell is that he never

00:19:09.603 --> 00:19:17.403 align:start position:0%
ever left it to others to lead a charge.
He was always willing to

00:19:14.903 --> 00:19:21.603 align:start position:0%
show his own courage and his own
confidence by leading.

00:19:19.803 --> 00:19:25.403 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; So is it the case that it was Cromwell's
military career that really turned him

00:19:23.503 --> 00:19:29.303 align:start position:0%
into the leader he became?
&gt;&gt; Well, I think fairly early on he starts

00:19:27.703 --> 00:19:33.103 align:start position:0%
winning battles and he never stops
winning battles. I mean, it's almost

00:19:31.203 --> 00:19:36.503 align:start position:0%
impossible to think of anyone who fought
on so many battlefields and won every

00:19:35.403 --> 00:19:42.403 align:start position:0%
time.
Here's a letter that Cromwell wrote on

00:19:38.403 --> 00:19:46.903 align:start position:0%
the 6th of June 1645. So by now he's
just been appointed as the head of the

00:19:44.403 --> 00:19:51.603 align:start position:0%
cavalry of the National Army, and he's
about to

00:19:48.703 --> 00:19:55.203 align:start position:0%
plan the battle that is to happen at
Naseby. And yet

00:19:53.703 --> 00:20:01.003 align:start position:0%
he is able
to tell two of his loyal men that he'd

00:19:58.503 --> 00:20:04.203 align:start position:0%
recruited himself there's a weakness in
the defenses.

00:20:02.403 --> 00:20:09.703 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; And the fact that he signed the letter
with his full signature implies a degree

00:20:07.003 --> 00:20:13.203 align:start position:0%
of communication of intimacy with his
men, a degree of respect between them.

00:20:11.503 --> 00:20:17.903 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; bow signature, isn't it? Look at the way
the C is formed. I mean, the big

00:20:15.303 --> 00:20:22.203 align:start position:0%
sweeping C, his signature on it, a
handwritten note written under pressure

00:20:20.503 --> 00:20:28.405 align:start position:0%
before one of the greatest battles in
English history is a wonderful example

00:20:24.905 --> 00:20:33.105 align:start position:0%
of this, you know, strong, purposeful,
determined handwriting of a man

00:20:31.505 --> 00:20:35.505 align:start position:0%
confident that he was on the winning
side.

00:20:36.605 --> 00:20:43.805 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Cromwell's military prowess gave him
huge influence.

00:20:40.505 --> 00:20:49.105 align:start position:0%
And by the 27th of December 1648
he's moved a long way from his humble

00:20:45.905 --> 00:20:49.105 align:start position:0%
roots in Huntingdon.

00:20:49.605 --> 00:20:55.105 align:start position:0%
He's moved his family to a house in
Drury Lane,

00:20:53.105 --> 00:20:59.805 align:start position:0%
but he spends most of his time closer to
the center of power,

00:20:57.305 --> 00:21:05.205 align:start position:0%
Whitehall.
Cromwell is now occupying a house known

00:21:02.005 --> 00:21:08.805 align:start position:0%
as the cockpit within Whitehall Palace.
However, his critics note that he's gone

00:21:07.305 --> 00:21:13.105 align:start position:0%
even further.
Not only is he living in the palace,

00:21:11.005 --> 00:21:16.905 align:start position:0%
he's actually sleeping in one of the
king's magnificent beds.

00:21:18.705 --> 00:21:24.605 align:start position:0%
Cromwell's military brilliance has got
him this far,

00:21:22.405 --> 00:21:29.005 align:start position:0%
but as yet on the political battlefield
he's made no progress against the king.

00:21:30.405 --> 00:21:34.705 align:start position:0%
But all that is about to change.

00:21:43.805 --> 00:21:48.105 align:start position:0%
This morning the Rump Parliament are
sitting.

00:21:48.805 --> 00:21:55.305 align:start position:0%
What was once just a rumor has become
one step closer to a reality.

00:21:57.205 --> 00:22:03.105 align:start position:0%
Cromwell is introducing a new course of
action to break the stalemate.

00:22:03.505 --> 00:22:11.205 align:start position:0%
A committee has drafted an act,
what at the time is called an ordinance,

00:22:08.405 --> 00:22:16.905 align:start position:0%
that the king be put on public trial and
charged with treason.

00:22:14.405 --> 00:22:21.205 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; To accuse the king of treason is
seriously problematic on on quite a few

00:22:18.905 --> 00:22:26.906 align:start position:0%
levels. Just on a basic level, how can
you have Rex v Rex? How can a king be

00:22:25.305 --> 00:22:30.206 align:start position:0%
charged with treason?
And in treason trials you're you're

00:22:28.106 --> 00:22:32.606 align:start position:0%
tried by your peers. A king has no
peers.

00:22:34.806 --> 00:22:40.906 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; When the ordinance is read, there's
uproar.

00:22:38.006 --> 00:22:46.106 align:start position:0%
So shocking is the very idea that Henry
Elsing, the clerk of the court, leaves

00:22:42.906 --> 00:22:46.106 align:start position:0%
the house in protest.

00:22:47.506 --> 00:22:54.206 align:start position:0%
For any ordinance to pass into law, it
has to go through the parliamentary

00:22:51.106 --> 00:22:57.606 align:start position:0%
process of the day.
&gt;&gt; It would

00:22:55.206 --> 00:23:02.406 align:start position:0%
be initiated in the Commons. It would be
read a number of times. It would then be

00:22:59.906 --> 00:23:07.306 align:start position:0%
sent up to the House of Lords, who if
the House of Lords confirmed it,

00:23:04.906 --> 00:23:10.706 align:start position:0%
would would then pass into well, it
would pass into law.

00:23:10.806 --> 00:23:17.306 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; But to pass this ordinance is an even
greater challenge than usual,

00:23:15.106 --> 00:23:22.806 align:start position:0%
as what is being proposed has never been
considered before.

00:23:20.206 --> 00:23:27.106 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; In the past, monarchs have been deposed,
they've been forced to abdicate, they've

00:23:24.506 --> 00:23:32.306 align:start position:0%
been killed. But what has never happened
before is no king in England had ever

00:23:29.406 --> 00:23:39.206 align:start position:0%
been put on trial
for any charge, let alone treason. By

00:23:35.906 --> 00:23:43.606 align:start position:0%
even suggesting having a treason trial,
the only punishment of of for which is

00:23:42.206 --> 00:23:47.506 align:start position:0%
death
is in itself treasonable.

00:23:51.206 --> 00:23:59.206 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; What makes Cromwell embark on this
mortally dangerous path?

00:23:57.006 --> 00:24:02.206 align:start position:0%
There's a clue in the powerful speech he
delivers to the house.

00:24:03.606 --> 00:24:11.806 align:start position:0%
If any man whatsoever should carry on
the design of deposing the king and

00:24:08.606 --> 00:24:16.606 align:start position:0%
disinheriting his posterity, or if yet
any man had any such thing, he should be

00:24:14.306 --> 00:24:20.106 align:start position:0%
the greatest traitor and rebel in the
world.

00:24:17.806 --> 00:24:25.506 align:start position:0%
But since the providence of God hath
cast this upon us, I cannot but submit

00:24:23.306 --> 00:24:29.707 align:start position:0%
to providence.
It sounds like a riddle, but basically

00:24:27.806 --> 00:24:33.707 align:start position:0%
what he's saying is that since God has
put them, the parliamentarians in this

00:24:31.707 --> 00:24:40.207 align:start position:0%
position by allowing them to win the
war, then he has no choice but to submit

00:24:36.408 --> 00:24:44.107 align:start position:0%
to God's will and try the king.
&gt;&gt; Providential thinking is very simply

00:24:42.507 --> 00:24:46.807 align:start position:0%
this idea that God's hand is in
everything, you know, so whether it's

00:24:45.207 --> 00:24:52.707 align:start position:0%
something small like
I don't know, toothache or or a cannon

00:24:49.207 --> 00:24:55.807 align:start position:0%
ball firing or who wins a war or even,
you know, whether or not you should put

00:24:53.707 --> 00:24:58.908 align:start position:0%
a king on trial for his life.
People are looking for God's hand in

00:24:57.307 --> 00:25:02.707 align:start position:0%
this. Everything is part of God's
greater design.

00:25:01.207 --> 00:25:07.908 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; What this means is there's a tremendous
anxiety about Puritanism. One is always

00:25:06.408 --> 00:25:13.507 align:start position:0%
looking
for God's providence and of course the

00:25:10.908 --> 00:25:18.307 align:start position:0%
most obvious sign of God's providence is
victory on the battlefield.

00:25:15.507 --> 00:25:23.707 align:start position:0%
If one wins, then God must want you to
win and you are thankful for that. But

00:25:21.207 --> 00:25:28.107 align:start position:0%
again and again Cromwell refers to this
idea

00:25:25.007 --> 00:25:32.908 align:start position:0%
that they are merely God's instruments.
&gt;&gt; It's really the key to him, I think.

00:25:30.107 --> 00:25:37.707 align:start position:0%
He's always looking for God's approval
or God's direction in everything he

00:25:35.107 --> 00:25:37.707 align:start position:0%
does.

00:25:41.107 --> 00:25:49.607 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The problem is that at Windsor Charles
is also equally convinced that he's

00:25:46.107 --> 00:25:49.607 align:start position:0%
God's representative on Earth.

00:25:50.007 --> 00:25:57.007 align:start position:0%
Charles spends much of his time at
worship, following the services in the

00:25:53.908 --> 00:25:57.007 align:start position:0%
Book of Common Prayer.

00:25:57.507 --> 00:26:04.507 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The Book of Common Prayer is really the
order of service for the Church in

00:26:02.707 --> 00:26:09.607 align:start position:0%
England. It's the liturgy, it's the
prayers, it's all the things that the

00:26:06.908 --> 00:26:13.707 align:start position:0%
church was expected to do and the way
that services were supposed to be

00:26:11.307 --> 00:26:18.807 align:start position:0%
conducted.
&gt;&gt; Charles is defying a Puritan law by

00:26:16.207 --> 00:26:22.408 align:start position:0%
following this book as they have banned
it.

00:26:20.707 --> 00:26:27.007 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The Puritans found the Book of Common
Prayer

00:26:23.607 --> 00:26:30.908 align:start position:0%
very much a Roman Catholic document or
at least something that smacked as they

00:26:28.908 --> 00:26:37.809 align:start position:0%
would consider it of popery.
It wasn't the vision of a much more uh

00:26:34.509 --> 00:26:42.909 align:start position:0%
equality based service. It was too
ceremonial. It was too much like Rome

00:26:41.109 --> 00:26:47.109 align:start position:0%
and the Roman Catholic Church. It was
too much like mass, which had been

00:26:45.309 --> 00:26:51.209 align:start position:0%
outlawed.
&gt;&gt; There are two books in Trinity College,

00:26:49.009 --> 00:26:56.109 align:start position:0%
Cambridge, which embody the difference
between these approaches to God, one of

00:26:53.809 --> 00:26:59.309 align:start position:0%
which is Charles's personal copy of the
Book of Common Prayer.

00:26:59.709 --> 00:27:08.609 align:start position:0%
It is wonderful to think
that this is the very book that Charles

00:27:05.809 --> 00:27:13.009 align:start position:0%
held in his hands, his Book of Common
Prayer.

00:27:10.009 --> 00:27:17.309 align:start position:0%
Here on the cover are the royal arms,
and inside

00:27:15.209 --> 00:27:24.209 align:start position:0%
an inscription,
Charles Stuart Rex, a book he took out

00:27:20.009 --> 00:27:24.209 align:start position:0%
of his closet, 1648.

00:27:25.309 --> 00:27:31.909 align:start position:0%
The Puritans may have banned the Book of
Common Prayer, but they have their own

00:27:29.509 --> 00:27:35.709 align:start position:0%
godly instruction manual. This is the
soldiers' pocket Bible, first printed in

00:27:34.109 --> 00:27:39.209 align:start position:0%
1643
and handed out to all parliamentarian

00:27:37.809 --> 00:27:44.509 align:start position:0%
troops.
It's almost like a motivational rather

00:27:41.909 --> 00:27:49.609 align:start position:0%
than a devotional manual. It's divided
into sections. Each section has an

00:27:47.409 --> 00:27:53.709 align:start position:0%
instruction for the soldier and then a
selection of verses from the Bible to

00:27:51.209 --> 00:27:59.009 align:start position:0%
justify it. For example,
a soldier must not do wickedly.

00:27:57.109 --> 00:28:03.309 align:start position:0%
A soldier must pray before he goes to
fight.

00:28:01.609 --> 00:28:08.209 align:start position:0%
And my favorite,
perhaps the most inspirational,

00:28:05.709 --> 00:28:12.909 align:start position:0%
and let soldiers and all of us know that
the very nick of time that God hath

00:28:10.109 --> 00:28:15.609 align:start position:0%
promised us help is when we see no help
in man.

00:28:15.909 --> 00:28:23.709 align:start position:0%
So we have two sides, two books.
Charles with his Book of Common Prayer,

00:28:21.509 --> 00:28:28.509 align:start position:0%
unquestionably convinced that his right
to rule is divine,

00:28:26.109 --> 00:28:32.409 align:start position:0%
and Cromwell and his new model army
marching into battle with this little

00:28:30.109 --> 00:28:36.810 align:start position:0%
Bible against their hearts, convinced
that they too are following divine will.

00:28:37.610 --> 00:28:45.410 align:start position:0%
On the 28th of December, the problem for
Charles is that God seems to have shown

00:28:42.110 --> 00:28:45.410 align:start position:0%
where his favor lies.

00:28:45.810 --> 00:28:54.410 align:start position:0%
He has lost the war and is a prisoner.
Within the mindset of the times, God has

00:28:51.010 --> 00:28:54.410 align:start position:0%
shown his displeasure.

00:28:55.710 --> 00:29:03.010 align:start position:0%
This evening in the Commons, the
proposal to try the king does survive

00:28:59.910 --> 00:29:08.310 align:start position:0%
its first reading.
It's a solution which could ultimately

00:29:04.810 --> 00:29:13.910 align:start position:0%
demand the king's life.
Parliament is one step closer to ending

00:29:11.110 --> 00:29:13.910 align:start position:0%
the impasse.

00:29:19.510 --> 00:29:25.010 align:start position:0%
Word of the potential trial of the king
is spreading out from London.

00:29:26.310 --> 00:29:33.710 align:start position:0%
Abingdon in Oxfordshire is a hotbed of
Puritan groups and home to a commoner

00:29:31.910 --> 00:29:36.310 align:start position:0%
who now steps into the political
limelight.

00:29:37.010 --> 00:29:45.510 align:start position:0%
A 26-year-old woman, the self-proclaimed
prophet Elizabeth Pool has had a vision

00:29:43.710 --> 00:29:50.910 align:start position:0%
and she's arriving to share it with the
General Council of Officers.

00:29:48.910 --> 00:29:58.610 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Who was Elizabeth Pool?
&gt;&gt; Well, nobody. Pool was um a woman who

00:29:55.410 --> 00:30:03.010 align:start position:0%
had at one time lived in Abingdon, where
we are now, um as part of a gathered

00:30:01.410 --> 00:30:05.710 align:start position:0%
church. This was a church that took
place in the kind of room we're sitting

00:30:04.910 --> 00:30:10.110 align:start position:0%
in now.
&gt;&gt; So it was a very informal church?

00:30:07.110 --> 00:30:15.010 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Exactly. There was no leader.
Instead, people would perhaps listen to

00:30:12.210 --> 00:30:21.010 align:start position:0%
a sermon from a lecturer and then they
would speak as the spirit moved them. So

00:30:17.810 --> 00:30:25.010 align:start position:0%
they believed that every person could be
possessed by the Holy Spirit, speak in

00:30:23.710 --> 00:30:29.210 align:start position:0%
the power of God.
&gt;&gt; So the the gathered churches were more

00:30:26.910 --> 00:30:32.410 align:start position:0%
perhaps egalitarian
than a traditional congregation?

00:30:31.010 --> 00:30:36.912 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; more egalitarian because there wasn't
even a vicar. So they really believed

00:30:34.710 --> 00:30:43.112 align:start position:0%
that they were overturning the hierarchy
of experts in favor of God's chosen

00:30:40.712 --> 00:30:47.312 align:start position:0%
spokespeople. And they argued that you
didn't have to have a degree from Oxford

00:30:45.512 --> 00:30:51.612 align:start position:0%
or Cambridge to speak in the power of
the spirit, that any person could. And

00:30:49.912 --> 00:30:55.512 align:start position:0%
that really opened the door to very
unconventional people taking the lead in

00:30:53.912 --> 00:30:59.412 align:start position:0%
matters of religion,
people like Elizabeth Poole.

00:30:57.412 --> 00:31:04.712 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; How did she come into contact with with
Cromwell and his his fellow leaders?

00:31:01.912 --> 00:31:08.312 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; She approached the army council because
she said she'd had a vision that was

00:31:07.112 --> 00:31:11.012 align:start position:0%
relevant to them.
&gt;&gt; That's quite a confident thing to do.

00:31:09.712 --> 00:31:14.912 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Yes, it was quite it was an
extraordinary thing to do, but that was

00:31:12.412 --> 00:31:20.012 align:start position:0%
the kind of moment that it was
historically. We're seeing a moment

00:31:17.112 --> 00:31:24.612 align:start position:0%
where very ordinary people are finding
themselves capable of overthrowing

00:31:22.312 --> 00:31:28.212 align:start position:0%
hierarchy and saying what they believe
God has told them to say.

00:31:26.312 --> 00:31:30.612 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; And what were Elizabeth's words? What
did she claim to have seen in her

00:31:29.612 --> 00:31:36.412 align:start position:0%
vision?
&gt;&gt; Well, this is where Elizabeth really

00:31:33.012 --> 00:31:43.712 align:start position:0%
played into their hands. She had a
wonderfully sustaining vision of the

00:31:40.312 --> 00:31:50.112 align:start position:0%
country as a weak, crippled, feeble
woman and the army as a strong, burly

00:31:47.712 --> 00:31:52.212 align:start position:0%
young man protecting that weak and
feeble woman.

00:31:50.912 --> 00:31:56.712 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; How extremely flattering.
&gt;&gt; It was indeed. And so, of course, they

00:31:54.412 --> 00:32:01.612 align:start position:0%
listened to her really attentively and
endorsed her. And Henry Ireton and army

00:31:59.112 --> 00:32:06.012 align:start position:0%
grandees said, I see nothing in her but
the fruits of the Holy Spirit.

00:32:03.712 --> 00:32:08.912 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; so what happened after Elizabeth had
delivered her vision to the council?

00:32:07.312 --> 00:32:12.712 align:start position:0%
What effect did it have?
&gt;&gt; Well, they were massively keen because

00:32:11.012 --> 00:32:17.712 align:start position:0%
she told them what they wanted to hear,
that they were these strong masculine

00:32:15.112 --> 00:32:23.212 align:start position:0%
figures and that the country needed them
to protect it. It was also because they

00:32:20.012 --> 00:32:26.612 align:start position:0%
really wanted God to show them some sign
that they were doing the right thing.

00:32:29.712 --> 00:32:37.512 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Elizabeth Poole seems to be confirming
everything that Cromwell and his

00:32:33.712 --> 00:32:43.813 align:start position:0%
parliamentarian allies want to hear,
but still they hesitate at the enormity

00:32:40.513 --> 00:32:43.813 align:start position:0%
of this course of action.

00:32:43.913 --> 00:32:50.713 align:start position:0%
If they pass a law which allows them to
put the king on trial for treason,

00:32:48.513 --> 00:32:54.513 align:start position:0%
they have to accept that the end result
could mean the king's death.

00:32:57.113 --> 00:33:04.313 align:start position:0%
At the Bodleian Library in Oxford
there's a document that shows the

00:33:01.313 --> 00:33:07.613 align:start position:0%
agonized soul searching that Charles's
enemies are going through.

00:33:08.513 --> 00:33:15.013 align:start position:0%
The title of this paper is a
deliberation about the execution of

00:33:12.013 --> 00:33:19.513 align:start position:0%
justice upon HR.
And the first thing that strikes me

00:33:16.113 --> 00:33:23.813 align:start position:0%
about it is how intimate it is. It's not
official, it's not formally worded, it's

00:33:21.613 --> 00:33:28.713 align:start position:0%
very private. It's as though I'm being
allowed to eavesdrop on a conversation

00:33:25.913 --> 00:33:32.813 align:start position:0%
that happened 400 years ago.
References in the text date it fairly

00:33:30.713 --> 00:33:38.313 align:start position:0%
precisely to the Christmas period of
1648. And although we don't know who the

00:33:35.713 --> 00:33:42.113 align:start position:0%
author or authors are, it does seem that
they were closely connected with the

00:33:39.713 --> 00:33:46.413 align:start position:0%
center of power, with Parliament or with
the Council.

00:33:44.013 --> 00:33:53.513 align:start position:0%
Effectively, it's a kind of rhetorical
laundry list. It starts with the prose

00:33:50.713 --> 00:33:58.713 align:start position:0%
that the execution of HR will stand with
the justice of our cause. That is is

00:33:56.813 --> 00:34:02.913 align:start position:0%
killing the king in line with what the
parliamentarians fought for.

00:34:01.313 --> 00:34:08.013 align:start position:0%
At times the document is quite
accusatory, enumerating the supposed

00:34:05.513 --> 00:34:11.213 align:start position:0%
crimes of the king against his people.
As here,

00:34:09.513 --> 00:34:16.413 align:start position:0%
would to God it were otherwise and that
he knew what a stream the blood of

00:34:13.513 --> 00:34:19.913 align:start position:0%
300,000 innocents would make. He would
have thought something of a humor and of

00:34:18.113 --> 00:34:25.713 align:start position:0%
a harrowing revenge were to be quitted
in himself rather than by holding to

00:34:22.413 --> 00:34:29.913 align:start position:0%
either seek to make that rivulet a sea.
Effectively what they're saying here is

00:34:27.413 --> 00:34:34.113 align:start position:0%
that King Charles ought to want to die
for shame at his crime against his own

00:34:31.913 --> 00:34:39.613 align:start position:0%
people.
And then the writer's doubts start.

00:34:37.413 --> 00:34:43.814 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; So here we begin with the arguments
against the execution of the king.

00:34:42.113 --> 00:34:47.614 align:start position:0%
The first of which is the state of the
parliamentarian cause.

00:34:46.114 --> 00:34:50.814 align:start position:0%
It's not the case that they're all in
unity. They're quarrelling amongst

00:34:49.114 --> 00:34:54.314 align:start position:0%
themselves. They're divided. No one
knows what to do. There are many

00:34:52.414 --> 00:34:59.414 align:start position:0%
different interests at stake. And the
authors summarize it thus.

00:34:56.314 --> 00:35:04.814 align:start position:0%
Rocks have grown out of ourselves.
It's sort of a horribly physical image

00:35:01.814 --> 00:35:07.814 align:start position:0%
of yes, dispute appearing like cankers
on the body politic.

00:35:08.914 --> 00:35:13.914 align:start position:0%
At the end of the list of arguments
against Charles's execution, we find

00:35:12.714 --> 00:35:18.914 align:start position:0%
this.
To have no kingly government at all, and

00:35:16.314 --> 00:35:24.714 align:start position:0%
then whatever lasting inlets will there
be left for torrents of mischiefs and

00:35:22.414 --> 00:35:28.914 align:start position:0%
machinations. It's a wonderfully
theatrical phrase, torrents of mischiefs

00:35:27.314 --> 00:35:32.314 align:start position:0%
and machinations.
And what they mean is

00:35:30.514 --> 00:35:35.814 align:start position:0%
if you get rid of the King, then what
kind of government what you might have?

00:35:34.514 --> 00:35:38.914 align:start position:0%
Could it be corrupt? Could it be
dangerous? Could it be worse than

00:35:37.214 --> 00:35:43.414 align:start position:0%
kingship itself?
And so the authors of the deliberation

00:35:41.414 --> 00:35:49.414 align:start position:0%
conclude
for these reasons therefore my vote is

00:35:47.114 --> 00:35:52.714 align:start position:0%
vivat rex.
May the King live.

00:35:55.114 --> 00:36:02.814 align:start position:0%
It's all too clear from this 17th
century writer's meticulous rehearsal of

00:35:59.714 --> 00:36:10.214 align:start position:0%
the arguments about the King's future.
But at Christmas 1648 there is nothing

00:36:05.814 --> 00:36:10.214 align:start position:0%
inevitable about what will come next.

00:36:18.914 --> 00:36:26.714 align:start position:0%
As the month of December draws to an
end, despite the ban on traditional

00:36:23.314 --> 00:36:30.314 align:start position:0%
Yuletide celebrations, some brave souls
are determined to have fun.

00:36:33.114 --> 00:36:41.114 align:start position:0%
In London, four playhouses have reopened
and are illegally putting on

00:36:38.314 --> 00:36:41.114 align:start position:0%
performances.

00:36:42.614 --> 00:36:48.616 align:start position:0%
This is the site of the Salisbury Court
Theatre, the last theatre to be built in

00:36:46.716 --> 00:36:53.316 align:start position:0%
London before the Puritans closed them
all down in 1642.

00:36:51.216 --> 00:36:57.616 align:start position:0%
But even in the febrile atmosphere of
London at Christmas 1648,

00:36:55.816 --> 00:37:01.016 align:start position:0%
the players at Salisbury Court believed
the show should go on.

00:36:59.516 --> 00:37:05.216 align:start position:0%
They took the opportunity to improvise a
satire on the current political

00:37:02.716 --> 00:37:09.716 align:start position:0%
situation, playing with the crown of one
of the characters. This newspaper, The

00:37:07.516 --> 00:37:15.516 align:start position:0%
Kingdom's Weekly Intelligencer, offers
an eyewitness account of what they did.

00:37:13.416 --> 00:37:19.816 align:start position:0%
In the way they oftentimes took the
crown from his head to act as the king

00:37:17.816 --> 00:37:22.416 align:start position:0%
and in sport would oftentimes put it on
again.

00:37:20.816 --> 00:37:27.116 align:start position:0%
The people, not expecting such a
pageant, looked and laughed.

00:37:25.416 --> 00:37:32.716 align:start position:0%
But sadly for the actors at Salisbury
Court, neither the laughter nor the play

00:37:29.916 --> 00:37:32.716 align:start position:0%
at last.

00:37:32.916 --> 00:37:40.316 align:start position:0%
Soldiers stormed the theatre and shut
the whole thing down.

00:37:37.916 --> 00:37:45.716 align:start position:0%
Resistance is futile.
A parliament controlled by the military

00:37:42.416 --> 00:37:45.716 align:start position:0%
is hard to defy.

00:37:48.316 --> 00:37:56.816 align:start position:0%
Trapped at Windsor, the king also has no
power to act against his captors.

00:37:54.716 --> 00:38:02.016 align:start position:0%
For Charles, the best hope of salvation
will either come from God or his family

00:37:59.416 --> 00:38:02.016 align:start position:0%
abroad.

00:38:02.316 --> 00:38:09.316 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Charles is very much a family man. He
loves his family.

00:38:06.016 --> 00:38:16.616 align:start position:0%
The kind of aloof public persona
is really quite at odds with the warm

00:38:13.316 --> 00:38:21.816 align:start position:0%
individual that he was.
He adored his wife, Henrietta Maria, and

00:38:19.416 --> 00:38:28.016 align:start position:0%
she is an exile in Paris trying
desperately to get the French to help to

00:38:25.216 --> 00:38:32.116 align:start position:0%
put pressure on Parliament and the new
Model Army.

00:38:30.216 --> 00:38:35.916 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Charles's older children, Charles,
James, and Mary, are in Holland. Mary's

00:38:34.516 --> 00:38:39.016 align:start position:0%
married to the Prince of Orange, and
they're trying to agitate from the

00:38:37.716 --> 00:38:43.516 align:start position:0%
distance.
And then there are two children in

00:38:40.516 --> 00:38:48.117 align:start position:0%
England. There's 13-year-old Elizabeth
and there's 8-year-old Henry, and they

00:38:45.016 --> 00:38:52.817 align:start position:0%
are both prisoners of Parliament.
&gt;&gt; The 13-year-old, Elizabeth, who has been

00:38:50.917 --> 00:38:56.217 align:start position:0%
writing to him, and and he has written
her a very touching letter, from which

00:38:54.617 --> 00:38:59.117 align:start position:0%
he says that he's sorry he hasn't been
writing to her much, but when he's

00:38:57.817 --> 00:39:04.417 align:start position:0%
feeling miserable, he says he doesn't
want to to write to her and and and and

00:39:01.617 --> 00:39:11.217 align:start position:0%
sort of pass on the misery essentially.
And asks her to give her little brother

00:39:06.517 --> 00:39:11.217 align:start position:0%
a kiss, the eight-year-old Henry.

00:39:21.817 --> 00:39:27.817 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; As the new month opens, the Rump
Parliament is sitting.

00:39:25.617 --> 00:39:33.317 align:start position:0%
And once again, the subject of debate is
the ordinance to try the king.

00:39:31.217 --> 00:39:36.817 align:start position:0%
Since its last reading, a committee has
been adding details to it.

00:39:37.217 --> 00:39:42.617 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; They're a committee of the House of
Commons.

00:39:39.717 --> 00:39:47.717 align:start position:0%
Uh they have been delegated the task of
drafting effectively what will become

00:39:45.417 --> 00:39:52.717 align:start position:0%
largely their charge against the king
and trying to give it some sort of

00:39:49.717 --> 00:39:58.217 align:start position:0%
statutory basis.
&gt;&gt; It's a daunting task.

00:39:55.017 --> 00:40:02.517 align:start position:0%
The committee faces two huge problems.
There are serious doubts amongst the MPs

00:40:00.517 --> 00:40:06.617 align:start position:0%
about the very legality of trying the
king.

00:40:04.017 --> 00:40:12.117 align:start position:0%
And beyond that, there's no court or
procedure through which to do it.

00:40:09.417 --> 00:40:16.317 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; They're struggling to find a structure
for something completely new.

00:40:14.217 --> 00:40:21.217 align:start position:0%
They could have tried to put him in an
ordinary court.

00:40:19.117 --> 00:40:26.117 align:start position:0%
The Court of King's Bench
would been would be the court on which

00:40:23.117 --> 00:40:30.317 align:start position:0%
treason trials would normally been held.
Um but then under the common law, then

00:40:28.717 --> 00:40:34.417 align:start position:0%
they of course they would have to have a
jury.

00:40:31.917 --> 00:40:38.617 align:start position:0%
Uh and a jury, although they can be
handpicked to a certain extent, are much

00:40:37.117 --> 00:40:43.417 align:start position:0%
less
uh capable of being controlled.

00:40:41.417 --> 00:40:48.717 align:start position:0%
And their deliberations are not
controlled. So even if you think you've

00:40:45.517 --> 00:40:53.518 align:start position:0%
got hot parliamentary men in the jury,
that may not be the way it it pans out.

00:40:54.818 --> 00:41:00.518 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The committee have come up with a
solution. They will create a temporary

00:40:58.618 --> 00:41:04.418 align:start position:0%
high court which will sit for just a
month with the sole purpose of trying

00:41:02.818 --> 00:41:07.918 align:start position:0%
the king.
And instead of the jury, they'll appoint

00:41:06.218 --> 00:41:14.318 align:start position:0%
carefully vetted
what they call commissioners who will

00:41:10.118 --> 00:41:14.318 align:start position:0%
act as judge and jury in one.

00:41:14.418 --> 00:41:21.018 align:start position:0%
This still doesn't get over the problem
of the dubious legal basis for trying a

00:41:19.018 --> 00:41:29.018 align:start position:0%
king.
How can you have Rex versus Rex?

00:41:25.318 --> 00:41:36.918 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The law of treason is really fashioned
to protect the king, not put him on

00:41:31.718 --> 00:41:43.318 align:start position:0%
trial. And it was a real conundrum until
a very brilliant lawyer solved it for

00:41:39.218 --> 00:41:50.518 align:start position:0%
Cromwell by charging tyranny,
which in our modern term is like

00:41:46.918 --> 00:41:58.118 align:start position:0%
committing crimes against humanity,
killing or civilians, torturing

00:41:54.018 --> 00:42:04.818 align:start position:0%
prisoners, that kind of behavior. And of
course, fighting, essentially declaring

00:42:01.818 --> 00:42:10.118 align:start position:0%
war on the English people,
which the king did.

00:42:06.418 --> 00:42:16.918 align:start position:0%
So that was the thinking finally that
broke this conundrum. Yes, a king can

00:42:14.218 --> 00:42:22.918 align:start position:0%
commit treason if he
makes war, kills his own civilian

00:42:20.418 --> 00:42:22.918 align:start position:0%
people.

00:42:24.218 --> 00:42:30.018 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Oliver Cromwell is amongst the MPs
debating that day, and he's been named

00:42:28.318 --> 00:42:34.718 align:start position:0%
as commissioner. He's on the committee
that's drafting the ordinance.

00:42:32.618 --> 00:42:40.418 align:start position:0%
But it's hard to find any evidence of
exactly what he's doing or, more

00:42:36.918 --> 00:42:46.418 align:start position:0%
importantly, thinking at this time.
There are no letters or documents

00:42:42.318 --> 00:42:46.418 align:start position:0%
detailing his thoughts or speeches.

00:42:47.118 --> 00:42:53.320 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The point is about Cromwell, he's always
elusive.

00:42:50.418 --> 00:42:56.020 align:start position:0%
He's there is this very famous phrase
that's attached to Cromwell that he's

00:42:54.720 --> 00:43:02.220 align:start position:0%
practiced
at not knowing. He's always slightly off

00:42:58.420 --> 00:43:07.220 align:start position:0%
stage. He's always someone who
manages not to get his hands dirty when

00:43:05.620 --> 00:43:11.020 align:start position:0%
a major political operation is taking
place.

00:43:09.020 --> 00:43:16.120 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It's a mistake made by a lot of
historians to think somehow that

00:43:13.420 --> 00:43:20.520 align:start position:0%
Cromwell is doing everything
and is planning

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:26.520 align:start position:0%
the death of the king and the republic.
That's not the case. Cromwell

00:43:23.020 --> 00:43:33.120 align:start position:0%
he's in a state of wonderment.
He's waiting on God. He's waiting for

00:43:29.320 --> 00:43:33.120 align:start position:0%
these divine signs.

00:43:35.020 --> 00:43:42.120 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Cromwell may well be waiting for a sign,
but parliamentary process is rolling on.

00:43:43.020 --> 00:43:48.820 align:start position:0%
The majority of MPs in the Commons
accept the committee's recommendations.

00:43:49.220 --> 00:43:55.820 align:start position:0%
The ordinance has passed another crucial
stage, but it's still not law.

00:43:56.220 --> 00:44:00.520 align:start position:0%
To do this, it has to go to the Lords.

00:44:12.220 --> 00:44:18.820 align:start position:0%
The very next morning, the ordinance to
try the king is sent up to the House of

00:44:16.220 --> 00:44:18.820 align:start position:0%
Lords.

00:44:20.120 --> 00:44:26.620 align:start position:0%
They are faced with the job of
scrutinizing this radical new

00:44:23.420 --> 00:44:34.520 align:start position:0%
legislation and, if they agree, taking
it one step closer to making it law.

00:44:30.820 --> 00:44:40.120 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; They asked the advice of the three
judges who had been named as those who

00:44:37.720 --> 00:44:46.220 align:start position:0%
were to preside over
the High Court of Justice. And all three

00:44:42.520 --> 00:44:50.320 align:start position:0%
judges said categorically that the whole
thing was a legal farce.

00:44:48.620 --> 00:44:54.321 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The House of Lords at this stage is only
really about a dozen peers, not

00:44:52.320 --> 00:44:57.921 align:start position:0%
royalists. They're kept away. So we're
talking about parliamentarian lords.

00:44:56.521 --> 00:45:00.921 align:start position:0%
They're mainly sort of veteran
campaigners, people like the Earl of

00:44:59.321 --> 00:45:04.621 align:start position:0%
Manchester who commanded the Eastern
Association in the first civil war.

00:45:05.521 --> 00:45:13.121 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Despite being parliamentarians,
helping to pass a law to actually put

00:45:10.121 --> 00:45:16.721 align:start position:0%
the king on trial and possibly to death
is a step too far.

00:45:17.121 --> 00:45:23.921 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; There was a good deal of nervousness.
The Lord Chancellor had jumped in a

00:45:21.721 --> 00:45:27.321 align:start position:0%
carriage to get away.
The Lord Chief Justice had gone to the

00:45:25.821 --> 00:45:32.721 align:start position:0%
country. People
of high position

00:45:29.521 --> 00:45:38.621 align:start position:0%
were well aware of the treason act and
that it was treason to depose the king

00:45:36.321 --> 00:45:44.721 align:start position:0%
and they were extremely nervous. So the
lords were very reluctant to deal with

00:45:41.421 --> 00:45:44.721 align:start position:0%
that. They went to water.

00:45:49.121 --> 00:45:54.221 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The lords vote against the ordinance and
then declare that they're adjourning for

00:45:53.021 --> 00:45:57.321 align:start position:0%
a week.
I think they hope that by the time they

00:45:55.621 --> 00:46:00.121 align:start position:0%
come back the whole business will just
have been dropped.

00:46:02.121 --> 00:46:09.621 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; If the lords' ploy works, it might halt
the wheels of the bureaucratic machine,

00:46:07.521 --> 00:46:13.121 align:start position:0%
but if it doesn't, everything is moving
towards the king's death.

00:46:14.821 --> 00:46:24.121 align:start position:0%
It's hard to know how much of this drama
being played out in Westminster is known

00:46:19.821 --> 00:46:27.521 align:start position:0%
by Charles, 25 miles away in Windsor.
&gt;&gt; There is sort of a sense with Charles

00:46:25.821 --> 00:46:31.721 align:start position:0%
right now that he's he's sort of
floating above it all and he's he

00:46:28.821 --> 00:46:35.221 align:start position:0%
doesn't really seem to have comprehended
the gravity of the situation.

00:46:34.021 --> 00:46:38.921 align:start position:0%
So sometimes, you know, he might even be
talking about planting melons in

00:46:36.921 --> 00:46:42.521 align:start position:0%
Wimbledon.
And previously he was talking about sort

00:46:40.421 --> 00:46:46.121 align:start position:0%
of rebuilding of Whitehall. So this is
sense I think that he's just sort of

00:46:43.821 --> 00:46:48.921 align:start position:0%
biding his time and and he I'm sure that
God will protect him.

00:46:49.521 --> 00:46:54.121 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Much of what we know about Charles at
this time

00:46:52.321 --> 00:46:58.723 align:start position:0%
comes from his servant Thomas Herbert's
memoir.

00:46:56.621 --> 00:47:04.023 align:start position:0%
He gives little insight into the king's
thoughts and feelings, but he does tell

00:47:01.223 --> 00:47:04.023 align:start position:0%
one story.

00:47:04.823 --> 00:47:11.723 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Thomas Herbert was Charles's gentleman
of the bedchamber, and he tells in his

00:47:09.223 --> 00:47:18.622 align:start position:0%
account of Charles's time of Windsor of
one episode where the diamond seal of

00:47:15.223 --> 00:47:22.523 align:start position:0%
Charles's timepiece was lost, and
Charles instructed one of his servants

00:47:20.323 --> 00:47:26.823 align:start position:0%
to look for it, and the servant looked
high and low and couldn't find it,

00:47:25.223 --> 00:47:29.823 align:start position:0%
and Charles was sort of disturbed and
unsettled by this.

00:47:30.922 --> 00:47:39.023 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; For Charles, denuded at the splendor of
his kingly court, a loss like this

00:47:36.122 --> 00:47:43.622 align:start position:0%
assumed a huge importance.
Thomas Herbert searched everywhere for

00:47:41.122 --> 00:47:48.122 align:start position:0%
the king's seal without success.
But in the next installment of this

00:47:45.223 --> 00:47:52.723 align:start position:0%
gripping saga, he recalls that a little
before his majesty went to bed, a good

00:47:50.523 --> 00:47:57.122 align:start position:0%
charcoal fire being in the chamber and
wax lights burning, the king cast his

00:47:55.122 --> 00:48:01.223 align:start position:0%
eye to one end of the room and saw
something sparkle, and pointing with his

00:47:59.523 --> 00:48:06.622 align:start position:0%
finger, bade Mr. Herbert to take a
candle and see what it was. By good

00:48:04.122 --> 00:48:13.723 align:start position:0%
providence, it was the diamond, which he
took up and found his majesty's arms in

00:48:09.323 --> 00:48:13.723 align:start position:0%
it, and with joy brought it to the king.

00:48:15.922 --> 00:48:23.922 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It's a sort of odd event to record, but
I think it does show how in a certainly

00:48:21.422 --> 00:48:28.223 align:start position:0%
as a symbol of Charles's kingly
authority, the trappings of his

00:48:25.723 --> 00:48:31.922 align:start position:0%
ceremony, the splendor, the magnificence
of his position

00:48:29.723 --> 00:48:37.523 align:start position:0%
gradually being lost, and he was that
left to kind of try and find the

00:48:34.823 --> 00:48:41.122 align:start position:0%
remnants of things like the diamond seal
of his timepiece, but actually, of

00:48:39.122 --> 00:48:44.023 align:start position:0%
course, time was ticking, and really he
knew

00:48:42.223 --> 00:48:48.523 align:start position:0%
that perhaps the end was coming and
certainly his kingly authority was

00:48:45.823 --> 00:48:48.523 align:start position:0%
ebbing away.

00:48:53.122 --> 00:48:58.023 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It's two days since the Lords blocked
the rump parliament's attempts to pass a

00:48:57.424 --> 00:49:02.024 align:start position:0%
a
to put the on trial for treason.

00:49:02.124 --> 00:49:10.924 align:start position:0%
Hiding in their country houses, the
Lords are hoping that in London the

00:49:06.124 --> 00:49:10.924 align:start position:0%
whole business has just gone away.

00:49:11.324 --> 00:49:17.924 align:start position:0%
But Cromwell and the Commons have no
intention of letting this happen.

00:49:15.824 --> 00:49:20.924 align:start position:0%
Now they do something truly
revolutionary.

00:49:22.424 --> 00:49:29.524 align:start position:0%
This amazing document is the original
manuscript of the House of Commons

00:49:27.524 --> 00:49:33.824 align:start position:0%
Journal, an account of the daily
business of the House.

00:49:31.624 --> 00:49:38.824 align:start position:0%
It's more than just a record. It's a
minute-by-minute live witnessing of the

00:49:36.824 --> 00:49:47.824 align:start position:0%
revolutionary events that were taking
place here over Christmas 1648.

00:49:43.324 --> 00:49:51.324 align:start position:0%
And here we have the 4th of January.
And this

00:49:48.924 --> 00:49:56.724 align:start position:0%
unassuming little paragraph
is what I'm looking for.

00:49:54.124 --> 00:50:02.224 align:start position:0%
Resolved that the Commons of England in
Parliament assembled do declare that the

00:49:59.324 --> 00:50:06.324 align:start position:0%
people under God are the origin of all
just power

00:50:04.224 --> 00:50:12.224 align:start position:0%
and do also declare that the Commons of
England in Parliament assembled being

00:50:08.524 --> 00:50:18.924 align:start position:0%
chosen by and representing the people
have the supreme power in this nation.

00:50:16.024 --> 00:50:22.524 align:start position:0%
These two little paragraphs contain a
revolution.

00:50:20.824 --> 00:50:26.524 align:start position:0%
They're saying that the House of Commons
is the sovereign power in the country

00:50:25.124 --> 00:50:32.124 align:start position:0%
and that they have no need of the
support of the Lords or of the king.

00:50:29.824 --> 00:50:38.824 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; In other words, we're going ahead.
Britain, or at least England, is now a

00:50:36.424 --> 00:50:41.224 align:start position:0%
republic. Although they don't realize
that, they don't

00:50:40.124 --> 00:50:47.124 align:start position:0%
get this
get what the implications of their

00:50:44.224 --> 00:50:54.924 align:start position:0%
actions are on the 4th of January. But
the direct implication is that England

00:50:50.824 --> 00:50:59.024 align:start position:0%
is ruled directly by Parliament and by
the House of Commons.

00:50:57.124 --> 00:51:04.925 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; You know, it it feels really kind of
technical and and and and, you know,

00:51:01.424 --> 00:51:08.625 align:start position:0%
picky to us, But it's a it it a kind of
constitutional revolution, because

00:51:06.425 --> 00:51:14.025 align:start position:0%
what's happening is the Commons is
passing an act without any intervention

00:51:11.925 --> 00:51:18.625 align:start position:0%
from the Lords, without any intervention
from from the King.

00:51:15.825 --> 00:51:21.525 align:start position:0%
So that in itself Well, let's say it was
it was it was revolutionary.

00:51:19.725 --> 00:51:25.325 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; And it's sort of circumventing
parliamentary process. It's laughing in

00:51:23.425 --> 00:51:29.125 align:start position:0%
the face of the rule of law. And as
Charles himself says actually, you know,

00:51:26.825 --> 00:51:32.525 align:start position:0%
if if power without law can make law,
can alter the fundamental laws of the

00:51:31.425 --> 00:51:37.725 align:start position:0%
kingdom,
then you know, no subject is safe in his

00:51:34.325 --> 00:51:37.725 align:start position:0%
person or his property.

00:51:40.625 --> 00:51:48.525 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; In politics, everything is moving
towards putting the King on trial.

00:51:46.225 --> 00:51:51.925 align:start position:0%
The country is inching closer to calling
the King to account

00:51:50.425 --> 00:51:54.725 align:start position:0%
for his role in the bloodshed of the
Civil War.

00:51:55.025 --> 00:52:01.325 align:start position:0%
But God is never far from politics, and
despite the revolution in parliamentary

00:51:59.525 --> 00:52:06.925 align:start position:0%
procedure,
those in power still want reassurance of

00:52:03.825 --> 00:52:06.925 align:start position:0%
divine approval.

00:52:07.425 --> 00:52:14.825 align:start position:0%
And in Abingdon, Elizabeth Poole, who a
week earlier prophesied that Parliament

00:52:12.225 --> 00:52:17.925 align:start position:0%
was the protector of the nation, now has
a new vision.

00:52:18.725 --> 00:52:25.325 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; So Elizabeth went back to the Council on
the 5th of January.

00:52:22.225 --> 00:52:26.525 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Yes, and that was also when she produced
this.

00:52:25.925 --> 00:52:33.125 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Wow.
&gt;&gt; Um which is a publication of her vision.

00:52:30.625 --> 00:52:35.425 align:start position:0%
Um so she went public with what she was
telling them.

00:52:33.825 --> 00:52:38.125 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; She sold her story to the papers.
&gt;&gt; Pretty much, yeah.

00:52:36.425 --> 00:52:41.625 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; What did she actually say?
&gt;&gt; Well, this is where I would I would

00:52:39.725 --> 00:52:46.725 align:start position:0%
suggest that what she did was in um
offered a gloss on her vision.

00:52:44.425 --> 00:52:52.425 align:start position:0%
Whereas the vision had been interpreted
by the Council of State as this

00:52:49.125 --> 00:52:55.425 align:start position:0%
immensely ego-salving tale of them as
healthy young men.

00:52:54.025 --> 00:53:00.925 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; A flattering endorsement.
&gt;&gt; Indeed. Poole was evidently dismayed by

00:52:58.625 --> 00:53:05.527 align:start position:0%
the way that they interpreted and in a
way nicked her prophecy as an

00:53:03.225 --> 00:53:10.627 align:start position:0%
endorsement to put the King on trial for
his life. She said something that really

00:53:07.827 --> 00:53:18.427 align:start position:0%
devastated the council's egos. She
recast her vision and explained that for

00:53:14.127 --> 00:53:24.227 align:start position:0%
her the king was the husband and
Parliament, including the army council,

00:53:21.527 --> 00:53:28.527 align:start position:0%
were the wife. And therefore she said,
I'm afraid you can't divorce your

00:53:26.527 --> 00:53:32.827 align:start position:0%
husband because that is the case in
English law. You never heard, she said,

00:53:30.827 --> 00:53:36.527 align:start position:0%
that a wife might put away her husband.
And then to make it a bit worse, she

00:53:34.727 --> 00:53:41.027 align:start position:0%
went on as follows.
For all that pass by and behold her will

00:53:38.627 --> 00:53:45.827 align:start position:0%
say this was a strumpet and not a
faithful wife that rejoiceth at the fall

00:53:43.427 --> 00:53:49.227 align:start position:0%
of her husband. And contrariwise the
faithful wife mourneth in secret for

00:53:48.127 --> 00:53:53.727 align:start position:0%
him.
So she's now casting Parliament as

00:53:52.327 --> 00:53:57.127 align:start position:0%
strumpet-like.
&gt;&gt; That's interesting, isn't it? Because

00:53:55.027 --> 00:54:02.127 align:start position:0%
from being sort of a strong, virile,
masculine figure, Elizabeth is then in

00:54:00.027 --> 00:54:07.527 align:start position:0%
terms of the time reducing them to a
feminine role and one which is sexually

00:54:05.727 --> 00:54:10.727 align:start position:0%
intemperate strumpet. I mean strumpet
means tart, doesn't it?

00:54:08.927 --> 00:54:15.927 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely. And we think
parliamentary language is violent now.

00:54:14.027 --> 00:54:19.227 align:start position:0%
I think you would probably be rebuked by
the speaker if you called Boris Johnson

00:54:17.327 --> 00:54:24.027 align:start position:0%
a prostitute.
Exactly, but you can also go with the

00:54:21.027 --> 00:54:28.127 align:start position:0%
army council not overjoyed to hear this.
&gt;&gt; What was the reaction of the council to

00:54:26.127 --> 00:54:30.627 align:start position:0%
Elizabeth's words? How did they respond
to that?

00:54:28.727 --> 00:54:38.927 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Very unenthusiastic as you would
imagine. They strongly wanted to be able

00:54:33.427 --> 00:54:38.927 align:start position:0%
to say, see, God is totally on our side.

00:54:43.127 --> 00:54:47.627 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The 5th of January is known as Twelfth
Night.

00:54:48.527 --> 00:54:55.727 align:start position:0%
The night when Christmas celebrations
come to a raucous end.

00:54:53.427 --> 00:55:02.327 align:start position:0%
It's a night of misrule when lords
become servants and servants lords. The

00:54:58.427 --> 00:55:08.428 align:start position:0%
world is turned upside down.
Elizabeth Paul's new prophecy could have

00:55:04.727 --> 00:55:12.628 align:start position:0%
upturned Parliament's course of action.
But for Cromwell, events have now

00:55:10.228 --> 00:55:16.528 align:start position:0%
overtaken any doubts.
In his providential worldview,

00:55:15.228 --> 00:55:22.228 align:start position:0%
the fact that the Commons are now the
sole power in Parliament shows where

00:55:18.928 --> 00:55:22.228 align:start position:0%
God's favor lies.

00:55:23.928 --> 00:55:32.628 align:start position:0%
So for Cromwell on Twelfth Night,
everything is, ironically, in order.

00:55:29.528 --> 00:55:37.228 align:start position:0%
But for Charles, this night of misrule
is fast becoming a permanent reality.

00:55:44.228 --> 00:55:50.928 align:start position:0%
On the 6th of January, two days after
the Commons have made themselves the

00:55:48.528 --> 00:55:56.328 align:start position:0%
supreme authority in the realm, they
pass a law to put the king on trial for

00:55:53.828 --> 00:55:56.328 align:start position:0%
treason.

00:55:59.228 --> 00:56:06.328 align:start position:0%
Finally, the stalemate is broken.
Parliament have made their move.

00:56:04.128 --> 00:56:12.828 align:start position:0%
Now the king will be forced to account
publicly for his part in the Civil War.

00:56:10.528 --> 00:56:16.928 align:start position:0%
The Commons choose Colonel Miles Corbet
to inform the king.

00:56:14.528 --> 00:56:22.128 align:start position:0%
He's known amongst royalists as
bull-headed, splay-footed, bacon-faced

00:56:19.728 --> 00:56:26.728 align:start position:0%
Corbet.
According to reports, Charles receives

00:56:24.128 --> 00:56:31.928 align:start position:0%
the news with his usual calm.
Later, he comes to walk here on the

00:56:28.428 --> 00:56:37.728 align:start position:0%
terrace, looking sadly towards London.
No one, he says, has the right to try

00:56:34.128 --> 00:56:41.728 align:start position:0%
him except his God and his conscience.
Cromwell has decided otherwise.

00:56:42.828 --> 00:56:50.128 align:start position:0%
During the 12 days of Christmas, what
was once almost unthinkable has now

00:56:48.128 --> 00:56:55.128 align:start position:0%
become reality.
Parliamentary procedure has been

00:56:52.128 --> 00:56:58.128 align:start position:0%
overruled and fundamental constitutional
change made.

00:57:00.328 --> 00:57:09.028 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; To try a king for treason is such an
outrageous, outlandish thing. I mean,

00:57:07.628 --> 00:57:12.629 align:start position:0%
this is a man
that can cure people just by touching

00:57:10.929 --> 00:57:17.529 align:start position:0%
them. You know, there is something
Christ-like about him to many people.

00:57:15.129 --> 00:57:21.729 align:start position:0%
So to try him is is
not only radical, but it's it's almost

00:57:19.429 --> 00:57:25.729 align:start position:0%
blasphemous.
&gt;&gt; A law may have been passed in

00:57:23.429 --> 00:57:32.129 align:start position:0%
Westminster, but now the challenge for
Cromwell and Parliament is to enact it.

00:57:29.129 --> 00:57:38.329 align:start position:0%
It just hasn't ever been done.
And nobody really knows how to put a

00:57:34.729 --> 00:57:38.329 align:start position:0%
king on trial.

00:57:41.729 --> 00:57:50.629 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Next time, the tension rises in London.
&gt;&gt; The people of London are in a state of

00:57:47.929 --> 00:57:56.829 align:start position:0%
great uncertainty. They're fearful.
They're edgy.

00:57:52.829 --> 00:58:01.129 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Some of Cromwell's allies get cold feet.
&gt;&gt; We're talking about deal or no deal.

00:57:59.529 --> 00:58:04.629 align:start position:0%
It's being called bargaining with
menaces.

00:58:02.429 --> 00:58:09.729 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; And Parliament struggles to find enough
men willing to look the king in the eye

00:58:07.529 --> 00:58:16.729 align:start position:0%
and sentence him to death.
&gt;&gt; They knew that most of these people

00:58:12.929 --> 00:58:16.729 align:start position:0%
would not take part.

00:58:49.729 --> 00:58:56.729 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It's January 1649.
Parliament has voted to put King Charles

00:58:55.029 --> 00:58:59.529 align:start position:0%
I on trial
for treason.

00:59:00.329 --> 00:59:05.129 align:start position:0%
If he's found guilty, he'll be executed.

00:59:05.429 --> 00:59:11.029 align:start position:0%
Parliament are demanding that Charles be
made to pay for his role in the conflict

00:59:09.329 --> 00:59:16.031 align:start position:0%
of the Civil War.
But how will the people of England when

00:59:13.031 --> 00:59:21.531 align:start position:0%
the news is made public?
And how exactly do you put a king on

00:59:19.431 --> 00:59:25.931 align:start position:0%
trial?
This confrontation comes after seven

00:59:23.931 --> 00:59:30.731 align:start position:0%
years of civil war
that has divided towns, friends, and

00:59:28.731 --> 00:59:36.631 align:start position:0%
even families.
&gt;&gt; There is nothing civil about civil war.

00:59:34.131 --> 00:59:41.331 align:start position:0%
They have seen so much suffering and
death and destruction. They've just had

00:59:39.231 --> 00:59:46.631 align:start position:0%
enough.
&gt;&gt; I'm historian Lisa Hilton.

00:59:43.831 --> 00:59:51.831 align:start position:0%
And over three episodes, I'm tracing day
by day, hour by hour, the five weeks

00:59:49.931 --> 00:59:59.931 align:start position:0%
that led to one of the most seismic
moments in our constitutional history.

00:59:54.831 --> 00:59:59.931 align:start position:0%
The trial and death of King Charles I.

01:00:00.931 --> 01:00:06.931 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; No king in England had ever been put on
trial

01:00:04.231 --> 01:00:11.931 align:start position:0%
for any charge, let alone treason. It
was unprecedented.

01:00:09.731 --> 01:00:18.531 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; How did the nation affect this radical
step, the reverberations of which are

01:00:14.431 --> 01:00:24.031 align:start position:0%
still felt even to this day?
&gt;&gt; What they did do change English law and

01:00:21.431 --> 01:00:29.431 align:start position:0%
politics forever.
&gt;&gt; Hidden within these weeks are the chance

01:00:26.431 --> 01:00:35.131 align:start position:0%
events, secret manipulations, and
revolutionary actions which led to this

01:00:32.031 --> 01:00:39.631 align:start position:0%
ultimate decision.
How did a country kill its king?

01:00:54.631 --> 01:01:02.131 align:start position:0%
Tuesday, the 9th of January 1649
dawns cold in the city of London.

01:01:03.031 --> 01:01:07.731 align:start position:0%
The people of Cheapside are going about
their business.

01:01:05.931 --> 01:01:11.531 align:start position:0%
Shopkeepers and merchants are trading
wares.

01:01:09.131 --> 01:01:15.232 align:start position:0%
Then, to the sound of drum beats, a
parliamentary official approaches a

01:01:13.531 --> 01:01:19.432 align:start position:0%
gathering crowd.
He's accompanied by armed guards.

01:01:19.732 --> 01:01:24.932 align:start position:0%
He has a message to deliver to the
public.

01:01:22.932 --> 01:01:27.932 align:start position:0%
And what he's about to say is
incendiary.

01:01:28.232 --> 01:01:34.532 align:start position:0%
This is a copy of the proclamation. It
states that Charles Stuart, the King of

01:01:32.632 --> 01:01:38.532 align:start position:0%
England, is to be put on trial for
treason. A special high court has been

01:01:37.132 --> 01:01:42.832 align:start position:0%
established to bring the king to
justice.

01:01:39.832 --> 01:01:48.632 align:start position:0%
What was merely days ago just a wild
threat is now a reality.

01:01:46.432 --> 01:01:52.932 align:start position:0%
Never before has an English monarch been
put in the dock.

01:01:50.132 --> 01:01:59.832 align:start position:0%
If Charles is found guilty, the sentence
is death. Regicide, the killing of a

01:01:55.932 --> 01:01:59.832 align:start position:0%
king, is now a possibility.

01:02:03.132 --> 01:02:11.132 align:start position:0%
For centuries, the idea that kings and
queens are anointed by God has been the

01:02:08.632 --> 01:02:17.232 align:start position:0%
very basis of monarchy.
The English people have accepted and

01:02:12.932 --> 01:02:17.232 align:start position:0%
obeyed the king's divine power.

01:02:19.032 --> 01:02:24.532 align:start position:0%
And this is the most significant symbol
of that relationship.

01:02:25.532 --> 01:02:32.532 align:start position:0%
This is the great seal of England,
imprinted in wax and attached to the

01:02:30.532 --> 01:02:35.532 align:start position:0%
royal charter of the Worshipful Company
of Clockmakers.

01:02:37.932 --> 01:02:46.432 align:start position:0%
It is such a special object to be close
to. It's completely intact, and you can

01:02:44.032 --> 01:02:51.532 align:start position:0%
really see the refinement and definition
of the sculptural relief. It's much more

01:02:49.132 --> 01:02:55.432 align:start position:0%
than a functional symbol. It's really an
artwork in its own right.

01:02:53.932 --> 01:03:00.132 align:start position:0%
It shows Charles I
enthroned, a very regal symbol that was

01:02:58.032 --> 01:03:04.532 align:start position:0%
designed to impress.
This is the principal seal of royal

01:03:02.332 --> 01:03:09.632 align:start position:0%
authority, attached to all the most
important state documents as a guarantee

01:03:07.632 --> 01:03:13.632 align:start position:0%
of royal ascent.
It's part of a tradition that goes back

01:03:11.032 --> 01:03:17.932 align:start position:0%
to before the Norman Conquest, a visual
statement of the monarch as the ultimate

01:03:15.732 --> 01:03:22.433 align:start position:0%
legal authority.
Tradition also has it that when a

01:03:20.133 --> 01:03:28.133 align:start position:0%
monarch died, the silver molds used to
make such seals would be broken up by

01:03:24.633 --> 01:03:28.133 align:start position:0%
the newly enthroned monarch.

01:03:29.133 --> 01:03:38.033 align:start position:0%
On the same day as the proclamation
about the king's trial is made,

01:03:33.533 --> 01:03:38.033 align:start position:0%
Parliament takes another bold step.

01:03:38.133 --> 01:03:45.933 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The House of Commons on the 9th of
January voted that the great seal of

01:03:42.433 --> 01:03:51.733 align:start position:0%
England should be destroyed and a new
one made, and they start to think over

01:03:48.533 --> 01:03:51.733 align:start position:0%
the design of the new one.

01:03:52.033 --> 01:03:57.533 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; This is the great seal of the
Commonwealth of England attached to the

01:03:55.933 --> 01:04:00.733 align:start position:0%
charter of the Worshipful Company of
Frame Knitters.

01:03:59.133 --> 01:04:05.433 align:start position:0%
Only a fragment of the original
survives, but in this replica you can

01:04:03.533 --> 01:04:09.133 align:start position:0%
see that it depicts the interior of the
House of Commons.

01:04:07.133 --> 01:04:13.433 align:start position:0%
Instead of a king enthroned, it shows
the speaker in the chair.

01:04:11.233 --> 01:04:21.233 align:start position:0%
The smashing of Charles's seal is
therefore a hugely symbolic act.

01:04:16.633 --> 01:04:21.233 align:start position:0%
No seal means no power over the people.

01:04:21.433 --> 01:04:29.033 align:start position:0%
We are still a long way from killing a
king, but the destruction of his seal is

01:04:26.333 --> 01:04:29.033 align:start position:0%
ominous.

01:04:29.633 --> 01:04:36.133 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Cromwell
has decided that Charles is a man of

01:04:35.033 --> 01:04:39.333 align:start position:0%
blood
and has to be brought to account in some

01:04:38.233 --> 01:04:42.533 align:start position:0%
way.
That doesn't mean for a moment that he

01:04:41.033 --> 01:04:49.333 align:start position:0%
thinks Charles
should be executed.

01:04:44.433 --> 01:04:49.333 align:start position:0%
Cromwell is a conservative by nature.

01:04:51.733 --> 01:04:58.833 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Whether or not Cromwell intends Charles
to die, England is contemplating a

01:04:56.633 --> 01:05:03.433 align:start position:0%
future without a king.
There's been no talk of choosing a

01:05:00.533 --> 01:05:08.833 align:start position:0%
relative, perhaps his youngest son, to
rule in his stead.

01:05:05.233 --> 01:05:12.533 align:start position:0%
This is a significant silence.
&gt;&gt; I think the the monarchy is on trial at

01:05:11.133 --> 01:05:17.433 align:start position:0%
this point as well. It's not just
Charles. And I think the army has

01:05:14.633 --> 01:05:22.935 align:start position:0%
convinced itself or a lot of the army
has convinced himself itself by 1649

01:05:20.935 --> 01:05:27.035 align:start position:0%
Um, that a republic is possible, I think
it's it's definitely something which is

01:05:24.935 --> 01:05:32.035 align:start position:0%
there. It's definitely a possibility,
and I think a lot of the army has

01:05:28.735 --> 01:05:32.035 align:start position:0%
already become convinced about it.

01:05:32.335 --> 01:05:37.035 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; There are some out-and-out republicans
who want to sort of throw a wrecking

01:05:35.335 --> 01:05:41.935 align:start position:0%
ball at the monarchy, but there aren't
that many. I think most of them, it's

01:05:38.835 --> 01:05:45.935 align:start position:0%
about religion. It's about
bringing the king to justice, and they

01:05:44.035 --> 01:05:49.935 align:start position:0%
see him as as this man of blood. You
know, they look at the book of numbers,

01:05:47.535 --> 01:05:54.535 align:start position:0%
and they think that the soil has been
defiled by blood, and that only by the

01:05:52.335 --> 01:05:58.335 align:start position:0%
blood of he
that caused it, i.e. Charles, can they

01:05:56.835 --> 01:06:02.035 align:start position:0%
start to be cleansed? Can they start to
move on? And I think that's mainly the

01:06:00.135 --> 01:06:06.335 align:start position:0%
opinion rather than the republic.
They're not looking to destroy the

01:06:03.835 --> 01:06:06.335 align:start position:0%
monarchy.

01:06:07.435 --> 01:06:13.735 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; But where do the people, Charles's
subjects, stand?

01:06:11.435 --> 01:06:17.935 align:start position:0%
As the proclamation is read out across
London, news of the trial spreads

01:06:16.135 --> 01:06:24.135 align:start position:0%
throughout the city.
It's on record that the crowds are

01:06:19.935 --> 01:06:28.135 align:start position:0%
without violence, injury, or affront.
No cheers of support, no cries of

01:06:26.735 --> 01:06:32.735 align:start position:0%
protest.
A strange neutrality prevails.

01:06:35.035 --> 01:06:42.735 align:start position:0%
What is certain is the public are worn
down, exhausted and resentful after

01:06:40.435 --> 01:06:46.235 align:start position:0%
years of civil war.
A foreign visitor comments that the

01:06:44.235 --> 01:06:53.635 align:start position:0%
people who formerly been friendly and
good-humored are now melancholy,

01:06:49.335 --> 01:06:53.635 align:start position:0%
spiteful, as if bewitched.

01:06:55.835 --> 01:07:05.035 align:start position:0%
The day after the order is given to
destroy the king's seal, the date of the

01:07:00.035 --> 01:07:05.035 align:start position:0%
trial is set for 10 days time.

01:07:08.635 --> 01:07:18.235 align:start position:0%
135 commissioners have been appointed to
this hastily convened high court.

01:07:14.235 --> 01:07:21.835 align:start position:0%
They will be judge and jury.
They're summoning to meet at the Painted

01:07:19.735 --> 01:07:25.236 align:start position:0%
Chamber in Westminster Palace to make
preparations for the trial.

01:07:25.436 --> 01:07:33.436 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Commissioners are both judges and jury.
It's a combination of

01:07:30.336 --> 01:07:37.536 align:start position:0%
both roles. They decide on fact and law.
So they decide on all aspects of the

01:07:35.136 --> 01:07:41.736 align:start position:0%
case and all aspects of the trial. They
are needed for legitimacy. They are

01:07:39.236 --> 01:07:47.436 align:start position:0%
needed to emphasize that this isn't the
decision of a single individual nor a

01:07:44.836 --> 01:07:52.736 align:start position:0%
single judge, not a single politician,
but a collection of military men, a

01:07:50.036 --> 01:07:56.736 align:start position:0%
collection of legal men, a collection of
people. It it is a people's decision.

01:08:05.036 --> 01:08:11.936 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; I've come to the National Archives to
see a document which takes us behind the

01:08:09.136 --> 01:08:14.736 align:start position:0%
scenes during this unique moment in
British history.

01:08:17.136 --> 01:08:23.236 align:start position:0%
The first and only time a king is put on
trial.

01:08:24.936 --> 01:08:31.836 align:start position:0%
I'm just captivated by this document.
Here in pen and ink are the minutes of

01:08:30.436 --> 01:08:35.236 align:start position:0%
the commissioners' meetings in the
Painted Chamber.

01:08:33.736 --> 01:08:38.536 align:start position:0%
It opens with a copy of the Act of
Parliament that's just been passed to

01:08:37.036 --> 01:08:44.836 align:start position:0%
try the king
in which Charles is accused of having

01:08:41.336 --> 01:08:49.636 align:start position:0%
a wicked design totally to subvert
the ancient and fundamental laws and

01:08:47.136 --> 01:08:56.336 align:start position:0%
liberties of this nation
and in their place to introduce an

01:08:52.136 --> 01:09:00.336 align:start position:0%
arbitrary and tyrannical government.
The Act is followed by a list of court

01:08:58.236 --> 01:09:04.536 align:start position:0%
commissioners who attend the meetings in
the Painted Chamber. Here's the list of

01:09:02.536 --> 01:09:12.036 align:start position:0%
commissioners on the 10th of January and
that's Cromwell's signature first.

01:09:07.836 --> 01:09:17.736 align:start position:0%
On the first day just 54 of 135
appointed commissioners appear.

01:09:14.936 --> 01:09:21.936 align:start position:0%
On the second day just 46 members show
up.

01:09:18.636 --> 01:09:26.137 align:start position:0%
That's 89 missing commissioners.
Support for the trial seems shaky to say

01:09:24.536 --> 01:09:30.538 align:start position:0%
the least.
&gt;&gt; When Parliament appointed commissioners,

01:09:28.237 --> 01:09:37.838 align:start position:0%
it did not ask them first if they wanted
to be commissioners, it named them.

01:09:33.538 --> 01:09:44.737 align:start position:0%
It named 150 originally, then 135, but
made a quorum of 20. That meant they

01:09:40.737 --> 01:09:48.538 align:start position:0%
knew that most of these people would not
take part. So it came That's why they

01:09:46.838 --> 01:09:52.237 align:start position:0%
had so many
um because they knew they had to get

01:09:50.737 --> 01:09:56.738 align:start position:0%
some.
&gt;&gt; Cromwell must convince some of the

01:09:53.737 --> 01:10:01.438 align:start position:0%
country's most high-ranking legal minds
that the trial is lawful.

01:09:59.738 --> 01:10:06.238 align:start position:0%
The doubters insist that neither
Parliament nor the army have the

01:10:03.637 --> 01:10:10.338 align:start position:0%
authority to try the king.
Only God can judge a king.

01:10:08.137 --> 01:10:14.738 align:start position:0%
Anyone else who does so is committing
treason.

01:10:12.238 --> 01:10:19.238 align:start position:0%
The commissioners have a monumental task
ahead of them.

01:10:16.438 --> 01:10:23.738 align:start position:0%
They must chart completely unnavigated
legal waters in order to pull off

01:10:21.538 --> 01:10:27.538 align:start position:0%
something that's never been done before,
the trial of a monarch who's usually

01:10:25.738 --> 01:10:31.738 align:start position:0%
above the law.
They must prepare their case, wrestling

01:10:29.637 --> 01:10:34.637 align:start position:0%
with the legal details in order to
present the trial as legitimate and

01:10:33.637 --> 01:10:39.637 align:start position:0%
legal.
If they fail, it's game over.

01:10:37.738 --> 01:10:43.338 align:start position:0%
And with just 10 days to go, the
commissioners are working round the

01:10:40.938 --> 01:10:43.338 align:start position:0%
clock.

01:10:45.637 --> 01:10:52.738 align:start position:0%
This is new ground for everyone, and
there's uncertainty even at the center

01:10:50.137 --> 01:10:55.137 align:start position:0%
of the action.
&gt;&gt; I think there are a number of people who

01:10:54.038 --> 01:10:58.338 align:start position:0%
are quite uncomfortable with the way
things are going. Uh and I think that's

01:10:57.137 --> 01:11:03.438 align:start position:0%
partly because there's a sort of deep
assumption of monarchy being at the

01:11:01.238 --> 01:11:08.137 align:start position:0%
center of the English constitution, even
amongst the the kind of die-hard

01:11:05.137 --> 01:11:12.338 align:start position:0%
parliamentarians. The other thing that
is definitely causing consternation is

01:11:10.338 --> 01:11:17.338 align:start position:0%
that it's very clear that this is being
driven by the army.

01:11:14.938 --> 01:11:23.038 align:start position:0%
And that involvement in the civilian
government uh is something which makes

01:11:19.738 --> 01:11:23.038 align:start position:0%
people very very uncomfortable.

01:11:23.938 --> 01:11:30.739 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; These misgivings are shared by
Cromwell's colleague Sir Thomas Fairfax.

01:11:31.839 --> 01:11:35.939 align:start position:0%
He's the man at the head of the army.

01:11:41.739 --> 01:11:49.439 align:start position:0%
Sir Thomas Fairfax is a war hero, famed
throughout the land as one of the most

01:11:46.439 --> 01:11:54.439 align:start position:0%
outstanding commanders of the Civil War.
As Lord General of the New Model Army,

01:11:51.739 --> 01:11:59.739 align:start position:0%
he's led Parliament to many victories.
He's been instrumental in defeating the

01:11:56.439 --> 01:12:04.539 align:start position:0%
Royalist forces and capturing the king.
Now, the final battle of the Civil War

01:12:02.139 --> 01:12:08.939 align:start position:0%
is being fought out not in the field,
but in the chambers and corridors of

01:12:06.439 --> 01:12:08.939 align:start position:0%
Whitehall.

01:12:10.639 --> 01:12:18.439 align:start position:0%
Fairfax has overseen Cromwell's rise
within the New Model Army and counts him

01:12:15.639 --> 01:12:22.239 align:start position:0%
among his most trusted friends.
&gt;&gt; And so what was Cromwell's personal

01:12:20.339 --> 01:12:27.039 align:start position:0%
relationship with Fairfax like?
&gt;&gt; To begin with, extremely good. And there

01:12:25.139 --> 01:12:32.239 align:start position:0%
are lots of personal letters between
them. And Cromwell always does the

01:12:29.339 --> 01:12:36.939 align:start position:0%
courtesy of handwriting them. He by by
the middle of the 1640s, he's using

01:12:34.539 --> 01:12:40.339 align:start position:0%
secretaries except when he writes to his
family, immediate family, and when he

01:12:38.539 --> 01:12:42.539 align:start position:0%
writes to Fairfax, which I think is a
sign of respect.

01:12:41.439 --> 01:12:46.839 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Or intimacy even.
&gt;&gt; Even of intimacy.

01:12:44.539 --> 01:12:51.039 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; But as Cromwell's power increases,
Fairfax becomes unnerved.

01:12:49.539 --> 01:12:55.539 align:start position:0%
We know he attends the first
commissioners' meeting,

01:12:52.739 --> 01:13:00.039 align:start position:0%
but by January the 11th, he's notable by
his absence.

01:12:58.439 --> 01:13:05.039 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; When it comes to the decision to put the
king on trial, Fairfax is irresolute.

01:13:03.639 --> 01:13:11.639 align:start position:0%
It's not that he's opposed to it. He
simply can't bring himself to really go

01:13:07.539 --> 01:13:15.339 align:start position:0%
go through with it. And he he withdraws
from the trial. But of course, lots of

01:13:13.539 --> 01:13:20.239 align:start position:0%
people that have been Cromwell's close
friends now lies through the 1640s are

01:13:17.539 --> 01:13:25.639 align:start position:0%
getting cold feet at that point.
&gt;&gt; If Fairfax himself seems to lack

01:13:22.339 --> 01:13:28.539 align:start position:0%
resolution, his wife, Anne, is a force
to be reckoned with.

01:13:30.339 --> 01:13:39.140 align:start position:0%
She had followed her husband battle
against the Royalists and had even been

01:13:34.640 --> 01:13:39.140 align:start position:0%
taken prisoner. She's fearless.

01:13:40.740 --> 01:13:48.340 align:start position:0%
At heart, they're both Presbyterians,
moderate Puritans. They do desire

01:13:45.740 --> 01:13:51.440 align:start position:0%
spiritual reform, but not at the cost of
the king's life.

01:13:52.740 --> 01:13:58.840 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Fairfax's absence is a huge blow for
Cromwell. I mean, personally as well as

01:13:57.140 --> 01:14:02.240 align:start position:0%
politically, they'd made a really good
team.

01:13:59.840 --> 01:14:07.740 align:start position:0%
Um and it's obviously terrible for army
morale if the head of the army is not

01:14:04.540 --> 01:14:07.740 align:start position:0%
seen to be supporting them.

01:14:08.940 --> 01:14:15.640 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; I don't think Fairfax has quite the
providential mindset that Cromwell has,

01:14:13.040 --> 01:14:21.640 align:start position:0%
and he's not prepared to go through on
the deal or no-deal scenario that is

01:14:18.140 --> 01:14:26.740 align:start position:0%
ultimately the uh trial of Charles I,
and he has almost nothing to do with it,

01:14:24.240 --> 01:14:31.640 align:start position:0%
and he washes his hands of it.
&gt;&gt; Moderate Puritan MPs expelled from

01:14:28.940 --> 01:14:35.740 align:start position:0%
Parliament implore Fairfax to take
action to stop the trial.

01:14:35.940 --> 01:14:41.640 align:start position:0%
This is a copy of a letter written to
Fairfax by one of the excluded MPs, Ned

01:14:40.540 --> 01:14:47.140 align:start position:0%
Harley.
In it, he condemns the army's actions

01:14:44.240 --> 01:14:52.540 align:start position:0%
and begs Fairfax to intervene.
Their evils for want of your prohibition

01:14:49.640 --> 01:14:57.040 align:start position:0%
will become your guilt, he writes. He
goes on, a general's words cannot be

01:14:54.940 --> 01:15:01.640 align:start position:0%
other than commands.
Fairfax is their general after all, so

01:14:59.540 --> 01:15:06.240 align:start position:0%
why does he not command the army to stop
the trial?

01:15:03.440 --> 01:15:11.040 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Fairfax has serious clout in it. If he
wanted to, he probably could have

01:15:08.440 --> 01:15:15.640 align:start position:0%
stopped this, possibly anyway, but it
would have divided the army. And you

01:15:12.840 --> 01:15:19.340 align:start position:0%
know, he loves his men. He commands real
loyalty with them. They have fought

01:15:17.840 --> 01:15:23.840 align:start position:0%
under him, and he doesn't want to
undermine them publicly. So

01:15:21.640 --> 01:15:27.540 align:start position:0%
yes, Fairfax probably could have done
more. You know, he comes across as as

01:15:25.440 --> 01:15:31.340 align:start position:0%
quite spineless, a bit of a Pontius
Pilate, but I have quite a lot of

01:15:30.040 --> 01:15:35.142 align:start position:0%
sympathy for him. I think there's
there's real anguish there.

01:15:37.942 --> 01:15:44.142 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Despite the absence of Fairfax, trial
preparations continue.

01:15:44.342 --> 01:15:52.642 align:start position:0%
But when another commissioner voices
deep reservations about the legality of

01:15:48.542 --> 01:15:52.642 align:start position:0%
a trial, Cromwell snaps.

01:15:52.742 --> 01:15:59.842 align:start position:0%
This letter from Algernon Sydney recalls
a furious exchange with Cromwell.

01:15:57.642 --> 01:16:04.342 align:start position:0%
Although a staunch Republican, Sydney is
vehemently opposed to the trial. He

01:16:02.342 --> 01:16:10.042 align:start position:0%
tells Cromwell that the trial is not
legal. Firstly, because the king could

01:16:06.942 --> 01:16:16.742 align:start position:0%
be tried by no court. And secondly, that
no man could be tried by that court.

01:16:13.142 --> 01:16:23.942 align:start position:0%
Cromwell is outraged. He responds with
much heat. But I tell you, we shall cut

01:16:19.842 --> 01:16:28.842 align:start position:0%
off his head with the crown upon it.
In hindsight, it's tempting to see great

01:16:26.242 --> 01:16:34.742 align:start position:0%
portent in Cromwell's outburst.
After all, we know how this ends. But at

01:16:32.442 --> 01:16:38.342 align:start position:0%
the time, nobody knew if this was a
revelation of policy or just a flash of

01:16:37.142 --> 01:16:41.442 align:start position:0%
temper.
Perhaps not even Cromwell.

01:16:41.542 --> 01:16:48.342 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Cromwell is a born negotiator
uh both in his military and his

01:16:46.842 --> 01:16:54.342 align:start position:0%
political career.
And the trial of the king is a further

01:16:51.542 --> 01:16:58.742 align:start position:0%
negotiation as to what the king becomes.
It's being called bargaining with

01:16:56.042 --> 01:16:58.742 align:start position:0%
menaces.

01:17:00.442 --> 01:17:10.142 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; I think that Cromwell
would rather that Charles submitted to

01:17:07.942 --> 01:17:13.342 align:start position:0%
the court. It would have made life a
great deal easier.

01:17:11.742 --> 01:17:18.342 align:start position:0%
That was definitely the preferred
option.

01:17:14.742 --> 01:17:24.442 align:start position:0%
But Cromwell was prepared to cut off
Charles's head if he did not submit to

01:17:21.842 --> 01:17:24.442 align:start position:0%
the court.

01:17:27.242 --> 01:17:32.542 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; With nine days until the trial,
Parliament is divided.

01:17:32.842 --> 01:17:36.442 align:start position:0%
The king's fate hangs in the

01:17:37.743 --> 01:17:44.343 align:start position:0%
The remaining commissioners are still
battling to pull together their case

01:17:41.443 --> 01:17:44.343 align:start position:0%
against the monarch.

01:17:44.443 --> 01:17:51.243 align:start position:0%
And if anyone is seriously thinking of
freeing Charles, they really should get

01:17:48.643 --> 01:17:51.243 align:start position:0%
a move on.

01:17:51.643 --> 01:17:59.343 align:start position:0%
With the trial of the king, the
principal topic of conversation, his

01:17:55.643 --> 01:18:03.143 align:start position:0%
subjects are hungry for news.
But news can be dangerous.

01:18:06.943 --> 01:18:13.643 align:start position:0%
Since the war began, there's been an
explosion in press activity, a vast

01:18:11.843 --> 01:18:18.043 align:start position:0%
increase in the number of publications
flying off the London presses for

01:18:15.743 --> 01:18:22.743 align:start position:0%
distribution around the country.
And thanks to this continuous flow of

01:18:20.143 --> 01:18:28.143 align:start position:0%
news books and pamphlets, ordinary
people now have access to information,

01:18:25.243 --> 01:18:31.043 align:start position:0%
misinformation, and propaganda from both
sides.

01:18:29.443 --> 01:18:37.143 align:start position:0%
In the battle between royalists and
parliamentarians, the printing press has

01:18:33.743 --> 01:18:37.143 align:start position:0%
become a political weapon.

01:18:38.443 --> 01:18:46.943 align:start position:0%
In the 1640s and 50s, London bookseller
George Thomason bought weekly pamphlets,

01:18:44.443 --> 01:18:50.843 align:start position:0%
broadsides, and news books, and bound
them into 2,000 volumes.

01:18:49.443 --> 01:18:56.343 align:start position:0%
Seeing them together at the British
Library, you get a sense of this media

01:18:53.443 --> 01:18:56.343 align:start position:0%
explosion.

01:18:56.643 --> 01:19:01.443 align:start position:0%
Do you have a sense that as as we move
towards the period of Charles's trial

01:19:00.043 --> 01:19:05.543 align:start position:0%
that there's a propaganda war being
fought in the press?

01:19:02.843 --> 01:19:08.443 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Absolutely. The propaganda war continues
and we can see it in some of the volumes

01:19:07.443 --> 01:19:10.843 align:start position:0%
in front of us.
&gt;&gt; There would have been people all over

01:19:09.443 --> 01:19:13.943 align:start position:0%
the country who who were reading this
material.

01:19:12.443 --> 01:19:18.143 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Yes, though literacy is much higher in
London than elsewhere. Within London,

01:19:15.643 --> 01:19:23.143 align:start position:0%
there's a large community of men who can
read, perhaps as many as 60 or 70% of

01:19:20.743 --> 01:19:27.843 align:start position:0%
the population of some degree.
&gt;&gt; Let's start with this one, the moderate.

01:19:25.243 --> 01:19:30.543 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The moderate is is aligned with the
Levellers.

01:19:28.643 --> 01:19:35.543 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Procrastination in peril is the mother
of ensuing misery,

01:19:32.643 --> 01:19:38.343 align:start position:0%
and delays oftentimes bring to pass that
he which should have died kills him

01:19:37.343 --> 01:19:40.545 align:start position:0%
which should have lived.
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

01:19:38.545 --> 01:19:42.344 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; he's been very circumlocutious, I think,
this fight.

01:19:40.945 --> 01:19:45.445 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; headline, is it? It's it's quite
abstruse.

01:19:43.244 --> 01:19:47.945 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Then it reproduces a letter from Windsor
Castle that's quite unsympathetic to the

01:19:47.344 --> 01:19:53.244 align:start position:0%
king.
&gt;&gt; We find his discourse of late very

01:19:50.144 --> 01:19:57.744 align:start position:0%
effeminate and talking much of women,
which is sure for the most part to bring

01:19:55.244 --> 01:20:03.445 align:start position:0%
in at the end of every subject.
&gt;&gt; And I think this is part of an attempt

01:19:59.644 --> 01:20:08.244 align:start position:0%
to kind of suggest uh both his
frivolousness,

01:20:04.744 --> 01:20:10.744 align:start position:0%
but also perhaps his uh susceptibility
to influence by his wife, the Catholic

01:20:10.144 --> 01:20:14.945 align:start position:0%
queen.
&gt;&gt; Moving on to this one. So this has a

01:20:12.644 --> 01:20:19.945 align:start position:0%
much more grand title. So here we have
Mercurius Pragmaticus, communicating

01:20:17.945 --> 01:20:23.945 align:start position:0%
intelligence from all parts, touching
all affairs, designs, humours, and

01:20:21.744 --> 01:20:26.844 align:start position:0%
conditions throughout the kingdom.
That's that's a fairly broad brief,

01:20:26.144 --> 01:20:29.644 align:start position:0%
isn't it?
&gt;&gt; And as you can see, it kind of begins

01:20:28.744 --> 01:20:33.045 align:start position:0%
with a poem.
&gt;&gt; So that's again, that's another

01:20:30.545 --> 01:20:38.744 align:start position:0%
contrast. Here we have um a poem and
here we have yes, a a very prosaic and

01:20:36.144 --> 01:20:43.144 align:start position:0%
prolix introduction. So the royalists
had poems.

01:20:40.045 --> 01:20:47.445 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Yes, but not because the poems represent
kind of elite culture or fine art.

01:20:45.545 --> 01:20:51.244 align:start position:0%
They're they're they're they're dirty.
They're they're nasty.

01:20:48.545 --> 01:20:56.644 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; So what were these poems like?
&gt;&gt; Shall I read an example? Falsehood,

01:20:53.644 --> 01:21:03.445 align:start position:0%
having won the day, now may boldly wear
the light, when poor truth must sneak

01:20:59.844 --> 01:21:07.144 align:start position:0%
away, slyly skulking in the night.
Yet

01:21:04.545 --> 01:21:10.744 align:start position:0%
to have eyes by night, you'll find is
better

01:21:08.144 --> 01:21:14.344 align:start position:0%
than by day be blind.
&gt;&gt; That's it doesn't really pull any

01:21:11.945 --> 01:21:18.844 align:start position:0%
punches, does it? I mean, it's it's it's
accusatory. It's graphic.

01:21:16.844 --> 01:21:23.945 align:start position:0%
Um the idea that that you know, truth
has been the victim um in in this

01:21:21.344 --> 01:21:29.144 align:start position:0%
process is is very clearly stated.
&gt;&gt; Yes, it's it's effective, not least

01:21:27.045 --> 01:21:34.244 align:start position:0%
because it sort of posits the the
parliamentarian position as as as as one

01:21:31.744 --> 01:21:34.244 align:start position:0%
of blindness.

01:21:38.844 --> 01:21:46.146 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; From political scaremongering to
predictions of the end of the world,

01:21:42.946 --> 01:21:50.646 align:start position:0%
both sides create a climate of fear.
Against a backdrop of conflicting

01:21:47.946 --> 01:21:56.345 align:start position:0%
opinions and deep divisions, the public
speculates on the king's fate.

01:21:53.845 --> 01:22:00.245 align:start position:0%
It's a war of words.
Who's winning the hearts and minds of

01:21:58.345 --> 01:22:04.646 align:start position:0%
the people?
&gt;&gt; In London, what will happen is that a

01:22:02.845 --> 01:22:08.646 align:start position:0%
newspaper, a piece of propaganda will be
printed. Uh someone will take it to the

01:22:06.946 --> 01:22:12.146 align:start position:0%
alehouse and they will read it and share
it between loads and loads of people. Um

01:22:10.646 --> 01:22:15.345 align:start position:0%
and it won't just be men reading it.
They will discuss it with their wives

01:22:13.646 --> 01:22:17.946 align:start position:0%
and and their wives will have an opinion
and and their sons will have an opinion

01:22:17.046 --> 01:22:20.446 align:start position:0%
and their daughters will have an
opinion.

01:22:21.046 --> 01:22:28.345 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The king himself is entirely ignorant of
these passionate debates.

01:22:26.146 --> 01:22:32.446 align:start position:0%
Since he was informed of his trial, he's
heard no more.

01:22:29.745 --> 01:22:37.146 align:start position:0%
No newspapers, no letters.
He doesn't know if he has the public

01:22:34.446 --> 01:22:37.146 align:start position:0%
support.

01:22:42.345 --> 01:22:47.946 align:start position:0%
There are eight days until the start of
the trial.

01:22:45.646 --> 01:22:53.345 align:start position:0%
Neither Charles nor Cromwell really
knows how the people of England will

01:22:49.946 --> 01:22:57.745 align:start position:0%
react to the coming spectacle.
Today is the third meeting of the High

01:22:55.245 --> 01:23:05.345 align:start position:0%
Commission appointed to try the king.
Just 34 of 135 named members turn up.

01:23:03.245 --> 01:23:09.046 align:start position:0%
A new member summoned today, a lawyer
named John Bradshaw.

01:23:07.146 --> 01:23:13.845 align:start position:0%
He's given the thankless task of
overseeing the commission.

01:23:11.446 --> 01:23:17.446 align:start position:0%
Whether he likes it or not, Bradshaw is
appointed Lord President.

01:23:18.345 --> 01:23:25.345 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; If we think about someone like Lord
President John Bradshaw,

01:23:22.546 --> 01:23:32.046 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Bradshaw, a completely insignificant and
unknown lawyer, he wasn't perhaps the

01:23:28.446 --> 01:23:36.646 align:start position:0%
brightest of the lawyers of the time,
but he was a good common lawyer.

01:23:35.245 --> 01:23:40.646 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; I mean, we think about imposter
syndrome, for example. Imagine that you

01:23:39.146 --> 01:23:45.347 align:start position:0%
are there
um a

01:23:42.446 --> 01:23:51.047 align:start position:0%
magistrate from Cheshire, who is very
very reluctant to put be put in this

01:23:47.747 --> 01:23:57.247 align:start position:0%
position of Lord President
and trying a king, being eyeball to

01:23:53.547 --> 01:24:02.847 align:start position:0%
eyeball with the anointed divine
representative of God's will on earth.

01:24:00.447 --> 01:24:07.447 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The royalist press have a field day
finding a new target in this aggrandized

01:24:05.647 --> 01:24:12.347 align:start position:0%
Lord President.
The contemporary writer Thomas Fuller

01:24:09.147 --> 01:24:15.947 align:start position:0%
dismisses Bradshaw as a man of execrable
memory of whom nothing good is

01:24:14.547 --> 01:24:21.347 align:start position:0%
remembered.
&gt;&gt; I think initially he said he didn't want

01:24:19.847 --> 01:24:27.547 align:start position:0%
to do it
because of his family. His wife in

01:24:23.747 --> 01:24:31.947 align:start position:0%
particular was worried about him.
&gt;&gt; Bradshaw makes an earnest apology for

01:24:29.747 --> 01:24:36.847 align:start position:0%
himself that he be excused the position
of Lord President.

01:24:33.547 --> 01:24:41.447 align:start position:0%
To his dismay, his request is refused.
Bradshaw presses his point again,

01:24:38.447 --> 01:24:45.947 align:start position:0%
emphasizing his reluctance. Again, he's
overruled.

01:24:42.847 --> 01:24:51.147 align:start position:0%
There's no wriggling out of it.
&gt;&gt; He was rightly frightened of the

01:24:47.947 --> 01:24:57.247 align:start position:0%
Cavaliers who was still had
their pistols and their rapiers and were

01:24:54.147 --> 01:25:04.547 align:start position:0%
in London and he was nervous, rightly
so.

01:24:59.147 --> 01:25:04.547 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It's Saturday the 13th of January 1649.

01:25:08.747 --> 01:25:16.147 align:start position:0%
There's just one week to go before the
trial. The commissioners fear their

01:25:12.947 --> 01:25:16.147 align:start position:0%
lives are at risk.

01:25:16.547 --> 01:25:23.847 align:start position:0%
Royalist factions could mount a
terrorist attack and Parliament is on

01:25:20.747 --> 01:25:23.847 align:start position:0%
high alert.

01:25:25.047 --> 01:25:31.947 align:start position:0%
The vaults beneath the chambers where
the judges set searched for explosives

01:25:29.047 --> 01:25:31.947 align:start position:0%
and sealed.

01:25:34.547 --> 01:25:41.747 align:start position:0%
With the foil plot of Guy Fawkes a
recent memory, this threat is very real.

01:25:44.647 --> 01:25:52.749 align:start position:0%
London is in lockdown. It's a huge
security operation. This is the trial of

01:25:49.749 --> 01:25:57.749 align:start position:0%
a king. It's never happened before.
And the people of London are in a state

01:25:55.049 --> 01:26:02.749 align:start position:0%
of great uncertainty. They're fearful.
They're edgy. What is going on? Is the

01:26:00.349 --> 01:26:05.849 align:start position:0%
king going to be assassinated? Is he
going to escape?

01:26:04.549 --> 01:26:09.149 align:start position:0%
Is there going to be civil war? Is there
going to be invasion? I mean, what is

01:26:07.349 --> 01:26:13.849 align:start position:0%
going on? The times are entirely
unprecedented and really anything could

01:26:11.349 --> 01:26:13.849 align:start position:0%
happen.

01:26:20.049 --> 01:26:27.649 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; As the trial draws nearer, there are
increasing concerns about how the public

01:26:24.749 --> 01:26:27.649 align:start position:0%
might react.

01:26:29.049 --> 01:26:34.349 align:start position:0%
Parliament fears supporters of the king
might rise.

01:26:34.749 --> 01:26:41.149 align:start position:0%
The government shuts down London's
printing presses in an attempt to

01:26:38.549 --> 01:26:44.849 align:start position:0%
silence pro-royalist voices for fear
they might stir up the people.

01:26:48.448 --> 01:26:54.849 align:start position:0%
There's one piece of royalist propaganda
that's particularly dangerous for

01:26:52.149 --> 01:26:54.849 align:start position:0%
parliament.

01:26:54.948 --> 01:27:03.349 align:start position:0%
Publication is imminent when
parliamentary forces raid a London

01:26:59.249 --> 01:27:03.349 align:start position:0%
printing house and destroy it.

01:27:16.049 --> 01:27:23.249 align:start position:0%
I've come to Trinity College in
Cambridge to get a sense of what the

01:27:20.049 --> 01:27:23.249 align:start position:0%
fuss was all about.

01:27:26.549 --> 01:27:35.149 align:start position:0%
This is the Wren Library, home for the
last 300 years to some of the most

01:27:31.649 --> 01:27:35.149 align:start position:0%
precious books in England.

01:27:38.749 --> 01:27:45.349 align:start position:0%
But what interests me is one particular
volume.

01:27:43.448 --> 01:27:50.150 align:start position:0%
This is the little book that parliament
are so scared of.

01:27:47.649 --> 01:27:55.450 align:start position:0%
It's the Icon Basilike, or royal
portrait,

01:27:52.550 --> 01:27:59.150 align:start position:0%
of his sacred majesty in his solitudes
and sufferings.

01:27:57.450 --> 01:28:04.450 align:start position:0%
This claims to be the spiritual
autobiography of Charles I, an account

01:28:02.150 --> 01:28:08.850 align:start position:0%
of his personal feelings, his religious
beliefs, and his justification for the

01:28:06.850 --> 01:28:13.350 align:start position:0%
war.
Though Charles was not the author, it is

01:28:11.350 --> 01:28:18.450 align:start position:0%
accepted that the text was compiled
using his own writings.

01:28:16.350 --> 01:28:23.950 align:start position:0%
It was made with the king's blessing,
and it had been his intention to have it

01:28:20.250 --> 01:28:23.950 align:start position:0%
published before the trial.

01:28:24.950 --> 01:28:31.250 align:start position:0%
Touching it, the first thing that comes
to mind is how tiny it is.

01:28:29.050 --> 01:28:36.350 align:start position:0%
It can easily be slipped into a pocket
or hidden under a cloak. This book is

01:28:33.550 --> 01:28:40.150 align:start position:0%
contraband. It's dangerous. It's the
product of a police state.

01:28:38.550 --> 01:28:44.850 align:start position:0%
Reading something like this could be
very dangerous.

01:28:42.150 --> 01:28:47.750 align:start position:0%
Inside, it becomes even more
insurrectionary.

01:28:51.250 --> 01:28:59.150 align:start position:0%
Hidden in this tiny volume
is a frontispiece

01:28:56.550 --> 01:29:02.250 align:start position:0%
which shows King Charles I as a
Christian martyr.

01:29:04.750 --> 01:29:12.050 align:start position:0%
The frontispiece is divided into three
sections, horizontally and vertically,

01:29:09.450 --> 01:29:17.050 align:start position:0%
and each axis expresses the progress of
the king towards a potential martyrdom

01:29:14.950 --> 01:29:21.750 align:start position:0%
or even sainthood.
Here at the bottom, we see his earthly

01:29:19.150 --> 01:29:25.550 align:start position:0%
crown. Inside it is written the word
vanitas. So this is a vanity, a thing of

01:29:24.450 --> 01:29:28.850 align:start position:0%
the world, something which is
spiritually worthless.

01:29:29.450 --> 01:29:34.950 align:start position:0%
As we move up, we see that the king's
right hand is on a crown of thorns,

01:29:33.450 --> 01:29:37.750 align:start position:0%
obviously linking him immediately to
Christ.

01:29:36.150 --> 01:29:43.550 align:start position:0%
Inside the crown of thorns, the word
gratia, grace. On the left side of the

01:29:40.750 --> 01:29:49.550 align:start position:0%
picture, some palm trees with heavy
metal bells depending from them.

01:29:46.050 --> 01:29:55.552 align:start position:0%
Crescit sub pondere virtuous.
Um virtue increases with

01:29:52.751 --> 01:29:58.351 align:start position:0%
or virtue increases with weight. What
it's saying is that

01:29:57.052 --> 01:30:01.052 align:start position:0%
through suffering we're redeemed,
through suffering we become spiritually

01:29:59.951 --> 01:30:06.351 align:start position:0%
better.
So these two equally balanced images

01:30:04.052 --> 01:30:10.151 align:start position:0%
both suggest Christ.
In the middle of the picture sits

01:30:07.751 --> 01:30:13.151 align:start position:0%
Charles in his cloak
with

01:30:10.951 --> 01:30:16.751 align:start position:0%
his hand upon his collar in an attitude
of submission. He's looking up at the

01:30:14.751 --> 01:30:22.151 align:start position:0%
lighted window. At the top of the
picture is his eternal glorious crown.

01:30:23.052 --> 01:30:30.951 align:start position:0%
Grace and eternity.
Although the imagery is dense and

01:30:27.451 --> 01:30:34.151 align:start position:0%
complicated, it's also very readable.
And what it's setting up is the idea of

01:30:32.851 --> 01:30:37.351 align:start position:0%
Charles I
as a Protestant saint.

01:30:38.451 --> 01:30:44.651 align:start position:0%
The icon is really a masterpiece of
Royalist propaganda.

01:30:42.151 --> 01:30:49.351 align:start position:0%
Here are Charles's words.
I will rather choose to wear a crown of

01:30:46.651 --> 01:30:52.651 align:start position:0%
thorns with my Saviour than to exchange
that of gold which is due me for one of

01:30:51.651 --> 01:30:57.351 align:start position:0%
lead,
whose embased flexibleness shall be

01:30:54.451 --> 01:31:02.351 align:start position:0%
forced to bend and comply to the various
and off-contrary dictates of any

01:31:00.052 --> 01:31:06.052 align:start position:0%
fashions.
All together, the icon presents a

01:31:04.151 --> 01:31:11.052 align:start position:0%
portrait of a man whose religious
beliefs are unwavering and whose

01:31:08.052 --> 01:31:14.851 align:start position:0%
political motives are untainted.
This

01:31:11.951 --> 01:31:18.851 align:start position:0%
is the king's secret weapon.
Were it to have been published before

01:31:16.251 --> 01:31:21.351 align:start position:0%
the trial, things could end very
differently.

01:31:23.351 --> 01:31:28.451 align:start position:0%
There are just 48 hours until the trial.

01:31:30.052 --> 01:31:35.251 align:start position:0%
Every day brings Cromwell a fresh set of
problems.

01:31:38.151 --> 01:31:43.851 align:start position:0%
Government lawyers engaged by the
commissioners are still wrestling over

01:31:42.251 --> 01:31:46.351 align:start position:0%
the draft of the charges against the
king.

01:31:46.751 --> 01:31:52.651 align:start position:0%
To add to Cromwell's woes, the court's
attorney general, William Steele,

01:31:51.052 --> 01:31:58.952 align:start position:0%
appointed to conduct the case against
the king, sends word that is very sick

01:31:56.052 --> 01:32:03.252 align:start position:0%
and unable to continue his duties. As
the all-important chief prosecutor, his

01:32:01.552 --> 01:32:08.452 align:start position:0%
was the most important voice in this
case, but on this occasion he seems to

01:32:05.852 --> 01:32:08.452 align:start position:0%
have lost it.

01:32:10.952 --> 01:32:16.252 align:start position:0%
So all of a sudden the commissioners
need a new prosecutor.

01:32:16.552 --> 01:32:27.052 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; John Cook is a fascinating person. He's
a youngish. He's in his 40s, barrister,

01:32:24.052 --> 01:32:32.952 align:start position:0%
and he's very bright. He's a brilliant
and he's of low birth. His parents were

01:32:30.552 --> 01:32:38.752 align:start position:0%
sharecroppers in Leicestershire, and
he'd made his way because of his

01:32:35.452 --> 01:32:42.653 align:start position:0%
exceptional brightness.
&gt;&gt; And it's John Cook, a lawyer who's

01:32:40.653 --> 01:32:46.952 align:start position:0%
already part of the team, who is the
next in line.

01:32:45.052 --> 01:32:51.653 align:start position:0%
Cook has the task of convincing the
court and the country that a king is not

01:32:49.852 --> 01:32:55.452 align:start position:0%
above the law.
He must challenge the divine right and

01:32:53.752 --> 01:33:00.052 align:start position:0%
sovereign immunity.
He must prove the allegations against

01:32:57.452 --> 01:33:05.052 align:start position:0%
Charles with robust arguments and hard
evidence. He must build a case which

01:33:02.752 --> 01:33:09.552 align:start position:0%
brings down the monarch and perhaps the
monarchy with it.

01:33:06.952 --> 01:33:14.952 align:start position:0%
It's a pretty tough brief.
&gt;&gt; He was

01:33:10.952 --> 01:33:20.052 align:start position:0%
rather overwhelmed and inclined to turn
it down. He thought,

01:33:16.852 --> 01:33:25.752 align:start position:0%
I'm not good enough for this. I'm not
respected enough to take on this the

01:33:23.153 --> 01:33:31.752 align:start position:0%
greatest trial history.
And then his

01:33:28.052 --> 01:33:34.952 align:start position:0%
religious fervour overtook him. He said,
well, maybe

01:33:33.653 --> 01:33:40.752 align:start position:0%
God
is working through me

01:33:37.252 --> 01:33:46.153 align:start position:0%
to show that even the most humble
barrister can be the agent of bringing

01:33:43.752 --> 01:33:50.552 align:start position:0%
down a tyrant.
&gt;&gt; The charge sheet has been going back and

01:33:48.153 --> 01:33:56.554 align:start position:0%
forth for days as the government
searches for the perfect form of words.

01:33:53.952 --> 01:33:59.954 align:start position:0%
Cook and Cromwell have less than 48
hours to get it right.

01:34:00.054 --> 01:34:03.354 align:start position:0%
It's rejected,

01:34:06.554 --> 01:34:09.854 align:start position:0%
and rejected again.

01:34:11.554 --> 01:34:17.754 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; They're flailing around. They're not
coming up with the right language for

01:34:14.954 --> 01:34:26.054 align:start position:0%
the charge. They haven't solved the
problem of how you can prosecute a king.

01:34:23.154 --> 01:34:29.954 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It had been to and fro before the
commissioners. Uh

01:34:28.254 --> 01:34:33.654 align:start position:0%
it was too wordy.
What they wanted something much more

01:34:31.654 --> 01:34:38.554 align:start position:0%
short and snappy.
Um and so um

01:34:36.354 --> 01:34:45.454 align:start position:0%
Coke in consultation with some of the
commissioners including Cromwell himself

01:34:41.354 --> 01:34:49.954 align:start position:0%
uh finally drafted a a tauter, tighter
document.

01:34:47.354 --> 01:34:56.254 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Charles is to be charged in the name and
on behalf of the people.

01:34:52.654 --> 01:34:56.254 align:start position:0%
This is a key phrase.

01:34:56.554 --> 01:35:04.454 align:start position:0%
Cromwell and the commissioner's dilemma
has always been how to try a king if he

01:35:01.154 --> 01:35:04.454 align:start position:0%
is the embodiment of the law.

01:35:05.754 --> 01:35:11.654 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; If you take an understanding of English
law in the English legal system as it

01:35:09.154 --> 01:35:16.054 align:start position:0%
stood at the time, it is quite hard
to justify the trial of a king. But

01:35:13.954 --> 01:35:18.954 align:start position:0%
crucially, the safety of the people is
the supreme law and that trumps

01:35:17.654 --> 01:35:22.254 align:start position:0%
everything.
And there's a really interesting

01:35:20.054 --> 01:35:25.854 align:start position:0%
metaphor which gets used, which is
imagine you're on a ship.

01:35:24.254 --> 01:35:29.154 align:start position:0%
Um now obviously if you're on a ship in
the 17th century, then the the

01:35:27.354 --> 01:35:33.354 align:start position:0%
structures of of the government of that
ship are very very hierarchical, very

01:35:30.754 --> 01:35:36.354 align:start position:0%
very strict. You can't launch a mutiny
against the captain. But what if the

01:35:34.554 --> 01:35:40.254 align:start position:0%
captain is driving it into some rocks
and he's going to kill everyone?

01:35:37.954 --> 01:35:43.754 align:start position:0%
Then of course it becomes completely
understandable that you have to mutiny

01:35:42.254 --> 01:35:49.354 align:start position:0%
against that captain and and get rid of
them. So that's how this kind of thing

01:35:46.954 --> 01:35:54.354 align:start position:0%
could be justified. It's a a case of
Charles being a danger to the people. So

01:35:51.654 --> 01:35:59.354 align:start position:0%
the natural thing that you should do is
you should get rid of that danger.

01:35:55.554 --> 01:36:02.156 align:start position:0%
Otherwise lots more people will die.
&gt;&gt; Throughout this crisis,

01:36:00.455 --> 01:36:07.856 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; there's one government body that's been
a thorn in the side of the Commons,

01:36:05.156 --> 01:36:11.055 align:start position:0%
the House of Lords.
The Lords have been blocked from the

01:36:09.455 --> 01:36:15.856 align:start position:0%
proceedings for voting against the
trial, but they try another way. They

01:36:13.856 --> 01:36:21.156 align:start position:0%
try to thwart the High Court by
preventing the use of the chosen venue,

01:36:18.156 --> 01:36:26.455 align:start position:0%
Westminster Hall. The Commons swat the
Lords away, declaring that they are the

01:36:23.756 --> 01:36:30.455 align:start position:0%
supreme power in England.
&gt;&gt; Westminster Hall is chosen because it's

01:36:28.055 --> 01:36:35.455 align:start position:0%
the center of public justice in England.
It's where the law courts are held. It's

01:36:32.656 --> 01:36:39.156 align:start position:0%
where the most high-profile trials have
taken place.

01:36:36.455 --> 01:36:44.955 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Symbolically, doing it there is a way of
suggesting continuity

01:36:41.856 --> 01:36:49.756 align:start position:0%
with um the English law
as as as as has gone before.

01:36:47.256 --> 01:36:54.256 align:start position:0%
Uh and as much as it is a radical
departure,

01:36:51.756 --> 01:36:59.756 align:start position:0%
it is kind of unlawful.
I think this is a way of trying to kind

01:36:55.756 --> 01:36:59.756 align:start position:0%
of paper that over a little bit.

01:37:02.055 --> 01:37:06.856 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The 19th of January is the eve of the
trial.

01:37:08.256 --> 01:37:13.356 align:start position:0%
The royal prisoner is moved from Windsor
to London.

01:37:14.856 --> 01:37:20.756 align:start position:0%
He's heavily guarded for fear his allies
will mount an escape attempt.

01:37:24.555 --> 01:37:32.055 align:start position:0%
En route, a man on horseback, one Mr.
Proctor, watches the royal coach go by

01:37:29.756 --> 01:37:37.856 align:start position:0%
and doffs his hat respectfully.
The king acknowledges the greeting, but

01:37:34.555 --> 01:37:43.356 align:start position:0%
the guards are furious and set upon poor
Mr. Proctor, flinging him into the

01:37:39.856 --> 01:37:43.356 align:start position:0%
ditch, horse and all.

01:37:48.656 --> 01:37:55.656 align:start position:0%
The soldiers shed no tears for Charles's
predicament. They blame him for all the

01:37:53.455 --> 01:37:58.656 align:start position:0%
hardships that have been imposed on them
and their families.

01:38:02.356 --> 01:38:09.957 align:start position:0%
Imagine the king arriving back in the
capital, the seat of his throne, after

01:38:07.757 --> 01:38:15.757 align:start position:0%
an absence of seven years.
It's a far cry from the welcome he would

01:38:11.757 --> 01:38:19.156 align:start position:0%
have expected. No fanfare, no flags, no
ceremony.

01:38:17.556 --> 01:38:26.357 align:start position:0%
For much of his life, Charles has been
surrounded by adoring crowds. What does

01:38:22.257 --> 01:38:26.357 align:start position:0%
this silent homecoming tell him?

01:38:27.457 --> 01:38:32.357 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; So I think Charles senses the danger
that he's in from very very early on

01:38:30.556 --> 01:38:36.357 align:start position:0%
actually. I think he knows how this
ends. He's been worried about people

01:38:34.257 --> 01:38:41.056 align:start position:0%
trying to kill him for a long time.
Uh I think perhaps the realization is

01:38:38.357 --> 01:38:45.656 align:start position:0%
becoming greater, becoming more real uh
in late 1648.

01:38:43.056 --> 01:38:49.257 align:start position:0%
I don't think in a in early 1649 he he
thinks that he's going to get out of

01:38:46.857 --> 01:38:49.257 align:start position:0%
this.

01:38:55.857 --> 01:39:01.857 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Charles spends the night before the
trial here at St. James's Palace under

01:39:00.056 --> 01:39:06.056 align:start position:0%
armed guard.
This is London's official royal

01:39:03.357 --> 01:39:09.757 align:start position:0%
residence, Charles's home, where he
lived with Henrietta Maria, where his

01:39:08.257 --> 01:39:14.556 align:start position:0%
heir was born.
Tomorrow he will face his subjects and

01:39:12.156 --> 01:39:17.556 align:start position:0%
his enemies to answer for his crimes
against his country.

01:39:26.056 --> 01:39:29.656 align:start position:0%
It's the 20th of January.

01:39:29.757 --> 01:39:33.757 align:start position:0%
The day of the trial has come.

01:39:33.857 --> 01:39:37.556 align:start position:0%
Charles's barber arrives.

01:39:37.757 --> 01:39:42.257 align:start position:0%
But this is a barber provided by
Parliament.

01:39:44.257 --> 01:39:49.656 align:start position:0%
Charles is nervous that this could be an
assassination attempt.

01:39:50.457 --> 01:39:56.556 align:start position:0%
As a means of getting rid of a
troublesome monarch, it has an obvious

01:39:54.156 --> 01:39:56.556 align:start position:0%
appeal.

01:39:57.457 --> 01:40:03.556 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Assassination would have been so much
easier. And And it's happened before

01:40:00.857 --> 01:40:07.258 align:start position:0%
with Kings, Edward II, Richard II, Henry
VI.

01:40:05.658 --> 01:40:11.158 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; If you simply assassinate Charles,
everyone will know that you've done it,

01:40:09.258 --> 01:40:14.558 align:start position:0%
you know. You can't just, you know,
pretend that something something

01:40:12.858 --> 01:40:18.358 align:start position:0%
terrible has accidentally happened.
&gt;&gt; That's the point. They don't want a

01:40:16.258 --> 01:40:22.358 align:start position:0%
grubby murderer in a castle. They want
this to be out in the open. They want

01:40:20.158 --> 01:40:26.858 align:start position:0%
this to be as valid as possible. And
it's something the army and the Cromwell

01:40:25.158 --> 01:40:30.558 align:start position:0%
are proud of. You know, they think this
is a glorious thing that they can bring

01:40:28.558 --> 01:40:35.058 align:start position:0%
a tyrant to justice.
&gt;&gt; They also want to justify it before God

01:40:32.558 --> 01:40:39.858 align:start position:0%
as well. And I think they know or their
view is that that God is you know, God

01:40:37.658 --> 01:40:45.258 align:start position:0%
may well have decided against monarchy,
but God is definitely not in favor of

01:40:42.258 --> 01:40:48.358 align:start position:0%
extrajudicial assassination.
&gt;&gt; Cook and the commissioners are working

01:40:46.758 --> 01:40:54.058 align:start position:0%
up to the wire.
With just hours to go, they're still

01:40:50.458 --> 01:40:57.258 align:start position:0%
tweaking the charge against the king.
Cromwell is nervous.

01:41:02.058 --> 01:41:05.858 align:start position:0%
London is on high alert.

01:41:06.158 --> 01:41:13.458 align:start position:0%
The Tower of London supplies arms and
armor for the thousand man guard charged

01:41:11.158 --> 01:41:18.458 align:start position:0%
with preventing rebellion or a royal
rescue attempt.

01:41:15.758 --> 01:41:22.658 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; This is a display of military might, a
clear message to the king and the people

01:41:20.458 --> 01:41:26.258 align:start position:0%
that the army is in charge. The
commissioners might be running the

01:41:23.958 --> 01:41:29.058 align:start position:0%
trial, but it's military muscle which is
enforcing it.

01:41:38.358 --> 01:41:43.258 align:start position:0%
This trial isn't taking place in the
shadows.

01:41:41.258 --> 01:41:49.158 align:start position:0%
Cromwell is adamant that the king be
exposed as a man of blood in the eyes of

01:41:46.558 --> 01:41:49.158 align:start position:0%
the people.

01:41:54.058 --> 01:42:00.158 align:start position:0%
There can be no venue more suited to
this than Westminster Hall.

01:41:58.158 --> 01:42:04.258 align:start position:0%
Built in 1097,
it's one of the oldest buildings in

01:42:01.758 --> 01:42:04.258 align:start position:0%
London.

01:42:07.058 --> 01:42:13.859 align:start position:0%
Having fought off the lords' attempt to
block its use, the hall is given over to

01:42:11.559 --> 01:42:19.459 align:start position:0%
what for once it's a gross
understatement to call the trial of the

01:42:16.859 --> 01:42:19.459 align:start position:0%
century.

01:42:22.659 --> 01:42:31.959 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Imagine this place. It's loud, busy and
volatile with 5,000 people tensely

01:42:29.159 --> 01:42:36.459 align:start position:0%
packed into huge viewing galleries.
The court opens the procession as 20

01:42:34.559 --> 01:42:39.959 align:start position:0%
armed guards march into the hall
followed by messengers, ushers and the

01:42:38.559 --> 01:42:44.559 align:start position:0%
court crier.
One official carries the sword of state.

01:42:42.559 --> 01:42:48.259 align:start position:0%
It's part of the King's regalia and
symbolizes the power of the monarch to

01:42:46.459 --> 01:42:52.559 align:start position:0%
use the might of the state against his
enemies.

01:42:49.759 --> 01:42:57.559 align:start position:0%
The irony is that it's Parliament using
the might of the state against Charles.

01:42:55.259 --> 01:43:00.459 align:start position:0%
Even the props of kingship have been
turned against him.

01:43:00.559 --> 01:43:06.259 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Lord President John Bradshaw occupies an
elevated position.

01:43:06.359 --> 01:43:09.859 align:start position:0%
Behind him sit the commissioners.

01:43:11.559 --> 01:43:16.659 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The eyes of England are on the drama as
it unfolds.

01:43:18.259 --> 01:43:26.559 align:start position:0%
While the court assembles, the King is
on his way to Westminster Hall.

01:43:23.159 --> 01:43:26.559 align:start position:0%
He's heavily guarded.

01:43:31.159 --> 01:43:41.559 align:start position:0%
There's great tension in the hall.
Nobody knows quite what to expect. This

01:43:36.959 --> 01:43:41.559 align:start position:0%
could easily go the King's way.

01:43:43.159 --> 01:43:50.459 align:start position:0%
The court crier silences the bustling
courtroom and asks each commissioner to

01:43:47.559 --> 01:43:53.959 align:start position:0%
stand and answer his name.
The first on the register is Sir Thomas

01:43:52.459 --> 01:43:59.659 align:start position:0%
Fairfax.
His name is called.

01:43:56.759 --> 01:43:59.659 align:start position:0%
There's silence.

01:44:00.459 --> 01:44:07.359 align:start position:0%
A masked woman shouts out from the
crowd. He hath more wit than to be here.

01:44:09.159 --> 01:44:14.961 align:start position:0%
The The
is Lady Fairfax. She does something that

01:44:12.561 --> 01:44:19.361 align:start position:0%
her husband doesn't dare to do. She
speaks out to oppose the army, the

01:44:17.161 --> 01:44:23.061 align:start position:0%
court, and Cromwell.
She's an indomitable woman.

01:44:21.561 --> 01:44:27.761 align:start position:0%
It's a pity that Charles doesn't have a
few more men like her on his side. And

01:44:25.661 --> 01:44:31.661 align:start position:0%
this is massive, you know, that the wife
of the head of the army can heckle the

01:44:29.861 --> 01:44:34.961 align:start position:0%
court in this manner in front of all
these armed guards. You know, it just

01:44:32.961 --> 01:44:37.961 align:start position:0%
shows that this is a live event and
anything can happen.

01:44:52.661 --> 01:45:01.961 align:start position:0%
The most important trial in history is
about to begin.

01:44:57.261 --> 01:45:01.961 align:start position:0%
The king takes his seat right here.

01:45:03.061 --> 01:45:08.861 align:start position:0%
This trial will determine the power,
authority, and legitimacy of the

01:45:06.861 --> 01:45:13.561 align:start position:0%
monarchy forever.
It's going to be a high court drama like

01:45:10.861 --> 01:45:18.761 align:start position:0%
nothing the world has ever seen.
&gt;&gt; It must have been really disorientating

01:45:15.661 --> 01:45:23.161 align:start position:0%
for him. He sits down. He's got the
crowd and the guards behind him. He

01:45:21.161 --> 01:45:25.561 align:start position:0%
can't see them. He can only feel them
and hear them.

01:45:24.361 --> 01:45:32.661 align:start position:0%
And in front of him, he's got all these
people that he barely recognizes. He's

01:45:28.061 --> 01:45:32.661 align:start position:0%
got no legal counsel. It's just him.

01:45:35.561 --> 01:45:38.861 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; John Cook stands.

01:45:39.161 --> 01:45:46.261 align:start position:0%
The charge sheet he's holding is the
result of days of intense and exhausting

01:45:43.861 --> 01:45:46.261 align:start position:0%
work.

01:45:46.861 --> 01:45:51.961 align:start position:0%
This is the biggest moment of the young
lawyer's life.

01:45:52.361 --> 01:46:02.661 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; And as Cook is starting his opening
speech, the king picks up his cane,

01:45:59.961 --> 01:46:06.561 align:start position:0%
which has a beautiful silver tip,
and bangs the barrister. Stop, he says.

01:46:05.261 --> 01:46:11.661 align:start position:0%
Hold.
&gt;&gt; And Cook carries on.

01:46:08.861 --> 01:46:16.662 align:start position:0%
And then Charles prods him again.
&gt;&gt; Cook ignored him. He poked him again,

01:46:13.662 --> 01:46:21.962 align:start position:0%
Cook ignored him. He then did it more
firmly. Bang comes the cane, the king

01:46:19.962 --> 01:46:26.363 align:start position:0%
striking the barrister.
&gt;&gt; And then the tip, the silver tip of his

01:46:24.062 --> 01:46:29.562 align:start position:0%
cane falls off
and it rolls on the floor.

01:46:30.462 --> 01:46:35.262 align:start position:0%
And Charles looks at Cook as if, you
know, pick it up.

01:46:36.363 --> 01:46:46.262 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Cook refuses and continues his opening
speech. And view of thousands of his

01:46:43.863 --> 01:46:51.062 align:start position:0%
subject, the king
steps down at the barrister's feet

01:46:49.762 --> 01:46:57.162 align:start position:0%
and to
almost grovelling to scrabble around on

01:46:54.262 --> 01:47:01.662 align:start position:0%
the floor to find the silver tip of his
cane. The symbolism couldn't have been

01:46:59.262 --> 01:47:05.863 align:start position:0%
worse. This is the king bowing before
the majesty of the law, not the other

01:47:04.262 --> 01:47:10.462 align:start position:0%
way round.
Um and many people saw it as as a bad

01:47:08.762 --> 01:47:16.962 align:start position:0%
omen.
&gt;&gt; And this is the moment, this is one of

01:47:14.762 --> 01:47:24.462 align:start position:0%
the most important moments in British
history when the people see their king

01:47:21.062 --> 01:47:27.562 align:start position:0%
beneath the law. However high ye be, the
law is above you.

01:47:31.363 --> 01:47:38.863 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Once Charles has recovered the cane top
and his dignity, the commotion dies down

01:47:36.662 --> 01:47:43.662 align:start position:0%
and John Cook continues.
The charge sheet takes 10 minutes to

01:47:41.162 --> 01:47:43.662 align:start position:0%
read.

01:47:45.962 --> 01:47:51.562 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; So he's blamed for it all. You know,
he's blamed for all the death,

01:47:49.363 --> 01:47:57.262 align:start position:0%
all the destruction. He is branded a
tyrant, traitor, and murderer, and a

01:47:54.662 --> 01:47:59.962 align:start position:0%
public and implacable enemy of the
Commonwealth.

01:48:03.462 --> 01:48:10.262 align:start position:0%
This is the first time Charles will have
heard the treason charges in full.

01:48:07.863 --> 01:48:14.662 align:start position:0%
Um he's trying to keep his cool. He's
looking at the judges. He's giving them

01:48:11.762 --> 01:48:20.663 align:start position:0%
a hard stare.
But when he hears those words, tyrant,

01:48:17.563 --> 01:48:25.964 align:start position:0%
traitor, murderer, he laughs out loud.
And this is a mistake because he is he

01:48:22.563 --> 01:48:25.964 align:start position:0%
is treating the court with scorn.

01:48:26.864 --> 01:48:33.964 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Lord President Bradshaw instructs the
king to answer the charges.

01:48:32.364 --> 01:48:38.163 align:start position:0%
The king pauses.
Then he plays his card.

01:48:38.663 --> 01:48:46.663 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Charles responds.
First, I must know by what power I am

01:48:44.063 --> 01:48:52.364 align:start position:0%
called hither before I will give answer.
By what authority? I mean lawful

01:48:49.163 --> 01:48:57.364 align:start position:0%
authority. Remember, I am your king,
your lawful king. I have trust committed

01:48:55.263 --> 01:49:01.663 align:start position:0%
to me by God.
Charles refuses to plead. He refuses to

01:49:00.163 --> 01:49:07.163 align:start position:0%
acknowledge the legal authority of the
court. He states that he is king by

01:49:04.163 --> 01:49:11.263 align:start position:0%
divine right and no power can remove him
from his God-given position.

01:49:09.364 --> 01:49:15.563 align:start position:0%
The legitimacy of the court hangs in
abeyance.

01:49:12.964 --> 01:49:21.163 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The king was expected to plead guilty or
not guilty. He refused to plead.

01:49:18.964 --> 01:49:26.263 align:start position:0%
This was the most sensible thing he
could possibly do

01:49:23.163 --> 01:49:31.763 align:start position:0%
because he was then throwing back at the
tribunal the problem,

01:49:28.563 --> 01:49:36.864 align:start position:0%
the problem that they always recognized.
How can we try a king legally?

01:49:44.464 --> 01:49:51.663 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It's a good opening gambit. It succeeds
in rattling the judges a little bit, but

01:49:49.763 --> 01:49:56.964 align:start position:0%
it's going to be hard to sustain. You
know, this is only day one and he is one

01:49:53.763 --> 01:49:56.964 align:start position:0%
man against many.

01:49:59.163 --> 01:50:04.864 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The king's strategy works. Cromwell and
Bradshaw are left with no choice but to

01:50:03.763 --> 01:50:09.663 align:start position:0%
adjourn.
Charles leaves the court triumphant to

01:50:06.964 --> 01:50:15.964 align:start position:0%
cries of God save the king.
At the end of the first day, it's the

01:50:11.563 --> 01:50:15.964 align:start position:0%
king one, Parliament nil.

01:50:19.063 --> 01:50:27.065 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; On the first day of his trial, uh
Charles wins the contest completely.

01:50:24.765 --> 01:50:33.465 align:start position:0%
Um, the commissioners have to withdraw.
They are nonplussed by what happened.

01:50:29.965 --> 01:50:36.065 align:start position:0%
Um, he flummoxes them.
And they knew that this was going to

01:50:35.065 --> 01:50:40.265 align:start position:0%
happen.
Cromwell had already anticipated that

01:50:38.065 --> 01:50:44.365 align:start position:0%
Charles would deny the authority of the
court, and that's precisely what he did.

01:50:42.565 --> 01:50:47.765 align:start position:0%
And they still had to go to good answer
to him

01:50:45.265 --> 01:50:52.665 align:start position:0%
because there wasn't one.
On the other hand, the trial is

01:50:49.365 --> 01:51:01.765 align:start position:0%
underway. It looks like a real trial,
and they've got the bonus of the king uh

01:50:56.665 --> 01:51:01.765 align:start position:0%
grabbling for his silver tip.

01:51:13.365 --> 01:51:19.365 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; That night, the king is drawn into
another battle.

01:51:17.565 --> 01:51:26.065 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Charles spends the night in a private
house, um where um he has soldiers

01:51:22.865 --> 01:51:31.365 align:start position:0%
guarding him even in his bedroom.
Um, and they're allowed to smoke and

01:51:27.765 --> 01:51:34.465 align:start position:0%
drink in his some private rooms. So he
has no privacy.

01:51:34.665 --> 01:51:40.065 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The furious king demands that the guards
leave him to sleep.

01:51:42.565 --> 01:51:46.965 align:start position:0%
After an angry dispute, he gets his way.

01:51:57.065 --> 01:52:05.565 align:start position:0%
It's day two of the trial.
&gt;&gt; On day two, the most important thing is

01:52:02.965 --> 01:52:12.065 align:start position:0%
that there is a day two.
In English criminal history, there

01:52:09.065 --> 01:52:18.365 align:start position:0%
usually wasn't a day two. Trials all
had to complete within one day.

01:52:15.965 --> 01:52:24.966 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Westminster Hall is so noisy that Lord
President John Bradshaw orders silence

01:52:21.366 --> 01:52:24.966 align:start position:0%
on pain of imprisonment.

01:52:25.566 --> 01:52:32.266 align:start position:0%
On the first day, the King could refuse
to plead to John Cook's charges.

01:52:30.166 --> 01:52:36.266 align:start position:0%
The court has no right to try him,
Charles maintains.

01:52:34.766 --> 01:52:42.166 align:start position:0%
This is a problem for Cromwell and the
commissioners, but potentially an even

01:52:38.666 --> 01:52:42.166 align:start position:0%
bigger one for Charles.

01:52:42.266 --> 01:52:48.766 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The punishment for refusing to enter a
plea is to be pressed

01:52:46.566 --> 01:52:52.866 align:start position:0%
to death. It's a medieval punishment,
and it's not obsolete. You know, as

01:52:50.366 --> 01:52:56.566 align:start position:0%
recently as Elizabeth I's reign, a woman
was pressed to death.

01:52:54.666 --> 01:53:00.066 align:start position:0%
She was ordered to lie down on a sharp
stone.

01:52:57.766 --> 01:53:05.666 align:start position:0%
A door was placed on top of her, and
seven or 800 weight was put on top of

01:53:02.366 --> 01:53:09.466 align:start position:0%
that until she was crushed to death.
Nobody wants to see that happen to the

01:53:07.166 --> 01:53:09.466 align:start position:0%
King.

01:53:12.366 --> 01:53:21.466 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Again, Charles refused to plead.
In justification, he plays a trump card.

01:53:18.566 --> 01:53:24.866 align:start position:0%
He aligns himself with the people.
Charles explains that if he can be

01:53:23.466 --> 01:53:29.166 align:start position:0%
legitimately tried by this
self-appointed court outside of

01:53:27.066 --> 01:53:33.766 align:start position:0%
conventional jurisdiction, then it could
try anyone.

01:53:30.666 --> 01:53:33.766 align:start position:0%
No one is safe.

01:53:34.166 --> 01:53:41.566 align:start position:0%
If power without law make any laws, I do
not know what subject in England can be

01:53:39.466 --> 01:53:45.566 align:start position:0%
sure of his life or anything he calls
his own.

01:53:42.766 --> 01:53:49.666 align:start position:0%
It is not my case alone, but the freedom
and liberty of the people.

01:53:47.766 --> 01:53:54.966 align:start position:0%
In refusing to recognize this court
then, Charles claims he's acting to

01:53:51.866 --> 01:53:54.966 align:start position:0%
protect his people.

01:53:58.366 --> 01:54:06.166 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; He sees around him power, the power of
the sword. Um, but

01:54:03.066 --> 01:54:10.966 align:start position:0%
does power have the right to make law?
No.

01:54:07.366 --> 01:54:18.066 align:start position:0%
And if he is to accept the court, then
he is basically going to let the people

01:54:13.966 --> 01:54:22.968 align:start position:0%
of England live in a country where
whoever has the sword makes the laws,

01:54:20.566 --> 01:54:26.367 align:start position:0%
and and make it up as they go along.
And as he says,

01:54:24.568 --> 01:54:29.768 align:start position:0%
you know, who would have any liberties
then?

01:54:27.367 --> 01:54:35.568 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; This is the king's cleverest move.
Rather than being portrayed as the

01:54:32.268 --> 01:54:40.468 align:start position:0%
person who is suborning the rights and
liberties of the people, he portrays

01:54:37.968 --> 01:54:45.367 align:start position:0%
himself as the champion of the rights
and liberties of the people. He is the

01:54:42.668 --> 01:54:49.168 align:start position:0%
defender of the law. He is the defender
of the ordinary person who could be

01:54:46.968 --> 01:54:54.068 align:start position:0%
oppressed by Parliament. He is not the
tyrant. Who's the tyrant? Parliament's

01:54:51.768 --> 01:54:58.268 align:start position:0%
the tyrant.
&gt;&gt; Lord President Bradshaw listens to this.

01:54:59.168 --> 01:55:05.268 align:start position:0%
He decides he has heard enough.
He spits.

01:55:03.168 --> 01:55:11.968 align:start position:0%
How great a friend you have been to the
laws and liberties of the people. Let

01:55:07.668 --> 01:55:11.968 align:start position:0%
all England and the world judge.

01:55:13.668 --> 01:55:22.968 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The king is determined not to fight the
merits of the charge,

01:55:20.268 --> 01:55:29.367 align:start position:0%
which means he has to keep himself to
his one legal point that this is not a

01:55:25.867 --> 01:55:33.867 align:start position:0%
lawful court. But inevitably, in his
argument with Bradshaw, he steps over

01:55:32.668 --> 01:55:39.968 align:start position:0%
the line.
And at one point, he says, I stand for

01:55:37.668 --> 01:55:45.968 align:start position:0%
the liberty of the subject more than you
do. That was why I was forced to

01:55:43.367 --> 01:55:51.367 align:start position:0%
take to and he suddenly realizes that
he's about to say why he was forced to

01:55:48.768 --> 01:55:55.568 align:start position:0%
take to arms. And that, of course, was
the

01:55:52.668 --> 01:56:01.568 align:start position:0%
the case against him that he started the
civil war. So he came perilously close

01:55:59.068 --> 01:56:06.668 align:start position:0%
then, and I think everyone realized that
that was a sort of semi-confession, and

01:56:04.268 --> 01:56:10.367 align:start position:0%
he drew back. He realized he'd made a
mistake.

01:56:07.867 --> 01:56:15.068 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The king has lost composure today. He
seems irritated and argumentative.

01:56:13.367 --> 01:56:19.468 align:start position:0%
Perhaps he feels that his strategy isn't
working.

01:56:16.568 --> 01:56:23.367 align:start position:0%
In this battle of wills and legislation,
the prosecution is gaining ground.

01:56:27.068 --> 01:56:29.769 align:start position:0%
Next time,

01:56:30.369 --> 01:56:38.169 align:start position:0%
Parliament battles to break the legal
stalemate.

01:56:34.569 --> 01:56:41.869 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Its central message was that no one is
above the law.

01:56:39.769 --> 01:56:45.369 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; But the threat of a treason charge still
hangs over those who would convict a

01:56:43.769 --> 01:56:50.369 align:start position:0%
monarch.
&gt;&gt; It speaks volumes about what a radical

01:56:48.269 --> 01:56:55.069 align:start position:0%
thing this really is.
&gt;&gt; And as the bitter end draws near, not

01:56:53.169 --> 01:56:59.469 align:start position:0%
even Parliament is ready for the
consequences.

01:56:56.569 --> 01:57:02.869 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It is just an extraordinary thing that
they rushed this legislation through

01:57:01.269 --> 01:57:07.369 align:start position:0%
minutes before
the blade was going to

01:57:04.269 --> 01:57:07.369 align:start position:0%
chop the king's head off.

01:57:40.369 --> 01:57:48.869 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; As the month of January 1649 draws to a
close, the future of England hangs in

01:57:46.469 --> 01:57:54.369 align:start position:0%
the balance.
Something momentous is unfolding.

01:57:52.369 --> 01:58:01.569 align:start position:0%
For the last two days, the king of
England has been publicly on trial,

01:57:57.869 --> 01:58:07.669 align:start position:0%
accused of being a tyrant, traitor,
murderer, and a public and implacable

01:58:04.469 --> 01:58:13.469 align:start position:0%
enemy to the Commonwealth of England.
But Charles is refusing to plead.

01:58:10.969 --> 01:58:18.769 align:start position:0%
Without a plea, the very legitimacy of
the trial hangs by a threat.

01:58:16.069 --> 01:58:22.069 align:start position:0%
And so do both Charles's life and the
future of the monarchy.

01:58:24.069 --> 01:58:32.970 align:start position:0%
This confrontation comes after seven
years of civil war that has divided

01:58:29.170 --> 01:58:38.870 align:start position:0%
towns, friends, and even families.
&gt;&gt; There is nothing civil about civil war.

01:58:36.370 --> 01:58:43.970 align:start position:0%
They have seen so much suffering and
death and destruction. They've just had

01:58:41.570 --> 01:58:49.970 align:start position:0%
enough.
&gt;&gt; I'm historian Lisa Hilton, and over

01:58:46.470 --> 01:58:55.370 align:start position:0%
three episodes I'm tracing day by day,
hour by hour, the five weeks that led to

01:58:53.170 --> 01:59:03.170 align:start position:0%
one of the most seismic moments in our
constitutional history.

01:58:58.170 --> 01:59:09.070 align:start position:0%
The trial and death of King Charles I.
&gt;&gt; No king in England had ever been put on

01:59:05.570 --> 01:59:14.270 align:start position:0%
trial for any charge, let alone treason.
It was unprecedented.

01:59:11.970 --> 01:59:20.870 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; How did the nation affect this radical
step, the reverberations of which are

01:59:16.770 --> 01:59:26.370 align:start position:0%
still felt even to this day?
&gt;&gt; What they did do change English law and

01:59:23.770 --> 01:59:31.970 align:start position:0%
politics forever.
&gt;&gt; Hidden within these weeks are the chance

01:59:28.870 --> 01:59:37.470 align:start position:0%
events, secret manipulations, and
revolutionary actions that led to this

01:59:35.170 --> 01:59:41.970 align:start position:0%
ultimate decision.
How did a country kill its king?

01:59:56.370 --> 02:00:06.870 align:start position:0%
It's the 23rd of January 1649,
the third day of Charles I's trial, and

02:00:04.270 --> 02:00:11.770 align:start position:0%
so far it has been nothing short of
disastrous.

02:00:09.470 --> 02:00:17.570 align:start position:0%
In Westminster's Great Hall, Oliver
Cromwell and the commissioners once

02:00:13.770 --> 02:00:17.570 align:start position:0%
again confront the king.

02:00:18.670 --> 02:00:27.070 align:start position:0%
He is the divinely appointed monarch,
and therefore he's absolutely not going

02:00:23.170 --> 02:00:31.172 align:start position:0%
to play ball and give them any sense of
uh the authority that they're claiming.

02:00:28.970 --> 02:00:35.572 align:start position:0%
Are Are really going to kill a king?
He's waiting and watching and expecting

02:00:33.372 --> 02:00:40.172 align:start position:0%
in this battle of nerve that they will
crumble first.

02:00:38.372 --> 02:00:46.772 align:start position:0%
Afraid the trial may collapse if the
king won't plead, prosecutor John Cook

02:00:43.272 --> 02:00:50.172 align:start position:0%
gives Charles an ultimatum.
&gt;&gt; Cook tells the king that a refusal to

02:00:48.772 --> 02:00:54.272 align:start position:0%
plead will have the weight of a
confession.

02:00:51.472 --> 02:00:58.172 align:start position:0%
This was Charles in a tricky position.
If he pleads to the charge, he

02:00:55.672 --> 02:01:03.272 align:start position:0%
acknowledges the legality of the court.
If he remains silent, his guilt will be

02:01:00.572 --> 02:01:08.272 align:start position:0%
assumed.
&gt;&gt; To reiterate Cook's warning, the chief

02:01:05.672 --> 02:01:13.972 align:start position:0%
judge, John Bradshaw, offers Charles a
final chance to admit to the charges or

02:01:11.272 --> 02:01:13.972 align:start position:0%
deny them.

02:01:14.972 --> 02:01:21.072 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Sir,
you are to give your positive and final

02:01:18.372 --> 02:01:28.072 align:start position:0%
answer in plain English
whether you are guilty or not guilty

02:01:24.072 --> 02:01:33.772 align:start position:0%
to these treasons laid to your charge.
&gt;&gt; The king is morally culpable for so much

02:01:31.172 --> 02:01:38.872 align:start position:0%
that went wrong in the 17th century.
That doesn't mean he was legally

02:01:35.872 --> 02:01:42.772 align:start position:0%
responsible for it.
&gt;&gt; The king is resolute.

02:01:40.672 --> 02:01:48.472 align:start position:0%
He will not legitimize proceedings with
a plea. He replies,

02:01:45.572 --> 02:01:52.972 align:start position:0%
For the charge, I value it not a rush.
It is the liberty of the people of

02:01:50.072 --> 02:01:57.772 align:start position:0%
England that I stand for. There is no
law that allows you to make your king

02:01:55.172 --> 02:01:57.772 align:start position:0%
your prisoner.

02:01:59.672 --> 02:02:05.972 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; When the king challenges the legality of
the court, he's on a very firm legal

02:02:04.772 --> 02:02:10.672 align:start position:0%
basis.
It gave him the moral high ground. It

02:02:08.172 --> 02:02:15.372 align:start position:0%
gave him the legal high ground.
The members of the court didn't know how

02:02:12.872 --> 02:02:19.272 align:start position:0%
to respond. Charles said, show me the
authority. Show me the show me the law.

02:02:17.672 --> 02:02:22.672 align:start position:0%
And of course, they couldn't show them
because it didn't exist.

02:02:23.872 --> 02:02:31.572 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Bradshaw has been walking a legal
tightrope, politely trying to persuade

02:02:28.072 --> 02:02:31.572 align:start position:0%
Charles to acknowledge the court.

02:02:31.972 --> 02:02:39.573 align:start position:0%
But after three days, his patience
snaps.

02:02:36.373 --> 02:02:42.573 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Bradshaw is visibly irritated.
He says this is the third time you've

02:02:41.173 --> 02:02:48.273 align:start position:0%
refused to enter a plea. This is the
third time you have impugned the court.

02:02:45.573 --> 02:02:52.773 align:start position:0%
And he says, you know, you claim to be
looking out for the people, to be

02:02:50.073 --> 02:02:58.173 align:start position:0%
protecting their liberties, but actions
speak louder than words. And he then

02:02:55.473 --> 02:03:02.473 align:start position:0%
goes on to say that uh you have written
your meaning in bloody characters

02:03:00.373 --> 02:03:07.373 align:start position:0%
throughout the realm.
You know, he can't speak out without

02:03:04.173 --> 02:03:13.873 align:start position:0%
accepting the authority of the court.
&gt;&gt; Once again, the scales tip. This time in

02:03:10.973 --> 02:03:19.473 align:start position:0%
Parliament's favor.
As night falls on the 23rd of January,

02:03:16.773 --> 02:03:24.073 align:start position:0%
Bradshaw, Cook, and Cromwell resolve to
prove the king is guilty as charged.

02:03:34.673 --> 02:03:41.073 align:start position:0%
It's the fourth day of the trial, and
the prosecution have a new tactic

02:03:39.673 --> 02:03:46.273 align:start position:0%
to convince the commissioners that
Charles is a murderer, a man of blood.

02:03:46.773 --> 02:03:52.873 align:start position:0%
In order to lawfully condemn the king,
the judges resolve that they need

02:03:50.273 --> 02:03:57.573 align:start position:0%
witness testimony against him, evidence
that he is indeed a man of blood. So the

02:03:55.873 --> 02:04:00.173 align:start position:0%
High Court decides to call such
witnesses.

02:04:00.573 --> 02:04:08.173 align:start position:0%
Calling witnesses is nothing new, but
Parliament takes the extraordinary

02:04:04.673 --> 02:04:08.173 align:start position:0%
decision to halt the trial

02:04:09.673 --> 02:04:17.073 align:start position:0%
and hear 33 testimonies away from open
court and away from the king.

02:04:20.573 --> 02:04:27.673 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The witness statements aren't needed.
It's just for the commissioners

02:04:24.973 --> 02:04:32.073 align:start position:0%
just to shore up their own beliefs in
what they are doing. They were borrowing

02:04:29.473 --> 02:04:35.573 align:start position:0%
from several different legal areas, one
of which that in martial law trials you

02:04:33.773 --> 02:04:39.675 align:start position:0%
take witness statements.
It was something that

02:04:37.175 --> 02:04:42.175 align:start position:0%
Charles himself had used throughout the
war.

02:04:40.574 --> 02:04:46.374 align:start position:0%
And so they were trying in some way the
king by his own

02:04:43.675 --> 02:04:50.175 align:start position:0%
legal standards.
&gt;&gt; The words of these witnesses were

02:04:47.675 --> 02:04:54.574 align:start position:0%
transcribed and are now kept in the
national archives.

02:04:53.074 --> 02:04:57.074 align:start position:0%
Where did the prosecution find these
witnesses?

02:04:55.275 --> 02:04:59.974 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; As far as we can tell they're mostly
drawn from the parliamentarian army

02:04:58.675 --> 02:05:03.374 align:start position:0%
itself.
&gt;&gt; Do you think it would be reasonable to

02:05:01.675 --> 02:05:07.974 align:start position:0%
say that the commissioner said okay
we're going to charge him with this, go

02:05:05.374 --> 02:05:11.675 align:start position:0%
and find me a man in the ex regiment who
can corroborate it?

02:05:08.974 --> 02:05:15.974 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Yes. If we take this chap um William
Brain, he's very much a committed

02:05:14.175 --> 02:05:19.474 align:start position:0%
parliamentarian.
William is present when the king's

02:05:17.675 --> 02:05:23.374 align:start position:0%
standard is raised at Nottingham.
&gt;&gt; So this act the raising of the royal

02:05:21.574 --> 02:05:25.374 align:start position:0%
standard that was that was crucial to
the case that the prosecutors were

02:05:24.574 --> 02:05:30.675 align:start position:0%
bringing.
&gt;&gt; Yes, it's really showing that Charles

02:05:27.275 --> 02:05:34.574 align:start position:0%
has personally initiated this conflict
uh and therefore that he is a traitor

02:05:32.374 --> 02:05:37.675 align:start position:0%
because he's waged war against his own
people and it's the people who are

02:05:35.974 --> 02:05:42.374 align:start position:0%
sovereign not the king.
One of the tantalizing witness

02:05:39.175 --> 02:05:47.974 align:start position:0%
statements is from Thomas Rawlins.
Rawlins says that the royalist forces at

02:05:45.275 --> 02:05:53.175 align:start position:0%
Fowey in Cornwall plunder the civilians
even though the king is very close by he

02:05:50.675 --> 02:05:56.474 align:start position:0%
does nothing to stop this.
But in fact we know that the king

02:05:54.874 --> 02:06:00.175 align:start position:0%
ordered that those royalist plunderers
should be executed.

02:05:58.175 --> 02:06:05.374 align:start position:0%
So he doesn't you know ignore what's
going on at all. When he's alerted to it

02:06:02.574 --> 02:06:09.374 align:start position:0%
he takes very swift very severe action
against that breach of the laws of war.

02:06:07.374 --> 02:06:13.675 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It's difficult to to feel that these
witness statements are anything other

02:06:11.374 --> 02:06:19.275 align:start position:0%
than extraordinarily
biased. Would you agree?

02:06:16.175 --> 02:06:24.775 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; I think yes we can see that
some of the witnesses you know we we can

02:06:21.374 --> 02:06:29.574 align:start position:0%
imagine uh um were kind of being pressed
to give this evidence. We know from the

02:06:27.474 --> 02:06:34.874 align:start position:0%
testimony of one of the witnesses Samuel
Burden he says he was commanded to give

02:06:32.974 --> 02:06:38.475 align:start position:0%
evidence by his officer. So he says he
was he was pretty much coerced.

02:06:37.074 --> 02:06:43.276 align:start position:0%
One of the really powerful things about
witnesses is that personal experience.

02:06:41.376 --> 02:06:49.276 align:start position:0%
And yeah, and that you're hearing from
real people who have suffered, and those

02:06:45.575 --> 02:06:49.276 align:start position:0%
real people need justice.

02:06:50.776 --> 02:07:00.776 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; With each passing day, Charles's control
over the trial is waning, and the

02:06:56.176 --> 02:07:00.776 align:start position:0%
outcome seems increasingly inevitable.

02:07:08.075 --> 02:07:16.276 align:start position:0%
The trial is paused for another day as
the court hear more witnesses speak out

02:07:13.376 --> 02:07:22.776 align:start position:0%
against the king.
&gt;&gt; What the witnesses are stating they saw

02:07:19.776 --> 02:07:28.676 align:start position:0%
are events that no one doubts happened.
He can't deny the fact that he was a

02:07:25.676 --> 02:07:34.176 align:start position:0%
part of the war, and therefore he was
the author, occasioner of all of these

02:07:31.575 --> 02:07:38.376 align:start position:0%
sufferings. He's guilty of it all.
&gt;&gt; Cromwell's tactic has paid off. After

02:07:37.176 --> 02:07:43.876 align:start position:0%
the witnesses have delivered their
testimony,

02:07:39.876 --> 02:07:49.676 align:start position:0%
69 out of 135 commissioners
at last conclude there is enough

02:07:45.975 --> 02:07:49.676 align:start position:0%
evidence to convict the king.

02:07:50.376 --> 02:07:55.676 align:start position:0%
Tomorrow they will sit to decide
Charles's fate.

02:08:02.776 --> 02:08:08.975 align:start position:0%
It's the sixth day of the trial.
Once again, Cromwell and the

02:08:07.376 --> 02:08:14.975 align:start position:0%
commissioners gather to consider the
case against the king. Once again, the

02:08:11.876 --> 02:08:14.975 align:start position:0%
king is absent.

02:08:15.276 --> 02:08:21.575 align:start position:0%
A draft of the final charge is read out,
amendments are made and agreed.

02:08:21.975 --> 02:08:28.876 align:start position:0%
The final charge concludes that Charles
can be convicted of treasonous crimes

02:08:26.975 --> 02:08:36.575 align:start position:0%
against the people.
The penalty for treason is death.

02:08:33.176 --> 02:08:40.977 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; They defined treason as a breach of the
rights of the English people. He had

02:08:38.575 --> 02:08:45.377 align:start position:0%
slaughtered people, killed people. He'd
used his power to abuse others.

02:08:46.477 --> 02:08:53.177 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; But the commission has also agreed that
if the King will finally acknowledge the

02:08:50.777 --> 02:08:57.577 align:start position:0%
authority of the court, they'll adjourn
and consider what's to be done.

02:08:55.177 --> 02:09:00.277 align:start position:0%
Tomorrow will be the King's last chance
to comply.

02:09:10.377 --> 02:09:19.277 align:start position:0%
It's the 27th of January and the final
day of the trial of Charles I.

02:09:17.477 --> 02:09:25.877 align:start position:0%
It's the culmination of not only the
trial, but seven years of civil war and

02:09:22.677 --> 02:09:30.377 align:start position:0%
seemingly endless negotiations.
In the Great Hall of Westminster,

02:09:27.777 --> 02:09:38.277 align:start position:0%
there's a huge crowd waiting to see what
fate will befall their King.

02:09:32.577 --> 02:09:38.277 align:start position:0%
Some shout justice, others execution.

02:09:38.577 --> 02:09:46.277 align:start position:0%
As Charles takes his seat here, he looks
over at John Bradshaw, the Lord

02:09:43.577 --> 02:09:51.577 align:start position:0%
President of this High Court of Justice.
Bradshaw is robed in red, traditionally

02:09:49.177 --> 02:09:57.477 align:start position:0%
worn only for ceremonial occasions or
criminal cases.

02:09:54.577 --> 02:10:03.477 align:start position:0%
This is the riskiest moment in English
legal history. If sentencing goes wrong,

02:10:00.777 --> 02:10:08.377 align:start position:0%
the tables could turn and charges of
treason could be leveled at Charles's

02:10:05.377 --> 02:10:14.377 align:start position:0%
accusers instead of at the King. History
is being made and the setting and

02:10:10.677 --> 02:10:19.177 align:start position:0%
ceremony reflect the gravity of the day.
As Bradshaw opens the proceedings, he

02:10:16.677 --> 02:10:27.277 align:start position:0%
takes a momentous decision.
He declares that Charles's refusal to

02:10:21.777 --> 02:10:27.277 align:start position:0%
plead must now be taken as a confession.

02:10:27.477 --> 02:10:35.177 align:start position:0%
Finally, Charles is allowed to speak and
the court scribe records his words.

02:10:32.777 --> 02:10:40.477 align:start position:0%
He says that he wishes to delay the ugly
sentence I believe will be passed on me,

02:10:37.977 --> 02:10:45.178 align:start position:0%
for such hasty sentences once passed are
sooner repented of than recalled.

02:10:43.277 --> 02:10:47.678 align:start position:0%
He speaks of his desire for the peace of
the kingdom.

02:10:48.779 --> 02:10:55.779 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; He says he wants his word to be heard in
front of the lords and the commons. So

02:10:52.479 --> 02:10:59.379 align:start position:0%
once again, he's impugning the validity
of the court.

02:10:57.379 --> 02:11:02.579 align:start position:0%
While Bradshaw is telling Charles that
he can't be heard, there's a commotion

02:11:01.279 --> 02:11:06.079 align:start position:0%
behind him. And one of the
commissioners, the MP for Arundel, John

02:11:04.479 --> 02:11:09.579 align:start position:0%
Downs,
stands up and shouts, have we hearts of

02:11:08.279 --> 02:11:13.579 align:start position:0%
stone?
Are we men? And Cromwell, who's right in

02:11:11.479 --> 02:11:17.879 align:start position:0%
front of him, so turns around and says,
are you mad? What ails you?

02:11:15.879 --> 02:11:21.178 align:start position:0%
And basically says, sit down, sit down.
And And Downs says he won't.

02:11:21.479 --> 02:11:25.979 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The court descends into chaos.

02:11:29.079 --> 02:11:35.779 align:start position:0%
Any compassion for the king threatens to
destroy these fragile proceedings.

02:11:38.579 --> 02:11:44.379 align:start position:0%
Once again, Bradshaw addresses the king,
telling him that he will not be heard by

02:11:42.379 --> 02:11:50.279 align:start position:0%
the lords and commons, but they will
proceed straight to sentencing.

02:11:47.379 --> 02:11:54.479 align:start position:0%
Once again, the king begs to be heard,
claiming that he has a new plan for the

02:11:52.079 --> 02:12:00.079 align:start position:0%
peace of the kingdom.
&gt;&gt; He tries to cut a deal, and the only

02:11:57.379 --> 02:12:04.178 align:start position:0%
deal they might accept is if he were
prepared to abdicate,

02:12:01.979 --> 02:12:10.779 align:start position:0%
be sent off in exile to the West Indies
or somewhere. In fact, the commissioners

02:12:07.479 --> 02:12:16.579 align:start position:0%
who see him now as a vengeful monarch
who can not be trusted

02:12:13.079 --> 02:12:20.379 align:start position:0%
not to make war of on them
decide to plow ahead.

02:12:23.479 --> 02:12:33.279 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Bradshaw cuts the king short
and begins a 40-minute oration in which

02:12:29.079 --> 02:12:33.279 align:start position:0%
he will sum up the case against Charles.

02:12:33.479 --> 02:12:41.979 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Bradshaw wants to prove that Charles is
below the law. To do that, he quotes

02:12:39.279 --> 02:12:46.680 align:start position:0%
from the Bible. He relies on classical
examples like Caligula and Nero. And he

02:12:45.579 --> 02:12:50.480 align:start position:0%
claims
uh that Parliament are acting in the

02:12:48.280 --> 02:12:54.780 align:start position:0%
same way that the barons did when
fighting King John before writing Magna

02:12:53.480 --> 02:13:00.480 align:start position:0%
Carta.
It was Parliament's duty to step in and

02:12:57.480 --> 02:13:05.580 align:start position:0%
defend the English Constitution against
any tyrannical king.

02:13:03.580 --> 02:13:10.480 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; But the heart of Bradshaw's argument
against the king rests on an event that

02:13:08.380 --> 02:13:16.180 align:start position:0%
had taken place just a few hundred
metres away in Westminster Abbey in

02:13:13.180 --> 02:13:16.180 align:start position:0%
1626.

02:13:16.280 --> 02:13:21.680 align:start position:0%
Since William the Conqueror, all but two
of the English monarchs have been

02:13:19.880 --> 02:13:25.180 align:start position:0%
crowned here,
and Charles is no exception.

02:13:25.780 --> 02:13:33.280 align:start position:0%
At the climax of the ceremony, Charles
undertook the solemn coronation oath in

02:13:31.580 --> 02:13:38.280 align:start position:0%
which he was asked to promise to keep
the peace to God, the holy church, the

02:13:36.080 --> 02:13:42.880 align:start position:0%
clergy, and the people.
I will, he responded.

02:13:40.680 --> 02:13:47.480 align:start position:0%
He was then asked to uphold the laws and
rightful customs which the commonality

02:13:45.280 --> 02:13:52.880 align:start position:0%
of this your kingdom have.
Again, he promised to do so.

02:13:49.980 --> 02:13:57.780 align:start position:0%
Now, in Westminster Hall, Bradshaw is
arguing that the king has broken this

02:13:54.980 --> 02:13:57.780 align:start position:0%
sacred oath.

02:13:58.880 --> 02:14:05.480 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The coronation oath was a bond between
the king and the people.

02:14:03.580 --> 02:14:10.780 align:start position:0%
I I think he was probably talking about
something uh broader and more

02:14:07.680 --> 02:14:15.780 align:start position:0%
contractual. The king and the people
have an agreement with each other, and

02:14:13.480 --> 02:14:19.680 align:start position:0%
if one of them breaks the terms of those
agreements

02:14:17.280 --> 02:14:22.780 align:start position:0%
uh by oppressing the other, then the
bond's broken.

02:14:23.280 --> 02:14:31.180 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It's a brilliant move because this same
oath has been at the very heart of

02:14:27.980 --> 02:14:37.380 align:start position:0%
Charles's defence.
Charles believes that he was born to be

02:14:33.180 --> 02:14:41.880 align:start position:0%
king, and he only answers to God.
For him, the coronation was the

02:14:38.980 --> 02:14:50.081 align:start position:0%
confirmation of this.
It's a low blow, but a blinding one.

02:14:46.380 --> 02:14:58.381 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; He is now conscious of justice, the
momentum if you like of justice. He

02:14:53.181 --> 02:15:04.281 align:start position:0%
realizes that his tactic has not worked.
&gt;&gt; Bradshaw cleverly ends this stage of his

02:15:00.981 --> 02:15:09.181 align:start position:0%
oration saying, Sir, if this bond be
once broken, farewell sovereignty.

02:15:09.781 --> 02:15:18.581 align:start position:0%
The authority of a ruler is only valid
if he protects his subjects.

02:15:14.381 --> 02:15:18.581 align:start position:0%
The king has no defense against this.

02:15:20.181 --> 02:15:27.081 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; And then he starts to panic. He's
flailing, he's stuttering. I think he

02:15:24.481 --> 02:15:30.381 align:start position:0%
suddenly realizes the penny is suddenly
dropping that, you know, they're going

02:15:28.381 --> 02:15:33.881 align:start position:0%
to do this.
&gt;&gt; Bradshaw then commands the clerk to read

02:15:32.381 --> 02:15:40.181 align:start position:0%
the sentence.
Charles Stuart as a tyrant, traitor,

02:15:37.481 --> 02:15:43.681 align:start position:0%
murderer, and public enemy to the good
people of this nation shall be put to

02:15:42.481 --> 02:15:48.581 align:start position:0%
death
by the severing of his head

02:15:45.781 --> 02:15:48.581 align:start position:0%
from his body.

02:15:50.181 --> 02:15:58.881 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; They really are going to chop off his
head. He's desperate then to speak, to

02:15:56.381 --> 02:16:02.881 align:start position:0%
say his peace, uh, to put forward his
argument.

02:16:01.381 --> 02:16:08.081 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Then Charles says, but I'm the king. Of
course I can speak.

02:16:05.581 --> 02:16:12.781 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; But in English law, a condemned man is
already dead

02:16:09.681 --> 02:16:16.881 align:start position:0%
and a dead man can't speak.
&gt;&gt; As the last words ring out across the

02:16:14.881 --> 02:16:23.281 align:start position:0%
hall, the commissioners rise to their
feet to signify their assent.

02:16:20.581 --> 02:16:27.781 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; 69 commissioners stand in agreement of
the judgment, showing that they support

02:16:25.681 --> 02:16:32.181 align:start position:0%
the judgment. It's not Bradshaw's
judgment, it's all of their judgments.

02:16:32.882 --> 02:16:39.882 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Bradshaw dismisses Charles Stuart and
orders the guards to take him away.

02:16:40.481 --> 02:16:48.082 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; England has killed kings before.
Uh, if one thinks of Richard II, one

02:16:45.281 --> 02:16:53.583 align:start position:0%
thinks of Henry VI, it's done it before.
But this has been done by people who are

02:16:50.882 --> 02:16:58.883 align:start position:0%
usurping that power. There's never been
a kind of judicial sense. It is an

02:16:56.182 --> 02:17:04.782 align:start position:0%
enormous leap to make when he's going
against God's will.

02:17:02.083 --> 02:17:09.782 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; By giving judgment, their aim and what
they did do change English law and

02:17:07.482 --> 02:17:16.383 align:start position:0%
politics forever.
They had attempted to shatter and remove

02:17:13.682 --> 02:17:21.383 align:start position:0%
the idea of a divine right of kings and
to warn every single governor and every

02:17:19.182 --> 02:17:25.083 align:start position:0%
ruler that they were always to be held
to account by the people.

02:17:28.682 --> 02:17:35.682 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Charles is carried by sedan chair from
Westminster Hall to Whitehall Palace.

02:17:33.383 --> 02:17:40.883 align:start position:0%
Troops line the route, but curious
crowds jostle to catch a glimpse of

02:17:37.883 --> 02:17:46.982 align:start position:0%
their fallen king.
&gt;&gt; Londoners are shocked and people are

02:17:43.482 --> 02:17:50.883 align:start position:0%
sort of weeping at windows and so forth.
Parliament and the new Model Army are

02:17:49.083 --> 02:17:53.883 align:start position:0%
anxious, very anxious about what's going
to happen next.

02:17:54.782 --> 02:18:02.383 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; In Whitehall Palace, Charles asks his
gentleman of the bedchamber, Thomas

02:17:58.682 --> 02:18:08.982 align:start position:0%
Herbert, to remove his much-loved dogs.
He wishes to spend whatever time he has

02:18:04.482 --> 02:18:08.982 align:start position:0%
left preparing to meet his God.

02:18:09.682 --> 02:18:16.083 align:start position:0%
How long Charles will live is now firmly
in the hands of Cromwell and the

02:18:13.583 --> 02:18:20.583 align:start position:0%
commissioners.
A committee is hastily formed to decide

02:18:18.782 --> 02:18:23.282 align:start position:0%
on the place and time of the king's
execution.

02:18:26.182 --> 02:18:33.883 align:start position:0%
In the last 24 hours, the Parliament of
England has changed the world.

02:18:34.282 --> 02:18:42.482 align:start position:0%
Since time immemorial, the king has been
the cornerstone of English government,

02:18:39.282 --> 02:18:42.482 align:start position:0%
law, and justice.

02:18:43.383 --> 02:18:47.883 align:start position:0%
Now he has been sentenced to death.

02:18:54.482 --> 02:19:00.784 align:start position:0%
As dawn breaks on the Sabbath, there's a
chance for the drama of the day before

02:18:59.184 --> 02:19:05.084 align:start position:0%
to sink in.
&gt;&gt; There was a real sense of incredulity

02:19:02.984 --> 02:19:10.284 align:start position:0%
and of course also unease. You know, if
you kill the king, the world really has

02:19:07.184 --> 02:19:15.084 align:start position:0%
been turned upside down.
&gt;&gt; Under the Puritan regime, Sunday is

02:19:12.484 --> 02:19:18.884 align:start position:0%
strictly a day of rest. And as Cromwell
and the commissioners are all good

02:19:16.484 --> 02:19:23.184 align:start position:0%
Puritans, any plans for the king's
execution must be put on hold for the

02:19:20.884 --> 02:19:23.184 align:start position:0%
day.

02:19:23.584 --> 02:19:28.584 align:start position:0%
But there's no rest for those guarding
the king.

02:19:26.584 --> 02:19:32.684 align:start position:0%
In the afternoon, they moved Charles
from Whitehall to St. James's Palace.

02:19:32.984 --> 02:19:40.484 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; These carefully choreographed moves are
just trying to mitigate the risk of some

02:19:37.984 --> 02:19:45.984 align:start position:0%
kind of escape attempt,
just to keep Charles under a strict

02:19:43.384 --> 02:19:50.084 align:start position:0%
guard as is possible and away from
people who might seek to try and rescue

02:19:47.984 --> 02:19:55.685 align:start position:0%
him.
&gt;&gt; The last known portrait of Charles I,

02:19:52.584 --> 02:20:00.484 align:start position:0%
painted by Edward Bower, now hangs here
in St. James's Palace.

02:19:58.685 --> 02:20:04.484 align:start position:0%
Bower probably made studies of the king
during his trial and then worked the

02:20:01.984 --> 02:20:09.185 align:start position:0%
portrait up afterwards in the studio.
He shows the red velvet chair on which

02:20:06.484 --> 02:20:12.984 align:start position:0%
the king sat, the hat which he never
removed, and the Order of the Garter

02:20:11.084 --> 02:20:17.584 align:start position:0%
hung about his neck.
I'm really intrigued by the king's

02:20:14.685 --> 02:20:21.584 align:start position:0%
hands. We know that Charles was small
and undersized as a child, so it could

02:20:19.584 --> 02:20:27.484 align:start position:0%
be that this is simply an accurate
rather than a flattering representation.

02:20:24.384 --> 02:20:31.185 align:start position:0%
And yet they seem so small, so pale, and
so weak. Is there something critical

02:20:29.384 --> 02:20:36.185 align:start position:0%
here? Is it suggesting that these are
not the hands of a man who's capable of

02:20:33.284 --> 02:20:41.784 align:start position:0%
wielding power?
I've never seen Charles looking less

02:20:38.185 --> 02:20:45.284 align:start position:0%
magnificent or more human.
Although the picture was probably

02:20:42.984 --> 02:20:50.284 align:start position:0%
commissioned by one of his opponents,
it's not hostile.

02:20:47.185 --> 02:20:56.784 align:start position:0%
It has a kind of ambiguous tenderness to
it. The king's face looks weary. He's

02:20:53.884 --> 02:21:02.586 align:start position:0%
aged. His His is grey. He looks tired,
but at the same time, there's a kind of

02:20:58.286 --> 02:21:02.586 align:start position:0%
serenity, or is it resignation?

02:21:04.686 --> 02:21:10.786 align:start position:0%
Some courtiers asked to see Charles, but
he sends them away.

02:21:08.985 --> 02:21:16.186 align:start position:0%
He does, however, accept a messenger
delivering a letter from his son,

02:21:13.186 --> 02:21:16.186 align:start position:0%
the Prince of Wales.

02:21:16.786 --> 02:21:22.786 align:start position:0%
This letter must have been written
before sentence was passed. Charles,

02:21:20.686 --> 02:21:27.186 align:start position:0%
Prince of Wales, was in Holland, and
even the best circumstances news took

02:21:24.786 --> 02:21:31.485 align:start position:0%
time to spread so far.
The Prince recalls that he's had no news

02:21:29.086 --> 02:21:35.985 align:start position:0%
of his father beyond rumour and printed
news sheets.

02:21:32.985 --> 02:21:40.886 align:start position:0%
He ends with the words, I do pray for
your majesty, not only according to my

02:21:38.186 --> 02:21:45.786 align:start position:0%
duty, but shall always do all that shall
be in my power to deserve the blessing I

02:21:43.086 --> 02:21:50.886 align:start position:0%
now humbly beg of your majesty. Perhaps
it was better that he wrote those words

02:21:48.086 --> 02:21:54.286 align:start position:0%
before he learned the full dire truth of
his father's circumstances.

02:21:55.386 --> 02:22:02.586 align:start position:0%
Believing he can still save his father,
Prince Charles, the Prince of Wales,

02:22:00.186 --> 02:22:09.286 align:start position:0%
initiates the only foreign intervention
made on the king's behalf. He persuades

02:22:05.186 --> 02:22:09.286 align:start position:0%
two Dutch ambassadors to go to London.

02:22:09.485 --> 02:22:15.786 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Prince Charles is trying to do whatever
he can to plead for his father's life,

02:22:13.886 --> 02:22:20.786 align:start position:0%
to find a way of
freeing his father, but I mean these are

02:22:17.686 --> 02:22:25.286 align:start position:0%
vain efforts. They are really not going
to undo the chain of events. Really the

02:22:22.886 --> 02:22:29.985 align:start position:0%
die has been cast.
&gt;&gt; Unaware of this attempt to save his

02:22:26.985 --> 02:22:35.086 align:start position:0%
life, Charles is instead preparing for
the next life with the Bishop of London,

02:22:32.186 --> 02:22:35.086 align:start position:0%
William Juxon.

02:22:35.586 --> 02:22:41.586 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; He spent his days praying, making his
peace with God, and of course it was God

02:22:39.786 --> 02:22:45.985 align:start position:0%
who he saw that he was ultimately
accountable only to God.

02:22:43.786 --> 02:22:49.786 align:start position:0%
A person who really had been marked by
insecurity for much of his life, but at

02:22:48.086 --> 02:22:54.186 align:start position:0%
this moment of great trial, it seemed
that he finds this inner strength, and I

02:22:51.485 --> 02:22:56.586 align:start position:0%
think that came through his hours of
prayer.

02:23:03.487 --> 02:23:11.087 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The Sabbath is over.
And for Cromwell and the commissioners,

02:23:07.287 --> 02:23:11.087 align:start position:0%
there's urgent work to be done.

02:23:12.188 --> 02:23:19.688 align:start position:0%
In order to authorize the execution of
Charles I, they must sign his death

02:23:17.188 --> 02:23:19.688 align:start position:0%
warrant.

02:23:19.987 --> 02:23:28.188 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; A death warrant means that the people
that take part in the execution are not

02:23:25.787 --> 02:23:33.087 align:start position:0%
in themselves culpable because they are
acting lawfully. The person has been

02:23:30.688 --> 02:23:37.987 align:start position:0%
condemned to death and this this
warrants his execution.

02:23:35.587 --> 02:23:42.888 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It may be lawful, but the commissioners
are rightly fearful of adding their

02:23:39.987 --> 02:23:47.587 align:start position:0%
names to the regicide.
&gt;&gt; Regicide is the Latin term for killing a

02:23:45.587 --> 02:23:51.688 align:start position:0%
king.
You are doing something that has never

02:23:49.787 --> 02:23:57.688 align:start position:0%
been done before.
You were killing the Lord's anointed.

02:23:54.688 --> 02:24:03.787 align:start position:0%
To do that, a you had to be very sure of
what you were doing, but most people

02:24:00.388 --> 02:24:09.587 align:start position:0%
would be absolutely um taken aback by
it. It was unprecedented.

02:24:07.487 --> 02:24:15.487 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; In order legally to authorize the
execution, Cromwell requires at least 20

02:24:12.487 --> 02:24:19.188 align:start position:0%
names on the death warrant.
While he's trying to round up

02:24:16.787 --> 02:24:23.388 align:start position:0%
signatures, Parliament agrees to hear
the Dutch mission.

02:24:21.587 --> 02:24:27.287 align:start position:0%
First, the Dutch ambassadors come to the
House of Lords where they plead for the

02:24:25.787 --> 02:24:30.787 align:start position:0%
king's life.
They're told that the Lords can do

02:24:28.587 --> 02:24:35.587 align:start position:0%
nothing without the Commons. So next,
they're received by the Commons where

02:24:32.487 --> 02:24:39.188 align:start position:0%
the MPs fall back on procedure.
They assure the ambassadors that they

02:24:37.487 --> 02:24:42.388 align:start position:0%
will give proper consideration to the
plea, but only after it's been

02:24:40.888 --> 02:24:46.688 align:start position:0%
translated.
Although the time of the execution has

02:24:44.188 --> 02:24:52.087 align:start position:0%
not yet been made public, the rumor is
it will happen tomorrow. The Commons are

02:24:49.287 --> 02:24:59.087 align:start position:0%
playing for time.
Just along from Whitehall at St. James's

02:24:54.388 --> 02:25:06.588 align:start position:0%
Palace, Charles is under heavy guard.
&gt;&gt; He asks uh Thomas Herbert to go and

02:25:02.388 --> 02:25:11.388 align:start position:0%
fetch a few trinkets that he has stored,
a few broken trinkets, a couple of

02:25:09.088 --> 02:25:15.988 align:start position:0%
orders of the Garter for example.
And he says to Thomas Herbert, look this

02:25:13.488 --> 02:25:18.488 align:start position:0%
is all the wealth I have left to give my
children.

02:25:20.188 --> 02:25:26.688 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; This exquisite portrait miniature shows
the middle children of Charles I,

02:25:24.688 --> 02:25:30.988 align:start position:0%
James, Duke of York, who was in France
in 1649,

02:25:28.388 --> 02:25:33.688 align:start position:0%
Princess Elizabeth, and Henry, Duke of
Gloucester.

02:25:32.388 --> 02:25:37.688 align:start position:0%
It was commissioned while the children
were in the care or custody of the Earl

02:25:35.788 --> 02:25:43.088 align:start position:0%
of Northumberland, but although he was a
parliamentarian, the Earl seems to have

02:25:39.288 --> 02:25:43.088 align:start position:0%
grown fond of his royal charges.

02:25:43.188 --> 02:25:50.188 align:start position:0%
Since Charles's arrest two years ago, he
hasn't been allowed to see his five

02:25:47.988 --> 02:25:53.088 align:start position:0%
surviving children, three of whom are
living in Europe.

02:25:53.688 --> 02:26:00.888 align:start position:0%
Parliament grants him permission to see
his two youngest,

02:25:58.488 --> 02:26:03.288 align:start position:0%
13-year-old Elizabeth and 8-year-old
Henry.

02:26:04.388 --> 02:26:10.988 align:start position:0%
Elizabeth left this account of her last
meeting with her father.

02:26:08.788 --> 02:26:15.188 align:start position:0%
She was known as a serious and scholarly
young woman, but she was so beside

02:26:13.288 --> 02:26:18.488 align:start position:0%
herself with tears that Charles feared
that she'd forget everything that he

02:26:16.488 --> 02:26:22.988 align:start position:0%
told her. She promised him that she
would write it down,

02:26:20.088 --> 02:26:26.688 align:start position:0%
and this is what she recorded.
He commanded us and all the rest of my

02:26:24.588 --> 02:26:30.688 align:start position:0%
brothers and sisters to forgive them. He
bid me tell my mother that his thoughts

02:26:28.688 --> 02:26:35.588 align:start position:0%
had never strayed from her and that his
love should be the same to the last.

02:26:33.788 --> 02:26:39.888 align:start position:0%
The king went on to try and comfort his
daughter who like him was very devout,

02:26:38.188 --> 02:26:44.488 align:start position:0%
saying that she shouldn't grieve for him
because he went to die a martyr.

02:26:43.288 --> 02:26:47.788 align:start position:0%
And then
in what I think is the most

02:26:45.688 --> 02:26:50.588 align:start position:0%
heartbreaking moment in Elizabeth's
description,

02:26:48.988 --> 02:26:54.988 align:start position:0%
he takes Henry, Duke of Gloucester, on
his knee. Henry's only 8 years old. He

02:26:52.688 --> 02:27:00.688 align:start position:0%
hasn't seen his father for years.
And the king explains, sweetheart, now

02:26:57.588 --> 02:27:04.890 align:start position:0%
they are to cut off thy father's head.
He then explained to Henry that he

02:27:02.588 --> 02:27:08.289 align:start position:0%
should never never accept a crown from
the army, certainly at least not while

02:27:06.689 --> 02:27:11.890 align:start position:0%
his brothers James and Charles were
living.

02:27:09.590 --> 02:27:17.189 align:start position:0%
Henry, trying desperately to be brave,
assured his father that he would rather

02:27:13.489 --> 02:27:21.289 align:start position:0%
be torn in pieces first.
Grief-stricken Charles presents his

02:27:19.090 --> 02:27:25.289 align:start position:0%
children with his few remaining jewels
and trinkets.

02:27:23.890 --> 02:27:29.390 align:start position:0%
He kisses them,
blesses them,

02:27:26.590 --> 02:27:29.390 align:start position:0%
and says goodbye.

02:27:31.289 --> 02:27:38.590 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; They start to leave and Elizabeth is
crying so hard many of the soldiers and

02:27:35.890 --> 02:27:42.189 align:start position:0%
things in the room overcome and Charles
himself, who's standing by a window at

02:27:40.590 --> 02:27:45.390 align:start position:0%
that stage, rushes back to them and
embraces them again.

02:27:45.590 --> 02:27:50.590 align:start position:0%
It's only then that they leave and at
that point Charles gets overcome.

02:27:53.890 --> 02:28:00.890 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; We've seen Charles in this story as the
aloof, arrogant monarch.

02:27:59.189 --> 02:28:05.590 align:start position:0%
We've seen him as
a pious prisoner,

02:28:03.090 --> 02:28:10.189 align:start position:0%
a contemptuous sovereign in the dock.
But here for the first time we see him

02:28:07.890 --> 02:28:14.590 align:start position:0%
in the raw, stripped of everything but
his love for his children, a desperate

02:28:12.390 --> 02:28:18.090 align:start position:0%
father saying farewell to his family,
all of them in tears, all of them

02:28:16.489 --> 02:28:20.390 align:start position:0%
knowing that they're never going to meet
again.

02:28:23.189 --> 02:28:29.590 align:start position:0%
Charles's despair will only be
compounded by those consenting to his

02:28:27.390 --> 02:28:33.390 align:start position:0%
regicide.
By evening

02:28:31.189 --> 02:28:40.390 align:start position:0%
Cromwell has far exceeded the minimum
number of 20 names on the death warrant.

02:28:36.189 --> 02:28:44.189 align:start position:0%
59 commissioners have signed it.
&gt;&gt; Even now some of the commissioners can't

02:28:42.289 --> 02:28:48.289 align:start position:0%
quite bring themselves to sign it. So
you you have nine who don't sign it and

02:28:46.390 --> 02:28:54.590 align:start position:0%
I think you know the silence of their
non-signatures speaks volumes about what

02:28:51.390 --> 02:28:59.289 align:start position:0%
a radical thing this really is.
&gt;&gt; For Cromwell signing the death warrant

02:28:56.590 --> 02:29:04.590 align:start position:0%
is the only legitimate course of action.
He believes it's God's will.

02:29:02.489 --> 02:29:10.191 align:start position:0%
Others, aware they may be in mortal
peril, later suggest Cromwell has

02:29:07.191 --> 02:29:10.191 align:start position:0%
coerced them.

02:29:10.891 --> 02:29:17.291 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; I think we have to be very careful about
these. One of them, Richard Ingoldsby,

02:29:14.991 --> 02:29:21.891 align:start position:0%
says that he was physically dragged to
the table and Cromwell held his hand and

02:29:19.591 --> 02:29:25.591 align:start position:0%
and forced him to sign. But this is much
later. This is at the time of the

02:29:23.291 --> 02:29:29.791 align:start position:0%
restoration. And he's trying to point
the finger of blame at Cromwell. But if

02:29:27.491 --> 02:29:33.791 align:start position:0%
you look at his signature, it doesn't
look like a signature that's been

02:29:31.191 --> 02:29:33.791 align:start position:0%
forced.

02:29:34.691 --> 02:29:42.191 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The death warrant of King Charles I must
be one of the most famous parliamentary

02:29:39.191 --> 02:29:46.891 align:start position:0%
manuscripts in England.
It's kept in the parliamentary archives,

02:29:44.991 --> 02:29:50.791 align:start position:0%
but is considered so precious that it
can no longer be taken out of storage to

02:29:48.991 --> 02:29:56.191 align:start position:0%
be studied.
This, though, is an exact copy.

02:29:54.491 --> 02:29:59.691 align:start position:0%
It's usually displayed in the House of
Lords, perhaps as a reminder of what

02:29:57.991 --> 02:30:04.291 align:start position:0%
happens when parliamentary sovereignty
is challenged.

02:30:01.791 --> 02:30:08.491 align:start position:0%
The text itself is fairly brief.
It merely outlines that Charles was

02:30:05.891 --> 02:30:13.591 align:start position:0%
sentenced upon Saturday last and
reiterates the manner of his death, the

02:30:10.691 --> 02:30:18.591 align:start position:0%
severing of his head from his body.
Here it states unequivocally the place,

02:30:16.291 --> 02:30:23.391 align:start position:0%
a street in Whitehall,
and the time, between 10:00 in the

02:30:20.491 --> 02:30:28.491 align:start position:0%
morning and 5:00 in the afternoon.
And then here are the signatures. First,

02:30:26.191 --> 02:30:34.391 align:start position:0%
the Lord President, Bradshaw,
and then O. Cromwell.

02:30:32.291 --> 02:30:40.191 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Each signatory stamps their personal
seal on the death warrant,

02:30:36.491 --> 02:30:44.791 align:start position:0%
each signing away the king's life.
&gt;&gt; What strikes me about this document is

02:30:42.491 --> 02:30:51.891 align:start position:0%
its bluntness.
It's toppling an ancient, mystical

02:30:47.491 --> 02:30:57.491 align:start position:0%
institution in just a few curt words.
Here they are, in black and white.

02:30:54.591 --> 02:30:57.491 align:start position:0%
The king will die.

02:30:58.591 --> 02:31:07.391 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; By signing the death warrant of the
king, the signatories knew

02:31:04.191 --> 02:31:13.593 align:start position:0%
that should things go pear-shaped
and should um the a monarchical regime

02:31:10.793 --> 02:31:20.492 align:start position:0%
come in sometime in the future,
what they'd done under the act of 1351

02:31:17.992 --> 02:31:26.293 align:start position:0%
was compass the death of the king.
They were clearly guilty of treason.

02:31:24.293 --> 02:31:30.192 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Once the death warrant has been signed,
it is something of a race against the

02:31:27.893 --> 02:31:34.492 align:start position:0%
clock to get the king executed. And I
think people are scared.

02:31:32.593 --> 02:31:38.692 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The longer that Charles was around, the
more chance he has to escape, the more

02:31:36.992 --> 02:31:43.093 align:start position:0%
chance they have for further riots,
further rebellions, further uprisings,

02:31:40.593 --> 02:31:47.793 align:start position:0%
more civil war, more destruction. They
needed him gone.

02:31:45.393 --> 02:31:52.192 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; As night falls, workers are busy
erecting a scaffold.

02:31:49.992 --> 02:31:57.793 align:start position:0%
Tomorrow it will be the stage for the
final scene in this shocking political

02:31:55.192 --> 02:31:57.793 align:start position:0%
theatre.

02:32:02.192 --> 02:32:11.492 align:start position:0%
It's the 30th of January,
the date set for the king's execution.

02:32:09.192 --> 02:32:16.992 align:start position:0%
In the turmoil of the last few days,
Parliament have forgotten one crucial

02:32:13.793 --> 02:32:20.893 align:start position:0%
piece of legislation.
&gt;&gt; They've forgotten

02:32:18.393 --> 02:32:26.992 align:start position:0%
that if the king dies,
then Long Live the King will be shouted.

02:32:24.593 --> 02:32:31.192 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It is one of the extraordinary things
that it was only on the morning of the

02:32:28.593 --> 02:32:36.093 align:start position:0%
execution that Parliament had to pass an
act that made it um a criminal offence,

02:32:34.492 --> 02:32:43.192 align:start position:0%
in fact treason,
to uh promulgate the name of uh of uh a

02:32:40.593 --> 02:32:47.293 align:start position:0%
successor to Charles I.
Uh in other words,

02:32:44.793 --> 02:32:52.593 align:start position:0%
if anyone were to name Charles II king,
they would be a traitor.

02:32:49.692 --> 02:32:56.593 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; As the clock ticks, Parliament rushes to
decide whether or not they should be

02:32:54.593 --> 02:32:59.192 align:start position:0%
killing both the monarch and the
monarchy.

02:32:59.992 --> 02:33:07.692 align:start position:0%
Unaware of a possible revolutionary
shift towards a republic, Charles

02:33:04.893 --> 02:33:10.893 align:start position:0%
resolves to remain regal for the future
prosperity of the crown.

02:33:09.793 --> 02:33:15.993 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; He's was
wary of being seen to be scared. He

02:33:14.093 --> 02:33:21.793 align:start position:0%
knows that it's crucial that he
continues this sense of kingly authority

02:33:19.693 --> 02:33:28.093 align:start position:0%
right to the end.
&gt;&gt; On this freezing winter's morning,

02:33:24.394 --> 02:33:32.993 align:start position:0%
Charles asks to wear two shirts.
&gt;&gt; He doesn't want to shiver and give the

02:33:30.293 --> 02:33:39.593 align:start position:0%
impression that he's frightened, so he
he layers up and he prepares to stay

02:33:35.793 --> 02:33:39.593 align:start position:0%
strong, to stay courageous.

02:33:39.793 --> 02:33:47.993 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Charles spends the early morning praying
with the bishop.

02:33:43.993 --> 02:33:52.793 align:start position:0%
He then receives the final sacrament.
At 10:00 a.m. comes the knock on the

02:33:50.394 --> 02:33:52.793 align:start position:0%
door.

02:33:53.093 --> 02:34:00.894 align:start position:0%
Charles's watch captures that exact
moment in time.

02:33:57.093 --> 02:34:00.894 align:start position:0%
It's held in the Royal Collection.

02:34:00.993 --> 02:34:06.993 align:start position:0%
Charles had two timepieces, one gold and
this silver one.

02:34:05.394 --> 02:34:11.593 align:start position:0%
Herbert recalls in his memoirs that
Charles used to wind them every night.

02:34:09.793 --> 02:34:16.493 align:start position:0%
When Charles heard the knock at the door
on that fateful morning, he said one

02:34:14.193 --> 02:34:21.693 align:start position:0%
more prayer with Bishop Jackson, then
turned to him and said, come, let us go.

02:34:19.993 --> 02:34:24.493 align:start position:0%
Herbert followed them carrying this
watch.

02:34:22.894 --> 02:34:28.293 align:start position:0%
As they left the palace, Charles asked
him the time.

02:34:25.793 --> 02:34:32.993 align:start position:0%
Herbert replied that it was 10:00 and
Charles told him to keep the watch in

02:34:30.193 --> 02:34:32.993 align:start position:0%
memory of him.

02:34:34.593 --> 02:34:42.793 align:start position:0%
Charles is surrounded by guards.
Once, his every journey through his

02:34:40.493 --> 02:34:48.793 align:start position:0%
capital would have been choreographed
with ceremony and comfort in mind.

02:34:45.894 --> 02:34:53.993 align:start position:0%
Today, however, he's on foot.
It's about a 10-minute walk between St.

02:34:50.894 --> 02:34:58.693 align:start position:0%
James's Palace and Whitehall.
The morning of the 30th of January is

02:34:56.093 --> 02:35:03.193 align:start position:0%
freezing cold and Charles asked to
quicken the slow, solemn pace.

02:35:03.894 --> 02:35:09.793 align:start position:0%
The guards march Charles to Whitehall
Palace, where he'll wait to be summoned

02:35:08.093 --> 02:35:12.293 align:start position:0%
to the scaffold outside banqueting
house.

02:35:12.593 --> 02:35:20.495 align:start position:0%
Once the site of opulent masked balls,
it will now play host to a very

02:35:17.395 --> 02:35:20.495 align:start position:0%
different spectacle.

02:35:21.095 --> 02:35:25.995 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Back putting house is chosen for
security. It's where the army is

02:35:24.694 --> 02:35:31.095 align:start position:0%
headquartered. They're all around
Whitehall. It's even got a battery of

02:35:27.595 --> 02:35:36.095 align:start position:0%
guns on the roof, so it's it's perfect.
&gt;&gt; I think it's because it's a confined

02:35:32.395 --> 02:35:39.995 align:start position:0%
space. Limits the size of the crowd.
And they're pushed well back. They don't

02:35:38.194 --> 02:35:44.795 align:start position:0%
want people hearing whatever it is that
Charles has to say.

02:35:41.995 --> 02:35:49.694 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; So who's going to do it? Who is actually
going to kill the king? The obvious

02:35:47.495 --> 02:35:54.595 align:start position:0%
choice is Richard Brandon, the city
executioner.

02:35:51.995 --> 02:35:59.595 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Undoubtedly it was
Brandon, the normal executioner, because

02:35:57.495 --> 02:36:04.194 align:start position:0%
he did a very good job.
&gt;&gt; Brandon has already beheaded the Earl of

02:36:01.595 --> 02:36:09.495 align:start position:0%
Strafford, and he prides himself on his
dexterity, eye, and steady hand.

02:36:13.595 --> 02:36:21.095 align:start position:0%
The axe, rumored to have been sharpened
by Brandon himself, is held in the Tower

02:36:18.495 --> 02:36:21.095 align:start position:0%
of London.

02:36:22.095 --> 02:36:29.095 align:start position:0%
So for sheer brutality and
functionality, this object really can't

02:36:27.795 --> 02:36:32.595 align:start position:0%
be beaten. Can you tell me a bit more
about it, Bridget?

02:36:30.395 --> 02:36:35.595 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Yes, it's traditionally the
executioner's axe at the Tower of

02:36:34.194 --> 02:36:39.895 align:start position:0%
London.
It's called a socket axe because it has

02:36:37.694 --> 02:36:44.295 align:start position:0%
that tube that runs up the back.
&gt;&gt; Oh, I can see that here. Yeah.

02:36:42.095 --> 02:36:47.595 align:start position:0%
And do we have any evidence that an axe
like this left the tower at the time of

02:36:46.795 --> 02:36:51.895 align:start position:0%
the trial?
&gt;&gt; We do. There is a warrant saying that

02:36:49.694 --> 02:36:56.395 align:start position:0%
the executioner's axe is to be delivered
to the sergeant of the court.

02:36:53.895 --> 02:36:59.895 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; So it's possible that this axe could
really have been the one that we used to

02:36:58.194 --> 02:37:04.495 align:start position:0%
behead Charles the First.
&gt;&gt; Could have been. We don't know exactly.

02:37:02.895 --> 02:37:09.395 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; And would this have been capable of of
dealing a very clean blow?

02:37:07.095 --> 02:37:13.395 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Yes, if it's been sharpened. A good
headsman should be able to use the axe

02:37:11.694 --> 02:37:17.194 align:start position:0%
well, and if he's been practicing.
Because quite often executioners did not

02:37:15.395 --> 02:37:19.896 align:start position:0%
have enough practice in some ways.
&gt;&gt; Mm.

02:37:17.597 --> 02:37:24.597 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; And certainly part of the myth of
Richard Brandon was that he was supposed

02:37:21.996 --> 02:37:27.597 align:start position:0%
to have practiced as a child on cats and
dogs.

02:37:25.796 --> 02:37:32.396 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; There was a degree of deliberate
ambiguity around the identity of the the

02:37:30.796 --> 02:37:35.896 align:start position:0%
headsman. Why was that?
&gt;&gt; I suspect it was for his own protection

02:37:34.296 --> 02:37:38.996 align:start position:0%
because people didn't know how everybody
was going to react.

02:37:37.696 --> 02:37:42.896 align:start position:0%
And they they were doing something that
nobody had really done before. It was an

02:37:40.796 --> 02:37:47.597 align:start position:0%
unprecedented event.
And I suspect that he your sense would

02:37:45.796 --> 02:37:51.896 align:start position:0%
say that you don't proclaim that's what
you've done when you don't know how

02:37:48.996 --> 02:37:57.196 align:start position:0%
popular it's going to be having done it.
&gt;&gt; As Brandon prepares the axe,

02:37:54.996 --> 02:38:02.896 align:start position:0%
the Dutch ambassadors make one last
attempt to save the king's life.

02:38:00.696 --> 02:38:08.696 align:start position:0%
They visit the head of the army, Sir
Thomas Fairfax.

02:38:05.996 --> 02:38:13.196 align:start position:0%
Fairfax has been steadfastly against any
form of regicide.

02:38:10.597 --> 02:38:16.196 align:start position:0%
But now he concedes the king should face
the executioner.

02:38:17.296 --> 02:38:25.396 align:start position:0%
He tells the ambassadors he would rather
protect many lives than one,

02:38:21.996 --> 02:38:25.396 align:start position:0%
even if it's the king's.

02:38:28.796 --> 02:38:35.196 align:start position:0%
A few hundred yards away, Parliament are
not only on the verge of killing the

02:38:33.097 --> 02:38:41.396 align:start position:0%
king, they're debating last minute
legislation that could also ensure the

02:38:37.597 --> 02:38:41.396 align:start position:0%
death of the monarchy itself.

02:38:42.996 --> 02:38:50.496 align:start position:0%
The House of Commons journal notes this
urgent bill presented on the 30th of

02:38:47.996 --> 02:38:54.396 align:start position:0%
January. Here we can see the title under
discussion concerning the proclaiming of

02:38:53.097 --> 02:38:58.796 align:start position:0%
a king.
Emergency legislation is being rushed

02:38:56.796 --> 02:39:02.396 align:start position:0%
through the Commons saying that no one
can be proclaimed king without the

02:39:00.396 --> 02:39:06.996 align:start position:0%
consent of Parliament.
The bill is given its first and second

02:39:04.696 --> 02:39:10.796 align:start position:0%
reading, and then the house adjourn at
midday for dinner.

02:39:08.696 --> 02:39:14.396 align:start position:0%
When they return, the bill is put to the
question

02:39:11.996 --> 02:39:18.597 align:start position:0%
and assented unto.
This must be one of the fastest bits of

02:39:16.597 --> 02:39:24.198 align:start position:0%
legislation that's ever passed through
Parliament.

02:39:19.796 --> 02:39:29.498 align:start position:0%
No time read the bill, just sign it.
&gt;&gt; It is just an extraordinary thing that

02:39:27.098 --> 02:39:32.598 align:start position:0%
they rushed this legislation through
minutes before

02:39:31.198 --> 02:39:35.798 align:start position:0%
the blade was going to
chop the king's head off.

02:39:34.298 --> 02:39:40.698 align:start position:0%
It's often said well they've forgotten
about it. Have they really forgotten

02:39:37.998 --> 02:39:46.798 align:start position:0%
about it or was it that they actually
hadn't made up their mind until as late

02:39:42.897 --> 02:39:51.598 align:start position:0%
as that that actually it's not just the
monarch who must go, it's it's the

02:39:49.098 --> 02:39:51.598 align:start position:0%
monarchy.

02:39:51.998 --> 02:39:57.598 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Unaware of these desperate manoeuvres,
the king has been waiting at Banqueting

02:39:56.397 --> 02:40:01.998 align:start position:0%
House.
His intention has been for only the

02:39:59.198 --> 02:40:07.598 align:start position:0%
sacrament to pass his lips, but as the
agonizing hours crawl by, Bishop Juxon

02:40:05.598 --> 02:40:10.098 align:start position:0%
persuades him to take a little bread and
claret.

02:40:12.698 --> 02:40:20.998 align:start position:0%
Just before 2:00 p.m., Charles begins
his final journey to the executioner's

02:40:18.498 --> 02:40:20.998 align:start position:0%
block.

02:40:27.298 --> 02:40:33.198 align:start position:0%
So Charles walks through here on his way
to the scaffold.

02:40:31.397 --> 02:40:36.397 align:start position:0%
His eyes must have been drawn to the
ceiling,

02:40:34.498 --> 02:40:39.798 align:start position:0%
to the masterpiece he himself had
commissioned the Flemish artist Rubens

02:40:38.198 --> 02:40:44.498 align:start position:0%
to paint.
It shows the apotheosis of his father

02:40:41.998 --> 02:40:48.397 align:start position:0%
King James, the king's elevation to
divine status.

02:40:46.498 --> 02:40:51.397 align:start position:0%
Charles's troubles had largely stemmed
from his own belief in his divine

02:40:50.098 --> 02:40:56.897 align:start position:0%
kingship.
And now he believes that he's on the way

02:40:53.198 --> 02:41:02.397 align:start position:0%
to meet his god, his own apotheosis.
How does this extravagant, intoxicating

02:40:59.998 --> 02:41:08.798 align:start position:0%
image of power seem to him now,
this last time?

02:41:04.698 --> 02:41:08.798 align:start position:0%
Hubris or vindication?

02:41:09.598 --> 02:41:18.098 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It must have been quite moving because
this is where the Stuart monarchy is

02:41:13.698 --> 02:41:24.499 align:start position:0%
most exalted. And even in the gloom, he
can look up and he will see that

02:41:20.298 --> 02:41:30.100 align:start position:0%
magnificent Rubens painting, the
of James the First. It's his father in

02:41:27.499 --> 02:41:36.100 align:start position:0%
the heavens. And yet he, Charles, is
very much on the ground and about to

02:41:32.699 --> 02:41:39.999 align:start position:0%
place his head on the block.
&gt;&gt; Charles is guided to the window from

02:41:38.199 --> 02:41:44.999 align:start position:0%
where he steps onto the specially built
scaffold.

02:41:41.699 --> 02:41:48.399 align:start position:0%
As he looks out, he sees a huge crowd
held back by troops.

02:41:50.399 --> 02:41:55.600 align:start position:0%
Charles sees the block where he'll soon
lay his head.

02:41:55.999 --> 02:42:02.499 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; The block is really low. It's only about
10 in high. And this troubles him. You

02:42:00.999 --> 02:42:06.999 align:start position:0%
know, he's going to have to lie down,
but he's told that they can't have a

02:42:04.100 --> 02:42:06.999 align:start position:0%
higher block now.

02:42:07.600 --> 02:42:14.699 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; He can see his executioner and the
executioner's assistant.

02:42:13.199 --> 02:42:20.199 align:start position:0%
In the contemporary images I've seen
show them wearing fishnet.

02:42:18.600 --> 02:42:23.699 align:start position:0%
So you can imagine what a macabre sight
this must have been seeing the man who's

02:42:22.199 --> 02:42:27.499 align:start position:0%
about going to kill you and the man
who's going to lift up your decapitated

02:42:25.399 --> 02:42:32.100 align:start position:0%
head with their features
distorted by fishnet.

02:42:31.199 --> 02:42:36.600 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; They were a
bit worried that Brandon might quail at

02:42:34.800 --> 02:42:43.199 align:start position:0%
the last minute.
So his assistant was clearly an army

02:42:40.499 --> 02:42:43.199 align:start position:0%
loyalist.

02:42:44.699 --> 02:42:51.300 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Charles reaches into his pocket for a
piece of paper.

02:42:48.899 --> 02:42:55.300 align:start position:0%
Clerks in the crowd take down every word
so that the king's last speech can be

02:42:53.100 --> 02:42:59.399 align:start position:0%
published for all to read.
Charles begins by stating his innocence.

02:42:58.199 --> 02:43:03.899 align:start position:0%
He says,
I do not think it needful for me to

02:43:01.199 --> 02:43:08.800 align:start position:0%
insist long upon this, for all the world
knows that I did not begin a war first

02:43:06.199 --> 02:43:15.300 align:start position:0%
with the two Houses of Parliament.
After days of refusing to plead, this is

02:43:11.600 --> 02:43:19.499 align:start position:0%
his moment to say not guilty.
&gt;&gt; Charles maintains his innocence once

02:43:17.499 --> 02:43:26.700 align:start position:0%
again. He says it it wasn't him who
started the war. It was Parliament. He

02:43:22.600 --> 02:43:30.600 align:start position:0%
says he has always been trying to
the liberties and privileges of the

02:43:28.400 --> 02:43:35.500 align:start position:0%
people. And then he says that famous
line, a subject and a sovereign are

02:43:33.200 --> 02:43:39.700 align:start position:0%
clean different things.
&gt;&gt; The king is gracious towards his

02:43:37.200 --> 02:43:44.100 align:start position:0%
enemies, declaring that he has forgiven
all the world, and even in particular

02:43:42.300 --> 02:43:47.200 align:start position:0%
those that have been the chief causes of
my death.

02:43:45.500 --> 02:43:51.600 align:start position:0%
He hopes that they will take the right
way towards the peace of the kingdom.

02:43:50.100 --> 02:43:55.300 align:start position:0%
The king's speech is interrupted when
one of the executioners accidentally

02:43:53.300 --> 02:44:02.400 align:start position:0%
bumps against the axe.
Hurt not the axe that may hurt me,

02:43:57.700 --> 02:44:02.400 align:start position:0%
Charles quips. A bit of gallows humor.

02:44:02.500 --> 02:44:09.200 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; He's worried about the axe
because he wants to die with dignity. He

02:44:06.900 --> 02:44:13.800 align:start position:0%
wants it to be a clean kill. He doesn't
want a blunt axe.

02:44:11.500 --> 02:44:18.200 align:start position:0%
Um the execution of his grandmother Mary
Queen of Scots had been a fairly messy

02:44:15.700 --> 02:44:21.000 align:start position:0%
affair.
&gt;&gt; Charles turns his thoughts to the

02:44:19.600 --> 02:44:26.300 align:start position:0%
people.
Truly I desire their liberty and freedom

02:44:23.500 --> 02:44:30.200 align:start position:0%
as much as anybody whosoever.
He says that he's only on the scaffold

02:44:28.000 --> 02:44:34.400 align:start position:0%
because he tried to protect the people
from having all laws changed according

02:44:32.400 --> 02:44:40.300 align:start position:0%
to the power of the sword.
Therefore I tell you,

02:44:36.200 --> 02:44:40.300 align:start position:0%
I am the martyr of the people.

02:44:40.800 --> 02:44:50.100 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Charles's speech is
a speech of huge courage. These are the

02:44:47.400 --> 02:44:54.500 align:start position:0%
words that will pave the way potentially
for his son to succeed him. These are

02:44:52.500 --> 02:44:58.300 align:start position:0%
the words that are going to echo through
the years, that are going to be his

02:44:56.200 --> 02:45:04.600 align:start position:0%
response to the unprecedented act of
killing a king, timed to play to the

02:45:01.800 --> 02:45:10.100 align:start position:0%
crowd, and it seems to get their their
sympathy and their respect even at this

02:45:07.100 --> 02:45:13.600 align:start position:0%
moment of humiliation.
&gt;&gt; He acts very much like a Christ-like

02:45:12.500 --> 02:45:19.300 align:start position:0%
figure,
changing the views of those who read it

02:45:16.400 --> 02:45:25.100 align:start position:0%
from thinking that he was potentially a
tyrant or some kind of murky monarch or

02:45:22.600 --> 02:45:30.802 align:start position:0%
difficult figure to being a martyr who
died for his principles in an unjust and

02:45:28.302 --> 02:45:36.201 align:start position:0%
unfair trial.
&gt;&gt; Cromwell is absent during this whole

02:45:33.401 --> 02:45:36.201 align:start position:0%
performance.

02:45:36.502 --> 02:45:45.002 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Where's Cromwell? Good question. Where's
Cromwell? And once again, he is just out

02:45:41.602 --> 02:45:49.201 align:start position:0%
of the frame. He's definitely around
sitting, watching, waiting.

02:45:51.002 --> 02:45:59.002 align:start position:0%
He hands his garter to the Bishop of
London, Juxon,

02:45:56.302 --> 02:46:05.102 align:start position:0%
with its George and it's the figure of
St. George killing the dragon of

02:46:01.302 --> 02:46:09.302 align:start position:0%
rebellion with 412 diamonds. So they're
sort of sparkling, you can imagine in

02:46:06.802 --> 02:46:14.102 align:start position:0%
the kind of winter light.
He wants the bishop to give the garter

02:46:13.002 --> 02:46:18.401 align:start position:0%
to
his son, Prince Charles, because he will

02:46:16.302 --> 02:46:22.401 align:start position:0%
now have to carry on
the wall.

02:46:20.102 --> 02:46:26.901 align:start position:0%
Um but he just says to the bishop
as he hands it over, he just says,

02:46:24.302 --> 02:46:26.901 align:start position:0%
remember.

02:46:27.802 --> 02:46:36.302 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Charles confers with the executioner and
says that he will pray briefly and then

02:46:32.802 --> 02:46:36.302 align:start position:0%
sign for him to strike.

02:46:38.201 --> 02:46:44.802 align:start position:0%
Bishop Juxon gives the final blessing to
which Charles Stuart, King of England,

02:46:43.602 --> 02:46:49.302 align:start position:0%
utters,
I go from a corruptible to an

02:46:47.102 --> 02:46:55.401 align:start position:0%
incorruptible crown
where no disturbance can be, no

02:46:51.602 --> 02:46:55.401 align:start position:0%
disturbance in the world.

02:46:59.302 --> 02:47:07.302 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; There's a macabre moment right at the
end when Charles is lying down and the

02:47:04.802 --> 02:47:10.901 align:start position:0%
executioner bends down
and he's just making sure that the

02:47:08.802 --> 02:47:13.502 align:start position:0%
hair's out of the way.
And

02:47:11.901 --> 02:47:17.502 align:start position:0%
Charles says, you know, wait for the
sign, wait for my sign.

02:47:16.201 --> 02:47:21.602 align:start position:0%
And the executioner says that he will
and he gets up and Charles composes

02:47:19.602 --> 02:47:24.201 align:start position:0%
himself and then he stretches out his
hands.

02:47:24.602 --> 02:47:27.802 align:start position:0%
And then the axe falls.

02:47:36.803 --> 02:47:47.003 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; So the axe has fallen.
Charles's head is raised with the usual

02:47:41.604 --> 02:47:47.003 align:start position:0%
words, behold the head of a traitor.

02:47:48.703 --> 02:47:56.003 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; A 17-year-old boy is among the watching
crowds. Later, he will write that he

02:47:53.903 --> 02:48:00.203 align:start position:0%
would never forget that day, nor the
sound that was drawn from the people as

02:47:58.503 --> 02:48:07.604 align:start position:0%
long as he lived.
Such a groan as I never heard and do

02:48:03.403 --> 02:48:07.604 align:start position:0%
desire never to hear again.

02:48:08.803 --> 02:48:17.303 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Some people were certainly stunned. It
was a very shocking moment. Others,

02:48:14.604 --> 02:48:20.604 align:start position:0%
however, reacted differently.
The soldiers on the scaffold were

02:48:19.104 --> 02:48:25.503 align:start position:0%
already thinking about the money they
could make.

02:48:22.203 --> 02:48:30.203 align:start position:0%
They were poking at the scaffold, trying
to dig up splinters of wood covered in

02:48:28.104 --> 02:48:35.604 align:start position:0%
blood so they could sell them as relics
or momentos. They were cutting bits of

02:48:32.703 --> 02:48:41.503 align:start position:0%
his hair, chopping bits of his hair so
that they could sell that as well.

02:48:39.203 --> 02:48:46.803 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It's not just the soldiers who are keen
to grab a grizzly relic.

02:48:45.003 --> 02:48:50.303 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; There's a search and a lot of people run
forward. They try to dip their hankies

02:48:48.903 --> 02:48:53.803 align:start position:0%
in his blood.
This is, you know, the stuff of martyrs

02:48:52.303 --> 02:48:59.104 align:start position:0%
and saints.
Relics seem to have sort of supernatural

02:48:56.903 --> 02:49:03.104 align:start position:0%
properties, a sort of holy
radioactivity. They're seen to sort of

02:49:01.403 --> 02:49:07.903 align:start position:0%
cure the sick.
The lame can walk, the blind can see.

02:49:05.303 --> 02:49:13.803 align:start position:0%
And there is one story that uh
a wild lion in the zoo at the Tower of

02:49:10.104 --> 02:49:20.903 align:start position:0%
London is tamed by the sight of of of a
blood-dipped piece of material from the

02:49:17.203 --> 02:49:20.903 align:start position:0%
covering over Charles's dead body.

02:49:23.203 --> 02:49:29.003 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Some of Charles I's relics are now part
of the Portland Collection in

02:49:26.803 --> 02:49:34.705 align:start position:0%
Derbyshire.
So these are hugely potent objects. Tell

02:49:33.604 --> 02:49:38.805 align:start position:0%
me some more about them.
&gt;&gt; The first one is the earring that would

02:49:37.205 --> 02:49:43.604 align:start position:0%
have been worn by Charles and was
actually taken from the king's head

02:49:41.305 --> 02:49:46.404 align:start position:0%
after he'd been decapitated.
&gt;&gt; Oh.

02:49:44.604 --> 02:49:50.904 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Yeah, pretty gruesome.
&gt;&gt; It's quite a beautiful thing, but it

02:49:48.505 --> 02:49:54.705 align:start position:0%
it's extraordinarily macabre.
&gt;&gt; After its removal, it was given to the

02:49:52.404 --> 02:50:00.904 align:start position:0%
king's daughter and made its way to the
king's granddaughter, Mary II.

02:49:57.904 --> 02:50:04.104 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; One hopes they gave it a wash first.
And this?

02:50:01.904 --> 02:50:08.404 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; So this is a silver communion cup. He
reportedly had his last communion using

02:50:07.305 --> 02:50:12.104 align:start position:0%
this cup.
&gt;&gt; They also have the quality of saintly

02:50:10.904 --> 02:50:15.805 align:start position:0%
relics.
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so you think the idea of having

02:50:13.705 --> 02:50:19.805 align:start position:0%
something that once belonged to a
venerated figure, whoever and they are

02:50:18.104 --> 02:50:24.505 align:start position:0%
and whenever they they lived, I mean, we
see it today, is something that has got

02:50:22.104 --> 02:50:28.904 align:start position:0%
a universal appeal.
&gt;&gt; Charles and his relics may have been

02:50:26.104 --> 02:50:34.805 align:start position:0%
gaining popularity after his death,
but for the moment at least, there seems

02:50:30.604 --> 02:50:34.805 align:start position:0%
little chance of a royalist return.

02:50:35.205 --> 02:50:42.205 align:start position:0%
The new Republic orders Charles's head
to be sewn back on his body and his

02:50:39.404 --> 02:50:48.005 align:start position:0%
corpse put on public display.
Stark proof of the death of both monarch

02:50:45.205 --> 02:50:51.805 align:start position:0%
and monarchy.
&gt;&gt; Indeed, when Cromwell looked over the

02:50:49.705 --> 02:50:58.205 align:start position:0%
body of the king,
he said cruel necessity. And by cruel

02:50:55.305 --> 02:51:03.205 align:start position:0%
necessity, what he meant was
this is God's will. There was no

02:51:00.604 --> 02:51:03.205 align:start position:0%
alternative.

02:51:05.705 --> 02:51:14.904 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It's the 8th of February, 1649,
and Charles Stuart is laid to rest in

02:51:11.205 --> 02:51:21.305 align:start position:0%
St. George's Chapel, Windsor.
Once his royal palace, then his prison,

02:51:18.005 --> 02:51:26.205 align:start position:0%
it's now his final resting place.
There's no fuss and barely a service.

02:51:27.904 --> 02:51:35.205 align:start position:0%
But on the same day in London, books
roll off the printing presses, a

02:51:32.705 --> 02:51:41.406 align:start position:0%
publication that parliamentarians can no
longer suppress the Eikon Basilike or

02:51:38.506 --> 02:51:44.807 align:start position:0%
the royal portrait.
The Eikon is likely to have been

02:51:42.807 --> 02:51:49.206 align:start position:0%
compiled using a selection of the king's
writings.

02:51:46.906 --> 02:51:52.807 align:start position:0%
A number of copies are now kept here at
the Wren Library in Cambridge.

02:51:54.506 --> 02:52:01.406 align:start position:0%
This little book destroyed the
publishers in the run-up to the king's

02:51:57.906 --> 02:52:06.206 align:start position:0%
trial is finally printed.
It has not been passed by the censor and

02:52:03.706 --> 02:52:11.807 align:start position:0%
it bears no publisher's name.
Inside, the subtitle explains that it is

02:52:09.206 --> 02:52:16.606 align:start position:0%
the portraiture of his sacred majesty in
his solitudes and sufferings.

02:52:14.307 --> 02:52:20.307 align:start position:0%
Immediately, the royalists accept this
as Charles's own words.

02:52:20.706 --> 02:52:27.906 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; It depicts Charles very sympathetically
as a devout,

02:52:24.706 --> 02:52:32.807 align:start position:0%
holy, suffering man, you know, who loves
his people.

02:52:29.206 --> 02:52:37.206 align:start position:0%
This is the passion of Charles I and it
turns him into a martyr.

02:52:35.606 --> 02:52:42.506 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; And look at this
icon, icon, icon, icon,

02:52:41.406 --> 02:52:46.606 align:start position:0%
icon.
In the year following the king's death,

02:52:44.206 --> 02:52:50.006 align:start position:0%
the icon was reprinted more than 30
times.

02:52:48.006 --> 02:52:54.706 align:start position:0%
The royalists now have a powerful weapon
for their cause, a martyred king.

02:52:52.906 --> 02:52:59.307 align:start position:0%
For Cromwell and his party, the work is
just beginning. They must reunite a

02:52:57.106 --> 02:53:02.706 align:start position:0%
war-torn country and rule it as a
republic.

02:53:01.006 --> 02:53:08.606 align:start position:0%
Charles was killed on the 30th of
January 1649,

02:53:05.307 --> 02:53:11.606 align:start position:0%
but the ideal and the threat of kingship
still live on.

02:53:13.706 --> 02:53:21.206 align:start position:0%
So did Charles have to die in order to
save both kingship and the kingdom?

02:53:21.606 --> 02:53:31.006 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; From the parliamentary point of view, um
yes, I think he probably did have to die

02:53:28.406 --> 02:53:37.006 align:start position:0%
for the pacification of the kingdom.
From his point of view, he had to die

02:53:33.706 --> 02:53:41.507 align:start position:0%
for the preservation of the monarchy.
&gt;&gt; Was Charles a murderer?

02:53:39.206 --> 02:53:45.606 align:start position:0%
He didn't murder anyone in cold blood,
but he had blood on his hands.

02:53:44.106 --> 02:53:49.106 align:start position:0%
Was he a martyr?
Maybe.

02:53:47.007 --> 02:53:52.207 align:start position:0%
He is a saint, the only saint of the
Anglican Church.

02:53:51.106 --> 02:53:55.007 align:start position:0%
He might have been a martyr for the
monarchy,

02:53:53.407 --> 02:53:58.907 align:start position:0%
but was he a martyr for the liberties
and privileges of the people?

02:53:57.507 --> 02:54:01.507 align:start position:0%
I think that's a stretch even for
Charles.

02:54:04.707 --> 02:54:12.807 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; Charles the First's trial and subsequent
death moved us from absolute kingship to

02:54:10.407 --> 02:54:17.807 align:start position:0%
make way for modern governments.
And it set a radical precedent for the

02:54:15.307 --> 02:54:23.907 align:start position:0%
judiciary, not only in Britain, but
across the globe.

02:54:19.707 --> 02:54:32.007 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; When looking around for precedents
for Nuremberg, for prosecuting Saddam

02:54:28.207 --> 02:54:39.007 align:start position:0%
Hussein and Milosevic and Pinochet,
one goes back to this as the beginning

02:54:36.106 --> 02:54:45.606 align:start position:0%
of denying impunity
to despots and to rulers who kill their

02:54:43.106 --> 02:54:51.106 align:start position:0%
own people.
&gt;&gt; Charles could have compromised very

02:54:48.007 --> 02:54:56.307 align:start position:0%
slightly and saved his own personal
monarchy, but in a way, I think he had a

02:54:53.507 --> 02:55:00.407 align:start position:0%
martyr's complex. And who's to argue
against Charles's providential

02:54:58.007 --> 02:55:06.606 align:start position:0%
worldview? Because in the end, it's the
monarchy that returned.

02:55:03.207 --> 02:55:12.007 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; As the axe fell, it crushed centuries of
royal rule, making way for parliamentary

02:55:09.207 --> 02:55:18.606 align:start position:0%
democracy and ensuring that no one is
above the law, not even a king.

02:55:16.207 --> 02:55:21.507 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; For the present in England, the death of
Charles the First means the death of

02:55:20.407 --> 02:55:26.307 align:start position:0%
monarchy.
The country will be ruled as a republic,

02:55:24.207 --> 02:55:31.807 align:start position:0%
left in the hands of the men who claimed
to represent the people. Will they be

02:55:29.207 --> 02:55:37.407 align:start position:0%
able to heal a country ravaged by years
of civil wars and destruction,

02:55:34.507 --> 02:55:41.708 align:start position:0%
or will they encounter the same pitfalls
as the king they have executed?

02:55:41.207 --> 02:55:46.908 align:start position:0%
&gt;&gt; If the
&gt;&gt; Republic fails, the 59 men who signed

02:55:45.009 --> 02:55:51.408 align:start position:0%
the king's death warrant will be in
mortal danger,

02:55:48.608 --> 02:55:57.208 align:start position:0%
themselves accused of treason.
The stage is set for the revenge of the

02:55:54.708 --> 02:55:57.208 align:start position:0%
king.

